{"type": "haystack.document_stores.in_memory.document_store.InMemoryDocumentStore", "init_parameters": {"bm25_tokenization_regex": "(?u)\\b\\w\\w+\\b", "bm25_algorithm": "BM25L", "bm25_parameters": {}, "embedding_similarity_function": "cosine", "index": "292ff959-b679-4eb5-a7b6-075390924d49"}, "documents": [{"id": "5fe59c9753ec1594967c6dc06fed0eb9399415b802dbd754e24bf4b3b3cadfa0", "content": "This chunk introduces the AET Schools Standards Framework, emphasizing its role as a tool for school leaders to enhance autism practices and improve outcomes for autistic pupils. It outlines the framework's alignment with SEND policy, educational inspections, and leadership standards, as well as its applicability across various educational settings.Schools\nStandards Framework\nThis framework can be used by leaders as a tool that enables them to enhance and embed good autism practice across their school\nFunded by\nFunded by\f2 Schools Standards Framework\nIntroduction to the AET Schools Standards The Autism Education Trust (AET) Schools Standards are a whole-school framework for leaders to enhance and embed inclusive practice and to improve outcomes for autistic pupils. They cover the continuum of provision (mainstream, resource base, special or specialist school setting) and support schools to reflect, plan, and implement school improvement processes. The Standards support leaders to meet special educational needs and disabilities (SEND) policy and legal requirements and their equality duties whilst complying with the Education Inspection Framework (2022) and the Teacher Standards (2011). They also align with the Head Teacher Standards (2020).\nBy using the AET Schools Standards, leaders and leadership teams can:\n\u2022 Identify priority areas to work on.\n", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "aet-schools-standards-framework.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/166LzAs4UUYY2nmfvEFE7NUcJyEIfBoc3", "source_id": "fd3b04f3804d8efbea64fc291fd78f2aefe