20290184021, -0.051316093653440475, 0.014265957288444042, -0.006430564448237419, -0.04995341971516609, -0.013900508172810078, 0.022639840841293335, -0.02043781243264675, -0.012566087767481804], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "8e49feb148a2e2a97b9653912cb5c2643b85d00a11539f6b908b34aa50339a3b", "content": "This chunk is part of the section discussing the importance of AET Schools Standards in addressing the unique learning barriers faced by autistic pupils. It emphasizes the need for high-quality, inclusive teaching approaches tailored to the specific differences in learning styles and experiences of autistic students.The AET Schools Standards support school leaders to address the specific barriers to learning experienced by autistic pupils. The standards provide pointers to teaching approaches, methods, and resources to use in educational provision and practice to enhance the school experience for this group of learners. The environment that autistic pupils require is one that should be saturated in high-quality teaching. High-quality teaching and inclusive teaching are not mutually exclusive and we cannot have one without the other. For autistic pupils, the following need to be taken into account:\n\u2022 Three key areas of difference in autism influence the way in which autistic pupils learn and experience their educational settings. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "aet-schools-standards-framework.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/166LzAs4UUYY2nmfvEFE7NUcJyEIfBoc3", "source_id": "fd3b04f3804d8efbea64fc291fd78f2aefe37cddffb9972efcc9615a49cfdf8c", "page_number": 3, "split_id": 3, "split_idx_start": 1622, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "98d13a1e5dfcc23b3dcb9bf457d738df00dc56211260c283f5f3705b382ecb3f", "range": [409, 535]}, {"doc_id": "96840cea942b5adcfc2314318e48fd666fddcf3f680c2b17ef9c055b80b0bf72", "range": [0, 200]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.031162625178694725, -0.09670863300561905, -0.027579668909311295, 0.0001505831