.03226340934634209], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "68f03a48829ae5a3f2438814d726981df226984e8d40ed6cbb86ad1226407d34", "content": "This chunk details the acknowledgment of co-occurring conditions in autistic pupils and sets the stage for the Eight Principles of Good Autism Practice, which are integral to the AET Schools Standards Framework. These principles aim to enhance educational approaches for autistic students within a whole-school framework, focusing on their unique needs and circumstances.\u2022 Autistic pupils often have additional co-occurring conditions and differences alongside a diagnosis of autism. As with all pupils, co-occurring conditions and differences need to be considered in educational planning.\nEight Principles of Good Autism Practice\nThe AET Schools Standards are structured around eight key principles that provide the framework for the development of whole-school approaches (ages 5\u201316) to enhancing provision for autistic pupils and their families. For each principle, there are two standards.\nThese principles are embedded within the AET\u2019s themes of \u2018Understanding the Individual\u2019, \u2018Positive Relationships\u2019, \u2018Enabling Environments\u2019, and \u2018Learning and Development\u2019. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "aet-schools-standards-framework.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/166LzAs4UUYY2nmfvEFE7NUcJyEIfBoc3", "source_id": "fd3b04f3804d8efbea64fc291fd78f2aefe37cddffb9972efcc9615a49cfdf8c", "page_number": 3, "split_id": 6, "split_idx_start": 2952, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "9b224cb1b37037af48bb2616855bcd7a50fd90c21230035f43929f975bb0e1da", "range": [356, 469]}, {"doc_id": "4c6dbcadfa0e5a9fa006673f002e3cff795b08680031c6caf946c624cc120166", "range": [0, 172]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.016190720722079277, -0.07841027528047562, -0.01108219102025032, 0.016328269615769386, 0.017370590940117836, 0.009557319805026054, -0.007699804380536079, -0.0430067740380764, -0