5167, 0.007666333112865686, 0.009803120978176594, -0.015230413526296616, 0.026164375245571136, -0.018540041521191597, 0.0038267767522484064, 0.010458065196871758, 0.006108418107032776, 0.01615436002612114, -0.030139600858092308], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "035acc8f9784488bfbfeb4f2117e32210935f509d139d6d19d75a7fbc8196f29", "content": "This chunk is part of the \"Eight Inclusion Promises\" created by the Autism Education Trust's panel of Autistic Young Experts, emphasizing the commitment to support and empower autistic pupils. It is followed by information about the AET Schools Professional Development Programme, which offers training and resources for staff to enhance inclusive practices for autistic students.8 Help you to feel safe, secure, empowered, and able to learn.\fSchools Standards Framework6 AET Schools Professional Development Programme\nThe AET Schools Standards Framework is part of the Schools Professional Development Programme. This Programme contains a set of modules and materials for staff working in Schools across mainstream and specialist provisions.\nIn addition to the Standards the AET Schools Professional Development Programme consists of:\n\u2022 The Schools Competency Framework that enables practitioners to evaluate and reflect upon their own practice when working with autistic pupils.\n\u2022 The AET Progression Framework, a comprehensive interactive assessment tool that supports practitioners to identify learning priorities, set key learning intentions, and track progress for autistic children and young people in both mainstream and specialist educational provisions.\n", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "aet-schools-standards-framework.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/166LzAs4UUYY2nmfvEFE7NUcJyEIfBoc3", "source_id": "fd3b04f3804d8efbea64fc291fd78f2aefe37cddffb9972efcc9615a49cfdf8c", "page_number": 5, "split_id": 13, "split_idx_start": 6059, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "1f44b47a4310452802a9261c298345