0.01600344479084015, 0.017608797177672386, -0.02054222859442234, 0.003143732203170657, -0.0641021654009819, 0.05238710716366768, -0.029659798368811607, -0.021008769050240517, 0.010888858698308468, 0.006654768250882626, -0.03141806647181511, -0.04601900279521942, -0.026072194799780846, 0.0026677970308810472, 1.902749517213257e-34, 0.00018327210273128003, 0.017976239323616028, 0.021268757060170174, -0.05125345289707184, 0.042430613189935684, -0.024117453023791313, -0.0774598941206932, 0.004273255355656147, 0.010974349454045296, -0.02029978297650814, -0.022592365741729736], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "dc5a5d6faeecdb0414ffb3e868ca3864b81fffd7840a170a3573935e0686ef11", "content": "This chunk is part of the competencies outlined in Principle One of the AET Schools Standards Framework, which emphasizes understanding the strengths, differences, and co-occurring conditions of autistic pupils. It highlights the importance of recognizing barriers to learning and understanding the causes of behavior, contributing to effective educational practices.I identify the key strengths and differences that autistic pupils face in the areas of social understanding and communication; flexibility, information processing and understanding, and sensory processing and integration.\nC2. I recognise that many autistic pupils have co-occurring conditions and differences. They experience high levels of anxiety and stress, and these can create barriers to learning. I know that a pupil may display behaviour based on a combination of factors and I work hard to understand the causes and functions of the behaviour. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "aet-schools-standards-framework.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/166LzAs4UUYY2nmfvEFE7NUcJyEIfBoc3", "source_id": "fd3b04f3804d8efbea64fc291fd78f2aefe37cddffb9972efcc9615a49cfdf8c", "page_number": 8, "split_id": 18, "split_idx_start": 9141, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "6da3c129636a7bd8890cd62fe080d6bf2a22e9bb5fe