2292099, 0.0069381059147417545, -0.047895245254039764, -0.039109040051698685, 0.005593396723270416, 1.7577325444076538e-34, 0.004237980581820011, 0.024535156786441803, 0.020563624799251556, -0.05243045464158058, 0.014183825813233852, -0.0067739663645625114, 0.012655404396355152, -0.0007975522894412279, 0.0016982555389404297, -0.017240921035408974, -0.03175970911979675], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "1860c6546af48b1738b261fab387085c070ae9152f1d29780f00545663709699", "content": "This chunk is part of the AET Schools Standards Framework, specifically under Principle Two: \"Enabling the autistic pupil to contribute to and influence decisions.\" It outlines a standard for involving pupils in discussions about their support, linking it to statutory frameworks related to effective learning and teaching practices.ROOM FOR IMPROVEMENT\nDEVELOPING WE DO THIS REALLY WELL\nWe regularly arrange times for pupils to meet with a trusted key person to discuss the support they are receiving and whether this is suitable to meet their needs.\nROOM FOR IMPROVEMENT\nDEVELOPING WE DO THIS REALLY WELL\nLinks to statutory frameworks\nHead Teachers Standards (2020), Standard 5ii: Headteachers establish and sustain practices that enable pupils to access the curriculum and learn effectively.\nTeacher Standards (2011), Standard 2: Teachers promote good progress and outcomes by pupils and demonstrate knowledge and understanding of how pupils learn and how this impacts on teaching.\fSchools Standards Framework19 Understanding the Individual\nHow do we document evidence of working on this standard? We document evidence of working on this standard through case studies, one page profiles, and the goals we co-produce with parents/carers and children (where possible), and by documenting our reasonable adjustments and the impact of those.\n", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "aet-schools-standards-framework.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/166LzAs4UUYY2nmfvEFE7NU