8152, -0.012745741754770279, 0.04235386475920677, 0.006038810592144728, -0.0048098512925207615, -0.010913961566984653, -0.016990315169095993], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "2beadd34f78fc495306f5bcde2a53e20dc8b531d024894a0a58130b2e6a32b73", "content": "This chunk appears in the section discussing the importance of collaboration with various parties, including parents and professionals, to support autistic pupils effectively. It emphasizes the necessity of shared responsibility among those involved in a child's education to ensure timely identification of needs and prompt interventions, aligning with the AET Schools Standards related to positive relationships and workforce development.If children and families are to receive the right help at the right time, everyone who comes into contact with them has a role to play in identifying concerns, sharing information and taking prompt action (Keeping children safe in education, 2020:5).\f26 Schools Standards Framework\nPositive Relationships\nHow do we document evidence of working on this standard? We document evidence of working on this standard through case studies, one page profiles, and the goals we co-produce with parents/carers and children (where possible), and by documenting our reasonable adjustments and the impact of those.\nEvidence to show how we meet this standard:\nWhat has been the impact of implementing this standard?\nOn staff:\nOn parents/carers:\nOn autistic pupils:\fSchools Standards Framework27 Positive Relationships\nPrinciple Four: Workforce development related to good autism practice.\n", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "aet-schools-standards-framework.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/166LzAs4UUYY2nmfvEFE7NUcJyEIfBoc3", "source_id": "fd3b04f3804d8efbea64fc291fd78f2aefe37cddffb9972efcc9615a49cfdf8c", "page_number": 25, "split_id": 58, "split_idx_start": 29173, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "f0143183a66d0bc9cfc42be19b26def3150fbb8b7a501edc5c95a69d97880b9d", "ran