5618668, -0.000926295411773026, -0.0447789691388607, -0.01495019905269146, 0.007912466302514076, -0.02296682633459568, -0.004400905687361956], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "195e92acd55df8fd75763eb75d27ce4305f96eeaefb1fa411796b6096ffa9aac", "content": "This chunk is from the section discussing Principle Six: An ethos and environment that fosters social inclusion for autistic pupils, specifically under Standard Eleven. It emphasizes the importance of monitoring and supporting the mental and emotional wellbeing of autistic pupils and making necessary adjustments to the school environment to accommodate their sensory needs.ROOM FOR IMPROVEMENT\nDEVELOPING WE DO THIS REALLY WELL\nExamples of how we can do this:\nWe identify, monitor, build, and maintain the mental and emotional wellbeing of autistic pupils and involve pupils in communicating how they view their ideal school.\nROOM FOR IMPROVEMENT\nDEVELOPING WE DO THIS REALLY WELL\nWe do the best we can to organise classrooms and the wider school environment to manage sensory differences that may be distracting or uncomfortable.\nROOM FOR IMPROVEMENT\nDEVELOPING WE DO THIS REALLY WELL\nWe observe pupils to find out more about their sensory differences and preferences and adjust the environment accordingly.\nROOM FOR IMPROVEMENT\nDEVELOPING WE DO THIS REALLY WELL\nLinks to statutory frameworks\nNo single practitioner can have a full picture of a child\u2019s needs and circumstances. If children and families are to receive the right help at the right time, everyone who comes into contact with them has a role to play in identifying concerns, sharing information and taking prompt action (Keeping children safe in education, 2020:5).\f", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "aet-schools-standards-framework.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/166LzAs4UUYY2nmfvEFE7NUcJyEIfBoc3", "source_id": "fd3b04f3804d8efbea64fc291fd78f2aefe37cddffb9972efcc9615a49cfdf8c", "page_number": 36, "split_id": 72, "sp