81446457, 0.024432333186268806, -0.0981702134013176, -0.008886146359145641, -0.010822976008057594, 0.039561279118061066, 0.008366703987121582, -0.005833139643073082, -0.018023045733571053, -0.02844589576125145], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "2640865e59cb75420d24c238b03c6eb1f7e452e882972dd297d2a16d325799b3", "content": "This chunk is part of the AET Schools Standards Framework, specifically within the section on Learning and Development. It emphasizes the importance of involving parents and children in decision-making, setting high expectations, tracking progress, and ensuring that educational approaches are evidence-based to promote positive outcomes for autistic pupils.They should ensure decisions are informed by the insights of parents and those of children and young people themselves; have high ambitions and set stretching targets for them; track their progress towards these goals; keep under review the additional or different provision that is made for them; promote positive outcomes in the wider areas of personal and social development, and ensure that the approaches used are based on the best possible evidence and are having the required impact on progress (SEND Code of Practice, 2014:25).\fSchools Standards Framework44 Learning and Development\nHow do we document evidence of working on this standard? We document evidence of working on this standard through case studies, one page profiles, and the goals we co-produce with parents/carers and children (where possible), and by documenting our reasonable adjustments and the impact of those.\nEvidence to show how we meet this standard:\nWhat has been the impact of implementing this standard?\nOn staff:\nOn parents/carers:\nOn autistic pupils:\fSchools Standards Framework45 Learning and Development\nPrinciple Eight: Adapting the curriculum, teaching, and learning to promote wellbeing and success for autistic pupils.\n", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "aet-schools-standards-framework.pdf"