35192197561264, -0.007716002874076366, 0.032697297632694244, -0.07626798748970032, 0.013977576047182083, -0.010889852419495583, -0.006183992139995098, -0.0005853349575772882, -0.0072097694501280785, -0.03538701310753822, -0.029741084203124046], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "19cf7f20e8ed3d2e80de8c063a5b61d4c8b03981e07e00b717502c63b8d2ec2f", "content": "This chunk is located in the \"Learning and Development\" section of the AET Schools Standards Framework, specifically under Principle Eight: Adapting the curriculum, teaching, and learning to promote wellbeing and success for autistic pupils. It discusses the importance of developing social and emotional skills through various educational programs and highlights the need for tailored activities to enhance learning for autistic pupils.It is a central element of our curriculum. ROOM FOR IMPROVEMENT\nDEVELOPING WE DO THIS REALLY WELL\nWe develop and promote social and emotional skills through relationships and sex education (RSE), personal, social, health, and economic (PSHE) education, as well as in the wider curriculum. Autistic pupils have access to enhanced and differentiated learning and teaching in these areas. ROOM FOR IMPROVEMENT\nDEVELOPING WE DO THIS REALLY WELL\nWe plan engaging activities that build on the strengths and interests of the pupil to deliver the curriculum. ROOM FOR IMPROVEMENT\nDEVELOPING WE DO THIS REALLY WELL\nLinks to statutory frameworks\nEarly years providers, schools and colleges should know precisely where children and young people with SEN are in their learning and development. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "aet-schools-standards-framework.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/166LzAs4UUYY2nmfvEFE7NUcJyEIfBoc3", "source_id": "fd3b04f3804d8efbea64fc291fd78f2aefe37cddffb9972efcc9615a49cfdf8c", "page_number": 45, "split_id": 83, "split_idx_start": 47887, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "35bbf2f48ee2a92596fae4c6fd7fef14db904252794d365f9183239360fd1084", 