volved in creating the AET Schools Standards and Competencies. It highlights the collaborative efforts of the Autism Education Trust, various professionals from the Special Educational Needs Inclusion Support Service, and input from key individuals and teams that supported the development of these frameworks.We are grateful to a number of people who have been involved in creating and co-authoring these frameworks. Co-authors have included the members of the AET\u2019s Autistic Young Experts panel who have provided input on key concepts, ideas and terminology, The Bridge London Trust and Liverpool\u2019s Special Educational Needs Inclusion Support Service have provided invaluable input into the development of content and advice on drafts, as has Emma Chantler, AET Content Manager. The Birmingham Communication Team shared valuable resources. Training Partner Co-Authors:\nFor the early-years based materials Rachel Read, Specialist Advisory Teacher with the Special Educational Needs Inclusion Support Service (SENISS), Liverpool: providing advice and support for children with special educational needs, in order to promote their inclusion within mainstream and nursery settings, enabling them to achieve their full potential.\nNatalie Reaney, Specialist Advisory Teacher with the Special Educational Needs Inclusion Support Service (SENISS), Liverpool: providing advice and support for children with special educational needs, in order to promote their inclusion within mainstream and nursery settings, enabling them to achieve their full potential.\n", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "aet-schools-standards-framework.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/166LzAs4UUYY2nmfvEFE7NUcJyEIfBoc3", "source_id": "fd3b04f3804d8efbea64fc291fd78f2aefe37cddffb9972efcc9615a49cfdf8c", "page_number": 49, "split_id": 88, "split_idx_start": 51987, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "d8a57dc7567cac80573443ffc0c1485d3a6ff759954a6634769abb5356a9e086", "range": [727, 835]}, {"