259673088789, 0.022492529824376106, -0.059123750776052475, -0.0344640351831913, -0.015628784894943237, 2.030784040480584e-34, -0.0039223236963152885, -0.042183130979537964, -0.004564146511256695, -0.08834192901849747, -0.0028643691912293434, -0.0015955523122102022, 0.025584055110812187, -0.002579348161816597, 0.007698436733335257, 0.029523126780986786, -0.031616587191820145], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "0f8ef712f2f215f293cb576345ef7b3896a84319560f80eaaf27999acc20368f", "content": "This chunk is part of the introduction to the recommendations in the guidance report on improving behaviour in schools. It emphasizes the importance of understanding individual pupils\u2019 needs, fostering good relationships, and implementing simple, cost-effective strategies to promote positive behaviour, while also addressing strategies for dealing with misbehaviour.A key theme from these recommendations is the importance of knowing individual pupils well, so that schools and teachers know which factors might affect pupil behaviour and what the school can do to address these. Focusing on developing good relationships also ensures pupils feel valued and supported, meaning they are less likely to misbehave. It is also good that many simple approaches that don\u2019t take much time or money to implement\u2014like providing breakfast clubs, or greeting pupils individually before a lesson\u2014can have a really positive impact on behaviour. The second focuses on how to deal with bad behaviour when it happens. A key message is that schools should use personalised approaches\u2014like daily report cards\u2014to address \u2018problem pupils\u2019, rather than universal systems. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Improving_behaviour_in_schools_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/18VgDE6jXP5eN_4w_IPbsUVu8tW-V4ep2", "source_id": "7a1d3f44ce494b71f1d9e0fe5b7517c578d4c867369077d76a9ccd359cf1603d", "page_number": 4, "split_id": 2, "split_idx_start":