43, -0.048959311097860336, -0.06737837195396423, -0.01516985334455967, 1.7795300438038636e-34, 0.0035983307752758265, -0.0395423099398613, 0.0132297920063138, -0.09661554545164108, -0.016480274498462677, 0.007927633821964264, 0.019534852355718613, -0.0014124085428193212, 0.00894657801836729, 0.014215371571481228, -0.03758236765861511], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "7f86b216c63e21b906022719a68fdc949fdd04d7255a54d021470ca41a9a7eec", "content": "This chunk appears in the \"Introduction\" section of the document, detailing the collaboration with Research Schools and emphasizing the guidance's applicability to various educational settings. It outlines the intended audience, including class teachers and senior leaders, for effective implementation of behavior policies in schools.Schools may also want to seek support from our national network of Research Schools\u2014a collaboration between the EEF, the Institute for Effective Education, and the Department for Education. Research Schools aim to lead the way in the use of evidence-based teaching, building affiliations with large numbers of schools in their region, and supporting the use of evidence at scale. Who is the guidance for?\nThis guidance is applicable to primary and secondary school settings. It is hoped that most of the guidance will be useful for class teachers, while all should be relevant to senior leaders who are considering school behaviour policies and approaches. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Improving_behaviour_in_schools_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/18VgDE6jXP5eN_4w_IPbsUVu8tW-V4ep2", "source_id": "7a1d3f44ce494b71f1d9e0fe5b7517c578d4c867369077d76a9ccd359cf1603d", "page_number": 5, "split_id": 6, "split_idx_start": 5299, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "e80f733d911ba7f47be2eeb5de8a4410b32c64c474d086370da985e78a97a1da", "range": [1007, 1197]}, {"doc_id": "e26a92bcba6954595cf986f7cfc53f40f9a1f52b0d1dc202aa2686110b4f0979", "range": [0, 182]}]}, "sc