sses the importance of the outlined strategies in addressing a wide range of pupil behaviors, emphasizing both positive and disruptive actions within educational settings.The strategies outlined should be helpful in schools and classrooms where behaviour is generally good as well as where there are problems. \u2018Behaviour\u2019, throughout this report, is taken to mean the whole range of ways pupils can act in school, including disruptive or aggressive behaviours, prosocial behaviours, and learning behaviours (which are explained further in Recommendation 2). This guidance aims to help schools to support their pupils with the entire range of behaviours they may encounter.\f4\nEducation Endowment Foundation There is much research on the ramifications of poor pupil behaviour on the school learning environment. It is one of the most difficult tasks that both experienced and new teachers have to contend with in schools2 and one of the perennial issues that affects teacher retention.3 However, while behaviour is a significant issue affecting teachers in many schools, most pupils in most lessons behave well. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Improving_behaviour_in_schools_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/18VgDE6jXP5eN_4w_IPbsUVu8tW-V4ep2", "source_id": "7a1d3f44ce494b71f1d9e0fe5b7517c578d4c867369077d76a9ccd359cf1603d", "page_number": 5, "split_id": 8, "split_idx_start": 6304, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "e26a92bcba6954595cf986f7cfc53f40f9a1f52b0d1dc202aa2686110b4f0979", "range": [530, 669]}, {"doc_id": "64e27a98483e4be0cbbb53d41db62191bd0826b497448a8c64ab05e8cbf8b445", "range": [0, 300]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.06259806454181671, -0.09469074010848999, 0.007364052347838879, 0.00102501199580729, -0.005261504556983709, 0.039649076759815216, 0.013335691764950752, 0.01767907105386257, -0.032715804874897, 0.0008107390021905303, 0.027702204883098602, -0.006957869976758957, -0.009465293027460575, -0.021100826561450958