76, -0.03165614604949951, -0.02940259873867035, 1.835146768220845e-34, 0.006949733477085829, -0.029194166883826256, 0.010419654659926891, -0.07584639638662338, -0.009977340698242188, 0.009652486070990562, 0.027139602228999138, 0.02744579128921032, 0.024718374013900757, 0.009235595352947712, -0.005052230320870876], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "2d4cc0ac26b27c52118465e1402b4b5197d8e276043da68f59767a3f4c33f8e9", "content": "This chunk appears in the section \"Recommendation 1: Know and understand your pupils and their influences.\" It emphasizes the importance of understanding individual students by gathering information from pupils and parents, suggesting that personalized knowledge of students' strengths and interests can lead to more effective behavior management strategies in schools.Information needs to be sought and willingly shared by pupils and parents. Understanding pupils better can be more effective than relying on a default response (see Box 1).\nHow can we get to know our pupils?\nConsider your school context and the system that would work for you. Is it possible to structure your school such that someone knows each pupil, their strengths and interests? ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Improving_behaviour_in_schools_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/18VgDE6jXP5eN_4w_IPbsUVu8tW-V4ep2", "source_id": "7a1d3f44ce494b71f1d9e0fe5b7517c578d4c867369077d76a9ccd359cf1603d", "page_number": 10, "split_id": 13, "split_idx_start": 13405, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "de94fc59874e15b02f4d9903935a07bdcea780cd319273bd76c8520a3b274bc8", "range": [3843, 3918]}, {"doc_id": "7a84da2502efdfc7ebf5deaf6f5a0594d1adebd1fd87546bb735ffe3860e93b5", "range": [0, 107]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.050689853727817535, -0.0632525086402893, -0.02550431713461876, 0.048957109451293945, -0.0001393942511640489, 0.02827962301671505, 0.02998373471200466, -0.036918286234140396, -0.013181501068174839, 0.038843199610710144, -0.013858918100