4, 0.03553688898682594, -0.0029071916360408068, 0.04212638735771179, 0.007263198494911194, -0.019862812012434006, -0.027878880500793457, 0.07065868377685547, 0.012187404558062553, -0.029699256643652916, 0.0029213603120297194, -0.058594804257154465, 0.01635960303246975, -0.02071251906454563, 0.09841246902942657, -0.04644092917442322, -0.03985048457980156, -0.01948746107518673, 0.01909831538796425, -0.02574099227786064, -0.017055492848157883, 0.01263561099767685, -0.016253532841801643, 2.114785050461337e-34, -0.013854656368494034, 0.000573362922295928, -0.0041096024215221405, -0.08396950364112854, 0.023551449179649353, -0.010774167254567146, 0.008727509528398514, -0.017277080565690994, 0.04950892552733421, -0.011835728771984577, -0.03839428350329399], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "7a84da2502efdfc7ebf5deaf6f5a0594d1adebd1fd87546bb735ffe3860e93b5", "content": "This chunk is part of Recommendation 1, \"Know and understand your pupils and their influences,\" in the guidance report on improving behaviour in schools. It emphasizes the importance of understanding individual pupils' strengths and interests through supportive relationships, highlighting the role of teachers and pastoral systems in fostering positive behaviour.Is it possible to structure your school such that someone knows each pupil, their strengths and interests? Can this be managed for some pupils, if not all? In primary schools and special schools, the class teacher may be able to provide this role. In secondary schools, an existing pastoral system might be a good place to start to proactively support your pupils to respond well to influences in and out of school that could affect their behaviour. At the teacher level, regularly and intentionally focusing small amounts of time working on relationships with individual pupils can have a big impact. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Improving_behaviour_in_schools_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/18VgDE6jXP5