"7bb664735f00f77ba32330f38584e9b91670f665c9edb31070049bb2a054881b", "content": "This chunk is part of the section discussing the influences on pupil behaviour, specifically illustrating how personal factors, such as bereavement and social relationships, can impact a pupil's behaviour and overall wellbeing. It emphasizes the importance of understanding these influences to effectively support students in a school environment.The pupil begins in Quadrant A (the optimal position). However, the bereavement is understandably a negative influence on behaviour and the pupil may gradually move through the quadrants (first to bottom right and then bottom left). These changes may be subtle but over time they can have a substantial effect on the pupil\u2019s wellbeing or curricular development.\nOr consider the example of Pupil B, a Year 10 pupil who has been disrupting lessons and walking out of class. Investigating this behaviour, the school staff team becomes aware that she has not made solid friendships so has low social confidence since moving to the school the previous term. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Improving_behaviour_in_schools_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/18VgDE6jXP5eN_4w_IPbsUVu8tW-V4ep2", "source_id": "7a1d3f44ce494b71f1d9e0fe5b7517c578d4c867369077d76a9ccd359cf1603d", "page_number": 11, "split_id": 18, "split_idx_start": 16223, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "6deb46d623bea0e16948efa6d7e234aae46281187c50a4ece79e26fb381a03bb", "range": [656, 711]}, {"doc_id": "69d54ca9febe662fcb9fe2edc0490f10961c6fc0c93330f07633645d496c28a1", "range": [0, 181]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.050899069756269455, -0.07644544541835785, 0.00100810662843287, 0.009977384470403194, -0.017338568344712257, 0.030019469559192657, 0.028279099613428116, -0.014580178074538708, 0.0019336913246661425, 0.0007041028002277017, 0.0369524322450161, -0.030280422419309616, 0.013627889566123486, -0.04912108555436134, -0.013028383255004883, -0.083