945, 0.02278321050107479, 0.00767150055617094, -0.09096698462963104, 2.6222164706268813e-07, -0.07962001860141754, 0.053824182599782944, 0.037262313067913055, -0.03836158663034439, 0.0026387013494968414, 0.0063957469537854195, 0.0051022120751440525, 0.027472032234072685, 0.11113669723272324, 0.011533343233168125, 0.03153187036514282, -0.029865987598896027, 0.010804631747305393, 0.03719072416424751, -0.004184570163488388, -0.06692187488079071, 0.00703725591301918, -0.11555835604667664, 0.01470007561147213, 0.012407067231833935, 0.05809614062309265, 0.008798540569841862, 0.07793119549751282, 0.03017471730709076, -0.004744058940559626, -0.007843460887670517, -0.04081236571073532, -0.0437857024371624, -0.057489823549985886, -0.004538829438388348, -0.03531669080257416, -0.05440745875239372, -0.02934396080672741, -0.00031179451616480947, 0.019167544320225716, 0.0039474680088460445, -0.028669169172644615, 0.02364608831703663, 0.03531312942504883, 0.06274689733982086, -0.03748216852545738, 0.007129207719117403, 0.015556786209344864, -0.02574782446026802, 0.023971328511834145, -0.01740817166864872, 0.01207204069942236, 0.046759095042943954, 0.03011426515877247, -0.008177628740668297, 0.010212444700300694, 0.03276587277650833, -0.030058978125452995, -0.035630106925964355, 0.06535429507493973, -0.022687621414661407, 0.019177785143256187, -0.012240317650139332, -0.01069896575063467, -0.035224560648202896, 0.011728206649422646, -0.005540072917938232, 0.030622679740190506, 0.011687525548040867, -0.012296075001358986, 0.03356098011136055, -0.029073012992739677, 1.4276494833050581e-34, -0.014887606725096703, -0.02355978637933731, 0.03935440257191658, -0.09915656596422195, 0.031166302040219307, 0.0033658405300229788, -0.03698457032442093, -0.02003837376832962, 0.03709956631064415, 0.011140490882098675, 0.0009415363310836256], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "a54580e15c27e50aba901566d0a0e7de5b8790fbc0acf5e16443dd7aa1266563", "content": "This chunk discusses the influence of adverse childhood experiences (ACEs) on behavior, emphasizing their potential long-term harmful effects. It is situated within a section focusing on negative influences on pupil behavior in schools and explores how understanding ACEs can inform tailored support strategies for students, particularly in relation to their behavioral challenges.These levers can be more powerful than the risk of negative consequences (such as an anti-bullying sanction).\nBox 3: A social network experiment\f13Improving behaviour in schools\nNegative experiences that might influence behaviour\nThere is a growing body of research identifying the harmful effects of adverse childhood experiences (ACEs) on the rest of a person\u2019s life. ACEs are significant stressful events occurring during childhood or adolescence and can be direct, such as the child suffering abuse or neglect, or indirect, such as the child\u2019s parent suffering mental illness or drug addiction. The research suggests that ACEs have a strong link with chronic diseases, social and emotional issues, with a higher ACE score correlating with worse outcomes on all fronts. Two thirds of people have at least one ACE, but the 8% of people in England who have four or more ACEs are at an increased risk of a range of negative health outcomes such as heart disease, respiratory disease, drug addiction, or self-harm.13\nACEs can be a helpful way of understanding how services can be tailored towards individuals. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Improving_behaviour_in_schools_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/18VgDE6jXP5eN_4w_IPbsUVu8tW-V4ep2", "source_id": "7a1d3f44ce494b71f1d9e0fe5b7517c578d4c867369077d76a9ccd359cf1603d", "page_number": 14, "split_id": 26, "split_idx_start": 23703, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "f3751f1467616413a682950d567c5e271e0836f63264af26f09420abc0faf2cf", "range": [949, 1059]}, {"doc_id": "de434907dfd257d447c68b4d587c5c7871b2f90712d9f0617b20f63578083a16", "range": [0, 33