2538160085678, -0.003090740879997611, -0.011742782779037952, 0.016917439177632332, -0.019496187567710876, 0.011463259346783161, 0.024472329765558243, 0.021446475759148598, 0.006975775584578514, -0.009718219749629498, 0.010782302357256413, -0.02521316520869732, 0.06411641836166382, -0.0005536916432902217, 0.04512438923120499, 0.07433167099952698, -0.0026550397742539644, 0.004945703782141209, -0.004933802876621485, 0.023981712758541107, -0.01706446334719658, 0.006597690284252167, 0.03864616900682449, -0.04783652722835541, 0.05184128135442734, -0.016011441126465797, 0.00874393805861473, 0.030229510739445686, 0.03061859682202339, -0.0028563961386680603, 0.020903415977954865, -0.0040770890191197395, -0.058845847845077515, -0.05468420311808586, 0.003278701798990369, 1.8133856586399878e-34, 0.0033195645082741976, 0.011622903868556023, -0.00612171646207571, -0.10208728164434433, 0.018585950136184692, -0.0038666443433612585, -0.02300495095551014, 0.002021920634433627, -0.015362477861344814, 0.04627605900168419, -0.011929220519959927], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "de434907dfd257d447c68b4d587c5c7871b2f90712d9f0617b20f63578083a16", "content": "This chunk is situated within the section discussing adverse childhood experiences (ACEs) and their impact on pupil behavior, highlighting the importance of understanding trauma in order to tailor support effectively. It connects to Recommendation 5, which emphasizes personalized interventions for students, particularly those facing behavioral challenges linked to their experiences.Two thirds of people have at least one ACE, but the 8% of people in England who have four or more ACEs are at an increased risk of a range of negative health outcomes such as heart disease, respiratory disease, drug addiction, or self-harm.13\nACEs can be a helpful way of understanding how services can be tailored towards individuals. This research is in its early stages even within healthcare, but as the terminology is becoming more prevalent in schools, some are beginning to use ACEs as a framework to understand their pupils. Knowing about any trauma a pupil has experienced or is experiencing can inform support a school provides. See Recommendation 5 (description of St Mary\u2019s) for an example of how one school is using this theory as part of their tailored support for pupils in relation to their behaviour.\f14\nEducation Endowment Foundation \u2022 Whole-school anti-bullying policy\n\u2022 Classroom rules: often set collaboratively by the class\n\u2022 School conferences: assemblies to introduce the initiative and inform pupils about bullying\n\u2022 Cooperative group work: school staff cooperating to work with bullies and victims of bullying\n\u2022 Information for parents: this could include a manual to structure a teacher\u2019s conversation or a leaflet for parents to digest at home\n\u2022 Improved playground supervision\n\u2022 Classroom management: see recommendation 3\n\u2022 Disciplinary methods: punitive measures such as being sent to the headteacher or being deprived of privileges\n\u2022 Teacher training: training taking place over more than 4 days and lasting 10 hours or more was most effective\n\u2022 Parent training/meetings: educational events for parents relating specifically to bullying\nBullying\nA key influence on a child\u2019s behaviour in school is being the victim of bullying. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Improving_behaviour_in_schools_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/18VgDE6jXP5eN_4w_IPbsUVu8tW-V4ep2", "source_id": "7a1d3f44ce494b71f1d9e0fe5b7517c578d4c867369077d76a9ccd359cf1603d", "page_number": 15, "split_id": 27, "split_idx_start": 24476, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "a54580e15c27e50aba901566d0a0e7de5b8790fbc0acf5e16443dd7aa1266563", "range": [773, 1109]}, {"doc_id": "74c8197b6d32a3ff665a8ef1244c2f8f66152874f6ce60f455d79077fdcbf764", "range": [0, 941]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.0768682137131691, -0.08501315116882324, 0.0