c outcomes and self-regulation, ultimately reducing the likelihood of misbehavior in school.@\n@\n@ @@\f16\nEducation Endowment Foundation 2\nThe importance of teaching learning behaviours Managing a child\u2019s misbehaviour does not necessarily lead to that child learning: they may be quieter, but not necessarily engaging with the content of the lesson. Instead, research suggests that when children improve their learning behaviours, this skill set can improve both academic achievement and cognitive ability. 20\nPupils who are aware of their own behaviour, who can self-regulate and deploy coping skills, will be less likely to misbehave in school. Once such strategies have been developed and strengthened, they turn into essential life skills and help students to become motivated and determined to succeed. Behaviour-for-\nlearning approaches can be supported by the evidence on social and emotional learning, self-regulation, and essential life skills.\n", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Improving_behaviour_in_schools_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/18VgDE6jXP5eN_4w_IPbsUVu8tW-V4ep2", "source_id": "7a1d3f44ce494b71f1d9e0fe5b7517c578d4c867369077d76a9ccd359cf1603d", "page_number": 17, "split_id": 32, "split_idx_start": 28947, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "6348da76f17a2229cb66113601730d611af3dd344037a97d68c14bfc0cb4654f", "range": [652, 908]}, {"doc_id": "3f5191afd967aa45ce4aaea8890a59564e0850e1c639d848c92e25408d7d94d4", "range": [0, 146]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.060588836669921875, -0.07846787571907043, -0.0029244970064610243, -0.0021979736629873514, -0.001651268219575286, 0.06013865768909454, 0.05178867280483246, 0.001958683133125305, 0.002639398444443941, 0.01783710904419422, 0.019747763872146606, 0.02101033180952072, -0.033027730882167816, -0.011848481371998787, 0.03635082393884659, -0.0683337077498436, 0.011509821750223637, -0.012577492743730545, -0.0073817078955471516, 0.02542576938867569, -0.00946544297039