 situated in the section discussing the importance of teaching learning behaviours alongside managing misbehaviour. It highlights the framework developed by Ellis and Tod for evaluating approaches to behaviour in the classroom, emphasizing the interconnectedness of students' relationships with themselves, peers, and the curriculum.What are the learning behaviours that we should focus on?\nAlthough much of the research available focuses on evaluating specific interventions or outcomes,21 Ellis and Tod\u2019s recent review of the literature provides a conceptual framework for teachers to use to evaluate approaches to managing misbehaviour and to teach behaviour for learning.\nThe model (see Figure 3) suggests that each of three pupil relationships \u2013 with themselves, with others and with the curriculum - impacts on the other, and positive change can be achieved by recognising which of these relationships needs to be developed or strengthened with specific teaching. This could be for the whole class, for a small group, or on an individual basis. In Recommendation 1 we explored the possible factors that influence pupil behaviour and the extent \u2018The successful management of behaviour relies on far more than a set of strategies to draw upon when pupils misbehave\u2019 16 Ellis and Tod (2018)\nEmotional\nSocial\nCognitive\f17Improving behaviour in schools\nto which schools can affect these. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Improving_behaviour_in_schools_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/18VgDE6jXP5eN_4w_IPbsUVu8tW-V4ep2", "source_id": "7a1d3f44ce494b71f1d9e0fe5b7517c578d4c867369077d76a9ccd359cf1603d", "page_number": 18, "split_id": 34, "split_idx_start": 30844, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "3f5191afd967aa45ce4aaea8890a59564e0850e1c639d848c92e25408d7d94d4", "range": [1183, 1241]}, {"doc_id": "95d757291dc39e4a79bfdd12cc4480bca90992a5a363c760f3d2c81e0b185046", "range": [0, 338]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.08858