": "91f19d9cd62a65a6154cc2d0d703cd940586a8aa238ee3a247cc9fa9d805ffef", "content": "This chunk is part of Recommendation 2, which emphasizes the importance of teaching learning behaviours in conjunction with managing misbehaviour. It discusses the dynamic interplay between emotional, social, and cognitive factors that influence pupil behaviour, highlighting the relationships learners have with themselves, their peers, and the curriculum.The triangle surrounding this behaviour is the \u2018triangle of influence\u2019 representing the behaviour being influenced by emotional, social, and cognitive factors. These factors and the learning behaviour itself can be addressed through the three relationships (with self, others, and the curriculum) experienced by the individual in the classroom. Changing a learning behaviour is a dynamic process with reciprocal influences, as represented by the arrows. The circle, and terms inside this, show the influence of context. For example, if dealing with setbacks was placed in the middle of the triangle, although this is a mostly emotional learning behaviour, it will be influenced by social and cognitive factors\u2014perhaps being embarrassed in front of peers (a social factor) is often a reason the pupil wants to give up, and limited experience of success is influencing the want to give up more quickly. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Improving_behaviour_in_schools_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/18VgDE6jXP5eN_4w_IPbsUVu8tW-V4ep2", "source_id": "7a1d3f44ce494b71f1d9e0fe5b7517c578d4c867369077d76a9ccd359cf1603d", "page_number": 19, "split_id": 36, "split_idx_start": 32309, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "95d757291dc39e4a79bfdd12cc4480bca90992a5a363c760f3d2c81e0b185046", "range": [747, 907]}, {"doc_id": "ff72f74641aa0c14604fed94d5f13b0d7825d8c7f74ecb0b2e82c6b1588c0f0e", "range": [0, 381]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.06228361651301384, -0.12203140556812286, -0.012685487046837807, -0.