205996334552765, 0.06241443380713463, 0.027445770800113678, 0.0029148696921765804, -0.008280125446617603, -0.011568550020456314, 0.014135482721030712, 0.036955300718545914, 0.031344786286354065, 0.09934009611606598, -0.008776633068919182, 0.01468687318265438, 0.055964358150959015, 0.022712238132953644, -0.036596495658159256, -0.0064305770210921764, 0.002737147267907858, -0.032976530492305756, 0.01101149432361126, 0.009917303919792175, 0.05287265405058861, -0.01901061460375786, 0.013812593184411526, 0.02243981882929802, -0.0017859089421108365, -0.014218160882592201, -0.01819366216659546, 0.0067983996123075485, -0.042353030294179916, 2.2125303903858352e-34, -0.011858051642775536, -0.05929683893918991, 0.029138214886188507, -0.06723830848932266, -0.010108561255037785, -0.028016550466418266, 0.03105287253856659, 0.032900501042604446, 0.04268025606870651, 0.04344375059008598, -0.06616124510765076], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "ff72f74641aa0c14604fed94d5f13b0d7825d8c7f74ecb0b2e82c6b1588c0f0e", "content": "This chunk is located in the section discussing the importance of teaching learning behaviours alongside managing misbehaviour. It outlines how setbacks can influence student engagement and provides strategies for teachers to enhance emotional, social, and cognitive relationships to support pupils in overcoming challenges and improving their classroom behaviour.For example, if dealing with setbacks was placed in the middle of the triangle, although this is a mostly emotional learning behaviour, it will be influenced by social and cognitive factors\u2014perhaps being embarrassed in front of peers (a social factor) is often a reason the pupil wants to give up, and limited experience of success is influencing the want to give up more quickly. A teacher hoping to improve this pupil\u2019s responses to setbacks could respond by:\n\u2022 increasing engagement (relationship with self) \u2014\ndiscussing a time the pupil has given up when experiencing a setback and challenging them to stick with the next task, listening to any concerns the pupil has about being able to achieve or issues with self-motivation;\n\u2022 improving access (relationship with curriculum)\u2014ensuring the pupil has appropriately-\nlevelled work that will lead to the experience of success as long as they stick with it; praising the pupil for achieving and highlighting that their perseverance got them through it; and\n\u2022 ensuring participation (relationship with others) \u2014\nset a classroom culture where pupils are proud to say they found a task difficult at first and are not afraid to get things wrong.\nA range of learning behaviours is outlined below though there may be other terms and concepts you already use that suit your own school context better.\n\u2022 Emotional learning behaviours: inner voice, mental well-being, dealing with setbacks; and self-esteem, self-worth, and self-competence.\n\u2022 Social learning behaviours: pupil relationship with teacher, pupil relationship with peers, collaborative learning, and bullying.\n", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Improving_behaviour_in_schools_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/18VgDE6jXP5eN_4w_IPbsUVu8tW-V4ep2", "source_id": "7a1d3f44ce494b71f1d9e0fe5b7517c578d4c867369077d76a9ccd359cf1603d", "page_number": 19, "split_id": 37, "split_idx_start": 32829, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "91f19d9cd62a65a6154cc2d0d703cd940586a8aa238ee3a247cc9fa9d805ffef", "range": [520, 901]}, {"doc_id": "74e3f7d6f77f752f92d1a3aaf27eca462d8e18262a4c875733f8f885550432f5", "range": [0, 132]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.015997420996427536, -0.09303543716669083, -0.0009128609672188759, 0.018767336383461952, -0.004728068597614765, 0.027395904064178467, -0.010567216202616692, -0.004826501477509737, -0.027176517993211746, 0.02058158628642559, 0.05237860605120659, 0.014452041126787663, 0.004314010497182608, 0.006656178273260593, 0.014618330635130405, -0.0946211963891983, -0.0096019