1109738945961, 0.01583554595708847, 0.003262850223109126, -0.015166462399065495, 0.012439134530723095, -0.004771669395267963, 0.0013589418958872557, -0.006424287334084511, 0.04437119886279106, -0.024592343717813492, -0.013023474253714085, 0.03658333793282509, 0.0050751399248838425, -0.017876723781228065, -0.04476034268736839, -0.007759456057101488, -0.029255298897624016, 1.968945690787639e-34, -0.011576496064662933, -0.029547540470957756, 0.022836944088339806, -0.028516029939055443, -0.012617403641343117, -0.02614356018602848, 0.06571999937295914, 0.028186418116092682, 0.030071396380662918, 0.0706600621342659, -0.04991299286484718], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "74e3f7d6f77f752f92d1a3aaf27eca462d8e18262a4c875733f8f885550432f5", "content": "This chunk is part of the section discussing the importance of teaching learning behaviours alongside managing misbehaviour. It outlines specific types of learning behaviours, including social and cognitive aspects, within a conceptual framework, emphasizing the role of effective teacher-pupil interactions and motivation in fostering a productive classroom environment.\u2022 Social learning behaviours: pupil relationship with teacher, pupil relationship with peers, collaborative learning, and bullying.\n\u2022 Cognitive learning behaviours: motivation, growth mindset, working memory/\ncognitive load, and communication\u2014improving through effective teacher-pupil dialogue, modelling.\nServices Family\nRelationship with others\nRelationship with self\nRelationship\nwith curriculum\nCommunity / Cultures\nPolicy\nLearning behaviour\nFigure 3: The behaviour or learning conceptual framework adapted from Powell and Tod 2004.\f18\nEducation Endowment Foundation Box 4: Motivation\nExtrinsic motivation\u2014in the form of external influences such as gaining rewards and praise\u2014is useful to address some minor misbehaviours or to encourage positive behaviour. Teachers can use tangible techniques such as rewards and sanctions, or le