8604130745, 0.008349891752004623, 0.02312465012073517, -0.01775112934410572, 0.012030095793306828, 0.09026084095239639, 0.034911468625068665, 0.04756132885813713, -0.027341334149241447, -0.014539536088705063, 0.019916828721761703, -0.0017932537011802197, -0.049200598150491714, -0.011107435449957848, -0.09798374772071838, 0.05204188823699951, -0.007572417613118887, 0.015972772613167763, -0.03567654639482498, 0.03309927508234978, 0.03741496428847313, -0.009042458608746529, 0.021622855216264725, -0.032936546951532364, -0.003436190076172352, 0.00047050995635800064, -0.0009096700232475996, 0.00766433821991086, -0.014799961820244789, -0.005297885742038488, 0.017974089831113815, 0.02412845380604267, -0.033489055931568146, -0.03206521272659302, 0.004922281019389629, 0.04404512792825699, 0.04845521226525307, -0.015418031252920628, -0.004207946360111237, -0.02249041572213173, -0.02506908029317856, 0.022729136049747467, 0.01865176297724247, 0.032272692769765854, 0.07626927644014359, -0.0025026199873536825, -0.01941603049635887, -0.01020084135234356, 0.032155316323041916, 6.376405508490279e-05, -0.010067185387015343, 0.005860324949026108, -0.04590073972940445, 0.021623987704515457, 0.028154700994491577, 0.04813506454229355, -0.043535854667425156, 0.007157200016081333, -0.005877050571143627, 0.0214488934725523, -0.010748815722763538, -0.05669618397951126, 0.01668114773929119, -0.022664282470941544, 1.74572137986515e-34, -0.029864590615034103, -0.06260422617197037, 0.028315464034676552, -0.0793718546628952, -0.0060715219005942345, -0.010420815087854862, -0.012001979164779186, 0.017867442220449448, 0.04903163015842438, 0.0711812973022461, -0.0512835793197155], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "15a8fe15f2c13fece679e8d70318afd274b26699eaa2d216b8465b2beaa7b9de", "content": "This chunk appears in the section discussing the importance of teaching learning behaviours alongside managing misbehaviour. It emphasizes the role of intrinsic motivation in improving pupil behaviour and academic achievement, referencing Carol Dweck's research on growth mindset and its impact on resilience and social dynamics in the classroom setting.Teachers can use tangible techniques such as rewards and sanctions, or less tangible strategies such as praise and criticism, to improve motivation, behaviour, and learning. However, it is intrinsic motivation, or self-motivation, that is crucial to improving resilience, achieving goals, and ultimately is the key determiner to success. Children who are intrinsically motivated achieve better and are less likely to misbehave.\nCarol Dweck\u2019s research on \u2018growth mindset\u2019\u2014the theory that intelligence is not a fixed characteristic but can instead be increased through effort\u2014suggests that teaching students to have this mindset can motivate children not only to improve academically but also to behave better. 23 In her book, Dweck also discusses how teaching children skills such as collaboration and self-improvement can reduce bullying and empower bystanders to stand up to bullying that may be taking place. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Improving_behaviour_in_schools_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/18VgDE6jXP5eN_4w_IPbsUVu8tW-V4ep2", "source_id": "7a1d3f44ce494b71f1d9e0fe5b7517c578d4c867369077d76a9ccd359cf1603d", "page_number": 20, "split_id": 39, "split_idx_start": 35074, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "74e3f7d6f77f752f92d1a3aaf27eca462d8e18262a4c875733f8f885550432f5", "range": [762, 936]}, {"doc_id": "5d216629d1d5d5f1b60ab117163e1c66451fc8ea504e16c0d15a702bc974ab25", "range": [0, 202]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.07444552332162857, -0.031730443239212036, 0.007937745191156864, 0.011905801482498646, 0.005719644483178854, 0.07164258509874344, 0.011330507695674896, -0.032572194933891296, -0.029436664655804634, 0.04315788298845291, 0.018118925392627716, 0.020825261250138283, 0.01802908442914486, -0.07102423906326294, 0.039142441004514694, -0.07258524745702