60744, 0.05401808023452759, -0.007509968709200621, -0.01024000532925129, 0.030783843249082565, -0.03764549270272255, 0.015022067353129387, -0.006382117513567209, 0.054162003099918365, -0.01355182845145464, -0.021121514961123466, 0.023314055055379868, -0.014865219593048096, 0.0209968201816082, -0.05457358434796333, -0.011668110266327858, -0.02004016749560833, 1.7425995469676294e-34, -0.03383573144674301, -0.040750425308942795, 0.044529203325510025, -0.09193859249353409, 0.0020544929429888725, 9.710666927276179e-05, -0.004355019424110651, -0.016110476106405258, 0.0063606868498027325, 0.05152289196848869, -0.05357832834124565], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "5d216629d1d5d5f1b60ab117163e1c66451fc8ea504e16c0d15a702bc974ab25", "content": "This chunk is situated in the section discussing the importance of teaching learning behaviors alongside managing misbehavior. It highlights Carol Dweck's concept of the \"growth mindset\" and its potential benefits in reducing bullying and promoting positive classroom interactions through intrinsic motivation, while acknowledging the challenges teachers face in implementing these concepts effectively.23 In her book, Dweck also discusses how teaching children skills such as collaboration and self-improvement can reduce bullying and empower bystanders to stand up to bullying that may be taking place. 24\nEncouraging a growth mindset rather than a fixed mindset involves, as a teacher:\n\u2022 having a growth mindset yourself\u2014truly believe that all your students will achieve and improve;\n\u2022 praising a students\u2019 effort rather than the person, the end piece of work or results (\u2018You have worked so hard on this\u2019; \u2018You\u2019re persevering brilliantly through tough new concepts\u2019); and\n\u2022 avoiding fixed mindset labelling that praises intelligence or talent (\u2018You\u2019re so clever\u2019; \u2018You\u2019re so talented\u2019).\nIntrinsic motivation approaches are rooted in cognitive psychology but are challenging to implement impactfully in schools. With growth mindset in particular, it has become very popular for schools to promote a growth mindset but study results have been mixed. Dweck herself has warned that mindset approaches are difficult to implement: \u201cIt\u2019s really hard to pass a growth mindset on to others and create a growth mindset culture. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Improving_behaviour_in_schools_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/18VgDE6jXP5eN_4w_IPbsUVu8tW-V4ep2", "source_id": "7a1d3f44ce494b71f1d9e0fe5b7517c578d4c867369077d76a9ccd359cf1603d", "page_number": 20, "split_id": 40, "split_idx_start": 35783, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "15a8fe15f2c13fece679e8d70318afd274b26699eaa2d216b8465b2beaa7b9de", "range": [709, 911]}, {"doc_id": "892efc934efb55ae7095cf9aa253f6347688d4b15f84396b57ab5a44be568d41", "range": [0, 170]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.0691627636551857, 0.014645978808403015, 0.013684999197721481, 0.020602762699127197, 0.011581196449697018, 0.05523368716239929, -0.020874079316854477, -0.03854276239871979, -0.01819094642996788, 0.049392055720090866, 0.01761961169540882, 0.010672680102288723, 0.02062092162668705, -0.02272305265069008, 0.006878427229821682, -0.07745171338319778, -0.0029940148815512657, 0.017652595415711403, -0.028774961829185486, 0.021858735010027885, -0.03840477764606476, -0.008246012963354588, -0.0029286914505064487, -0.01042081881314516, -0.05115533992648125, -0.034902069717645645, 0.05559217184782028, 0.024005262181162834, 0.008764606900513172, -0.008785095997154713, 0.0214287880808115, -0.006657087709754705, -0.011161413975059986, -0.017836574465036392, 1.8766091898214654e-06, -0.015279385261237621, -0.007092328742146492, -0.053566258400678635, -0.003266223706305027, 0.02919124998152256, 0.010321577079594135, 0.020648332312703133, -0.006571785546839237, 0.014095567166805267, 0.008059686981141567, 0.005137881729751825, -0.015827002003788948, 0.058635607361