47688d4b15f84396b57ab5a44be568d41", "content": "This chunk is located in the section discussing the importance of teaching learning behaviours, specifically focusing on the challenges of implementing growth mindset approaches in schools. It emphasizes that merely promoting growth mindset concepts is insufficient without a comprehensive, practical application by educators.Dweck herself has warned that mindset approaches are difficult to implement: \u201cIt\u2019s really hard to pass a growth mindset on to others and create a growth mindset culture. It\u2019s not about educators giving a mindset lecture or putting up a poster \u2013 it\u2019s about embodying it in all their practices.\u201d\nSchool leaders should be aware that light-touch training in a growth mindset approach is unlikely to be effective on its own: this strategy requires careful consideration.\f19Improving behaviour in schools\nFirst stop for further reading\nElton (1989) \u2018Discipline in Schools\u2019 , London: Her Majesty\u2019s Stationery Office. http://www.educationengland.org.uk/documents/elton/elton1989.html On classroom environment, the Elton Report provides checklists of strategies to manage and avoid misbehaviour in lessons. The focus is mostly on classroom environment; get this right and you are less likely to have children misbehave.\n", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Improving_behaviour_in_schools_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/18VgDE6jXP5eN_4w_IPbsUVu8tW-V4ep2", "source_id": "7a1d3f44ce494b71f1d9e0fe5b7517c578d4c867369077d76a9ccd359cf1603d", "page_number": 20, "split_id": 41, "split_idx_start": 36732, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "5d216629d1d5d5f1b60ab117163e1c66451fc8ea504e16c0d15a702bc974ab25", "range": [949, 1119]}, {"doc_id": "4c7072406d86ed38813b60a54adf61f77adabc52047e343270fd1af5aeed83f0", "range": [0, 113]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.037494149059057236, -0.01982959918677807, 0.0012521957978606224, 0.011852159164845943, 