78, -0.007233178708702326, -0.001203144434839487, -0.047781337052583694, -0.01857634447515011, 0.10433223098516464, 0.022010302171111107, 0.05602191388607025, -0.03376097232103348, 0.003243096172809601, -0.0017012847820296884, 0.05769205838441849, -0.05599222332239151, 0.01148127019405365, -0.03090861439704895, 0.019132623448967934, 0.007848517037928104, -0.004899469204246998, -0.002606581198051572, 0.04654056206345558, 0.011162353679537773, -0.023408204317092896, 0.05155118182301521, -0.021969230845570564, -0.007541080005466938, 0.04735438525676727, 0.012494244612753391, 0.005800456274300814, 0.03470030054450035, -0.028202027082443237, -0.03908107057213783, 0.009920163080096245, -0.017854517325758934, -0.07494381070137024, -0.003950731363147497, 0.05498075112700462, 0.04915784299373627, 0.0013644478749483824, 0.017033539712429047, 0.0018821393605321646, 0.013969329185783863, 0.04642540216445923, 0.0035474023316055536, 0.03296733647584915, 0.06277304887771606, -0.02123439684510231, -0.010899494402110577, 0.04740900918841362, 0.044828347861766815, -0.021387659013271332, -0.01558517012745142, 0.008159772492945194, -0.034063901752233505, -0.001317773712798953, 0.019840124994516373, 0.05546659976243973, -0.02068878524005413, -0.007130677346140146, 0.01673501916229725, 0.010708220303058624, 0.0028407101053744555, -0.07069748640060425, -0.018009206280112267, -0.02092498540878296, 1.5856746097583301e-34, -0.034297361969947815, -0.030117496848106384, 0.02689245529472828, -0.0836234986782074, -0.03049713745713234, -0.016431758180260658, 0.026166154071688652, 0.016628703102469444, 0.021505316719412804, 0.042917340993881226, -0.03900078684091568], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "70dd67321c464e22ad44fd0ba063d128d13fdf3fcd8a794db04d5727a3f301c4", "content": "This chunk is found in the section discussing effective strategies for improving classroom behavior, specifically focusing on the importance of training teachers in classroom management. It highlights the negative impacts of poor classroom management on teachers and student outcomes, emphasizing the need for structured approaches to enhance classroom environments.This is equivalent to 38 days of teaching lost per year.\u201925\nOfsted, 2014\nUse classroom management strategies to support good classroom behaviour\nTraining for classroom management\nEvidence suggests that effective training for building classroom management expertise involves teachers or school staff:\n\u2022 Reflecting on their own approach;\n\u2022 Trying a new approach; and\n\u2022 Reviewing progress over time. 32 Training teachers in classroom management practices can improve pupil behaviour in the classroom\u2014and not just for those pupils most likely to misbehave. There are several programmes that schools can buy \u2018off the shelf\u2019 to improve classroom management and the evidence suggests these should improve classroom behaviour, particularly in classrooms with above average levels of disruption.\nClassroom management is a major concern for teachers, often leading to stress, burnout, and exit from the profession as well as being a deterrent for those considering teaching as a career.26 It is cited as a challenge for headteachers across all school phases. 27\nEffective classroom management can reduce challenging behaviour, pupil disengagement, bullying, and aggression, leading to improved classroom climate, attendance, and attainment. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Improving_behaviour_in_schools_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/18VgDE6jXP5eN_4w_IPbsUVu8tW-V4ep2", "source_id": "7a1d3f44ce494b71f1d9e0fe5b7517c578d4c867369077d76a9ccd359cf1603d", "page_number": 22, "split_id": 45, "split_idx_start": 39238, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "3a96f29129490f21a55c79523ace320abb034d13ad791097c00de948e6905daf", "range": [660, 1057]}, {"doc_id": "7b466a1e69e3fb70615c12ed898cf40b3d829a6e04e92098456265ea8ba7792a", "range": [0, 182]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.08