 0.012328102253377438, -0.009086011908948421, -0.00946005154401064, 0.016086041927337646, 0.03563564643263817, -0.037882376462221146, -0.01204736065119505, -0.035835184156894684, -0.008854569867253304, 0.012144948355853558, -0.025690963491797447, 0.008970577269792557, -0.01696627028286457, 1.7658121165652636e-34, 0.01470373198390007, -0.0469823032617569, 0.02095303125679493, -0.10386797785758972, 0.016212867572903633, -0.013088408857584, 0.050928786396980286, -0.007678197696805, 0.016977204009890556, 0.04651150479912758, -0.01131411176174879], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "c2058c08937c2b8104a6dd4b1253875cd31360275d7fc2091e1c3c9ae3befd4f", "content": "This chunk is found in the section discussing effective classroom management strategies to support good behavior in schools. It specifically highlights the Incredible Years \u00ae Teacher Classroom Management (TCM) programme as a resource for teachers of young children, emphasizing its role in enhancing classroom management, promoting social skills, and fostering a cooperative learning environment.The TCM programme is part of a suite of programmes for children, parents, and teachers (www.incredibleyears.\ncom). This programme aims to provide teachers of 3-8-year-olds with the skills to effectively manage their classroom and promote children\u2019s social, emotional, and academic competence. The focus is on strengthening teachers\u2019 classroom management strategies , promoting children\u2019s prosocial behaviour , and reducing children\u2019s classroom aggression and non-cooperation with peers and teachers. The programme also helps teachers work with parents to support their school involvement and promote consistency between home and school. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Improving_behaviour_in_schools_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/18VgDE6jXP5eN_4w_IPbsUVu8tW-V4ep2", "source_id": "7a1d3f44ce494b71f1d9e0fe5b7517c578d4c867369077d76a9ccd359cf1603d", "page_number"