.014886497519910336, -0.013971117325127125, -0.007308490574359894, -0.008253791369497776, -0.010150094516575336, 0.02461167983710766, -0.11534307152032852, -0.048053037375211716, -0.04743223637342453, 1.8300347453272104e-34, -0.0202496275305748, -0.06669170409440994, 0.010878345929086208, -0.1253206878900528, -0.012432537041604519, -0.0073936269618570805, 0.047904569655656815, -0.028054310008883476, 0.056480392813682556, 0.042401786893606186, -0.056659433990716934], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "a92fee52c1d68759f2a9d9be478d84b51c920666482df13bf41bfe7b1e81d1cd", "content": "This chunk appears in the section discussing classroom management strategies to support good behaviour. It evaluates the effectiveness of various reward systems, highlighting their use in whole-school approaches and how existing structures in schools might influence the success of such interventions, particularly in relation to specific programs like the Good Behaviour Game.Reward systems are often included in whole-class and whole-school approaches to behaviour and could include star charts or house points systems.\nResearch in the U.K. has been inconclusive about interventions focusing exclusively on reward systems, such as Good Behaviour Game .33 This was shown to have some positive results previously but did not improve outcomes in a recent EEF study, similarly failing to have an impact in other recent studies where the programme was targeted at pupils with behavioural issues. This should not suggest that clear reward and sanction systems in class are ineffective: indeed, the evidence suggests rewards and sanctions can be motivating for children and young people. Schools in England generally have structured reward systems in place which have been adapted carefully to suit their context\u2014indeed, in the EEF study of Good Behaviour Game, most schools in the trial already had systems in place, and perhaps because of this found adopting an external system without adaptation (for the purposes of the trial) challenging. Bought-in systems may be effective in schools without such systems already functioning. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Improving_behaviour_in_schools_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/18VgDE6jXP5eN_4w_IPbsUVu8tW-V4ep2", "source_id": "7a1d3f44ce494b71f1d9e0fe5b7517c578d4c867369077d76a9ccd359cf1603d", "page_number": 25, "split_id": 52, "split_idx_start": 46007, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "0ad6e1905cf2a75077723749283b85f583bac74065a213b2c5ba309f09c6d93c", "range": [900, 1045]}, {"doc_id": "20d12bd9e28fe351522d4a008698bdbd3014e469c01634af7eec08d55cb8b3fc", "range": [0, 88]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.07826116681098938, -0.05115026608109474, -0.00014484293933492154, 0.010360201820731163, -0.010023616254329681, 0.05687607452273369, 0.04110097885131836, -0.0014248138759285212, -0.014793980866670609, 0.05603348836302757, 0.014918999746441841, 0.04727550595998764, 0.014420212246477604, 0.010850245133042336, 0.039735469967126846, -0.052443839609622955, 0.016569821164011955, 0.0027095929253846407, -0.0026540616527199745, 0.023929903283715248, -2.4236656827270053e-05, -0.026044784113764763, 0.00041526678251102567, 0.01882392168045044, -0.008215893991291523, -0.007595838513225317, -0.007197796367108822, -0.015731459483504295, 0.03826054558157921, -0.10087945312261581, 0.06701972335577011, 0.007611692883074284, -0.013582414016127586, -0.005051587242633104, 1.9598610379034653e-06, 0.008249809965491295, 0.02357030101120472, -0.004303728695958853, 0.003962271846830845, 0.037475720047950745, -0.02507229521870613, 0.00822833739221096, 0.02404192090034485, 0.0007944703102111816, 0.005881805904209614, 0.009745253250002861, 0.017620902508497238, 0.0270528644323349, 0.00039483903674408793, -0.06081166863441467, -0.012227127328515053, -0.025237055495381355, -0.025126753374934196, -0.03486502915620804, 0.08629825711250305, -0.02815382182598114, 0.0009152072016149759, 0.025407012552022934, 0.0655223727