 pupil behaviour. It highlights findings from a literature review focusing on classroom-based approaches used worldwide, primarily in primary education settings.Bought-in systems may be effective in schools without such systems already functioning. Figure 5 suggests some different reinforcement strategies, the potential benefits for children, and the extent to which schools should consider using them.\nA review of the literature found 31 studies of classroom-based strategies to manage behaviour aimed at the whole class. Studies came from a range of countries including the U.K. though most were from the U.S.A. (19). The vast majority of studies took place in primary schools, with only three studies including secondary-age pupils.\n", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Improving_behaviour_in_schools_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/18VgDE6jXP5eN_4w_IPbsUVu8tW-V4ep2", "source_id": "7a1d3f44ce494b71f1d9e0fe5b7517c578d4c867369077d76a9ccd359cf1603d", "page_number": 25, "split_id": 53, "split_idx_start": 47069, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "a92fee52c1d68759f2a9d9be478d84b51c920666482df13bf41bfe7b1e81d1cd", "range": [1062, 1150]}, {"doc_id": "0878b7d6cb55bf1989e7bf8696c63e53000e2f10fd351703abd4975a3da6c1d1", "range": [0, 116]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.08872175961732864, -0.08469708263874054, 0.00010103216482093558, -0.0032061240635812283, -0.018117792904376984, 0.018845994025468826, 0.08253549039363861, -0.012735715135931969, -0.03895366191864014, 0.03055664338171482, 0.025256605818867683, 0.011658075265586376, -0.010235749185085297, -0.001489973277784884, 0.035310059785842896, -0.10225984454154968, 0.022908661514520645, -0.00799041148275137, -0.04078734293580055, 0.04144144430756569, -0.010062254965305328, -0.03522558882832527, -0.007834779098629951, -0.0028496861923485994, -0.0045603117905557156, 0.004497856833040714, -0.0055543528869748116, -0.001449326053261757, 0.021453948691487312, -0.05623269081115723, 0.11654936