6, -0.09212502837181091, -0.03410107269883156, -0.04291721433401108, 1.7762474987880664e-34, 0.0012879754649475217, -0.04818064719438553, 0.027044905349612236, -0.07840842008590698, -0.0014820531941950321, 0.004618752282112837, 0.012222879566252232, 0.008964572101831436, 0.04777749255299568, 0.00983679760247469, -0.04569566622376442], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "0878b7d6cb55bf1989e7bf8696c63e53000e2f10fd351703abd4975a3da6c1d1", "content": "This chunk is located in the section discussing the effectiveness of various classroom management strategies outlined in the guidance report on improving behaviour in schools. It emphasizes the positive impact of teacher training in classroom management, specifically highlighting the Incredible Years\u00ae programme and the importance of combining training with reinforcement strategies.The vast majority of studies took place in primary schools, with only three studies including secondary-age pupils.\nOverall, consistently positive (small to medium) effects are seen for approaches that train teachers in classroom management approaches. Of programmes available in the U.K., the Incredible Years \u00ae Teacher Classroom Management programme is the \u2018off-\nthe-shelf\u2019 programme with the largest evidence base, accounting for seven studies in our literature review.\nEvidence for \u2018bought-in\u2019 reinforcement programmes is mixed; however, most programmes that focus on teacher training in classroom management also involve reinforcement systems. This combination of training teachers alongside delivering a rewards-focused reinforcement programme holds most promise. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Improving_behaviour_in_schools_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/18VgDE6jXP5eN_4w_IPbsUVu8tW-V4ep2", "source_id": "7a1d3f44ce494b71f1d9e0fe5b7517c578d4c867369077d76a9ccd359cf1603d", "page_number": 25, "split_id": 54, "split_idx_start": 47530, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "20d12b