8, 0.053342096507549286, -0.0034431698732078075, -0.016278598457574844, -0.016395585611462593, -0.006095679011195898, 0.04925280436873436, -0.07242250442504883, -0.03754252567887306, -0.03592086210846901, 2.165932141280309e-34, 0.008323612622916698, -0.03742118552327156, 0.0036046344321221113, -0.07193748652935028, -0.01742280274629593, 0.0034834479447454214, 0.013486525043845177, -0.01031473372131586, 0.05978656932711601, 0.02651967667043209, -0.06277923285961151], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "b1e8ddc3cece7af59d177877a228e06485e56c7b33bb7042359ee156aba07999", "content": "This chunk is located in the section discussing simple and cost-effective strategies to improve student behaviour and learning outcomes in schools. It highlights the positive impact of breakfast clubs on pupil behaviour and attainment, emphasizing that interventions can be straightforward and affordable, leading to significant improvements in the learning environment.Most of these strategies have little or no cost, yet the research suggests that they could reduce challenging behaviour and lead to improved attainment, improved attendance, and a more purposeful learning climate.\n\u2018Many problems\u2014or behaviors\u2014that affect wellbeing do not require lengthy or complex interventions involving consultations, workshops, training, or support\u2019 34 Dennis Embry and Anthony Biglan, 2008\nBreakfast clubs The EEF \u2018Magic Breakfast\u2019 research showed that pupil behaviour and attainment improved for schools that ran a breakfast club. Interestingly, it appeared that it was not whether more pupils ate breakfast that made the difference, as a similar number of children in the classes reported eating breakfast at the end of the study as at the beginning. It may be that school breakfasts are more nutritious or that attending the club effectively prepares pupils for learning. While around 25% of children attended the clubs, schools that ran a breakfast club saw an improvement in attainment across the class. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Improving_behaviour_in_schools_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/18VgDE6jXP5eN_4w_IPbsUVu8tW-V4ep2", "source_id": "7a1d3f44ce494b71f1d9e0fe5b7517c578d4c867369077d76a9ccd359cf1603d", "page_number": 26, "split_id": 56, "split_idx_start": 49099, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "4531897848fb89b674695c66f3eb3a5b63c91b7aeff9081287d9f17acfbd0fdb", "range": [920, 1134]}, {"doc_id": "867f936417e36238bf009289afaab043ee3ea21cae063f0c190172601185e373", "range": [0, 134]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.044536300003528595, -0.07261104136705399, 0.008416138589382172, 0.009793574921786785, -0.02405957691371441, 0.06860937178134918, 0.02947292849421501, -0.014732823707163334, -0.041063930839300156, 0.010049859993159771, 0.054445844143629074, 0.022564029321074486, 0.03230791538953781, -0.024053940549492836, 0.024256670847535133, -0.055799007415771484, 0.05349258705973625, 0.023434730246663094, -0.06640421599149704, 0.03511055186390877, -0.04564746469259262, -0.012205199338495731, -0.02460660971701145, -0.006578730419278145, 0.006159177981317043, -0.00016244403377640992, -0.0008340808562934399, 0.015363446436822414, 0.00957843754440546, -0.021603692322969437, 0.04790915176272392, 0.045998722314834595, -0.06503674387931824, -0.040121305733919144, 2.0017030237795552e-06, 0.02837955206632614, -0.0264212004840374, 0.021646982058882713, -0.029499463737010956, 0.05548685044050217, 0.01437851320952177, 0.04107873886823654, 0.005486297886818647, 0.03720437362790108, 0.026179790496826172, 0.039892327040433884, 0.010760917328298092, 0.014909015037119389, -0.04480591043829918, -0.0009483249159529805, 0.00981101393699646, -0.041581232100725174, -0.08555140346288681, -0.0329255573451519, 0.0794607549905777, -0.0017016585916280746, 0.015336625277996063, -0.019039997830986977, -0.0071677654050290585, 0.02782248519361019, 0.020541278645396233, 0.005530121270567179, -0.020584577694535255, -0.03111911006