esting their implementation as a proactive measure within behaviour management strategies.While around 25% of children attended the clubs, schools that ran a breakfast club saw an improvement in attainment across the class. Teachers reported improved behaviour in their classrooms, suggesting that breakfast clubs provide an opportunity to improve outcomes for all children, not just those who attend a breakfast club, through better classroom environments.\nSchools should consider breakfast clubs as a cost-\neffective way to raise pupil attainment. Schools wishing to achieve an impact equivalent to two months\u2019 additional progress should aim to deliver a breakfast club similar to the model tested by the EEF: free, universal, and before school.\f25Improving behaviour in schools\nSeveral \u2018pro-active\u2019 behaviour approaches aim to improve behaviour by reinforcing positive behaviours, prior to negative behaviours occurring. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Improving_behaviour_in_schools_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/18VgDE6jXP5eN_4w_IPbsUVu8tW-V4ep2", "source_id": "7a1d3f44ce494b71f1d9e0fe5b7517c578d4c867369077d76a9ccd359cf1603d", "page_number": 26, "split_id": 57, "split_idx_start": 49995, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "b1e8ddc3cece7af59d177877a228e06485e56c7b33bb7042359ee156aba07999", "range": [896, 1030]}, {"doc_id": "5132c8324186ef2498fbd93ec08add56a76d7d091c9e4f6695eac908cff32a94", "range": [0, 176]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.05505046620965004, -0.06566295772790909, 0.01431718934327364, 0.002647602465003729, -0.022018972784280777, 0.05890674516558647, 0.03638700768351555, -0.01875513419508934, -0.06303776800632477, 0.019092394039034843, 0.051540955901145935, 0.013306403532624245, 0.01640891097486019, -1.9768876882153563e-05, 0.025061916559934616, -0.03425898402929306, 0.057474538683891296, 0.025689007714390755, -0.04398403316736221, 0.04473203420639038, -0.0374087318778038, -0.016521217301487923,