392513871193], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "40f1937a3a31aa933e8144149cf6474adba4e0505e734f394208ab461d06f599", "content": "This chunk is taken from the section discussing simple approaches to manage behavior in schools. It highlights the use of positive practice, or \"overcorrection,\" as a strategy to reinforce correct behaviors among pupils, particularly in classrooms with high disruptive behavior, emphasizing the need for consistent implementation and further research on such strategies.Alternatively, they could be used to discourage unwanted behaviours, such as positive practice (or \u2018overcorrection\u2019), where pupils repeat the correct behaviour when they have acted inappropriately \u2013 for example if a child walked down a silent corridor noisily, they might be asked to walk down the corridor again silently. The greatest impact on improving behaviour with simple strategies is seen in classrooms with high rates of disruptive and inattentive behaviour but there is potential for strategies implemented consistently across the school to see bigger changes. Some of the research is small-scale or not UK-specific. More studies are needed to ensure that teachers and headteachers can make informed decisions about simple strategies to implement.\nWhere\u2019s the evidence?\n", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Improving_behaviour_in_schools_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/18VgDE6jXP5eN_4w_IPbsUVu8tW-V4ep2", "source_id": "7a1d3f44ce494b71f1d9e0fe5b7517c578d4c867369077d76a9ccd359cf1603d", "page_number": 29, "split_id": 63, "split_idx_start": 54606, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "7cd2cf03dee5a7e4335e8bd694093388692009911ca7bd9fcde34d7f272f2b18", "range": [958, 1281]}, {"doc_id": "43cdee9c1fab4479c9a7c728d4c0bac729ef0f64fd6971b8a4ba43871b622ce9", "range": [0, 22]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.018977440893650055, -0.07323633879423141, -0.0012673380551859736, -0.014461121521890163, -0.011266582645475864, -0.01306