17297089099884, 0.03420677036046982, -0.04108858481049538, -0.00285524339415133, -0.025465160608291626, 0.0002223566552856937, -0.03636563941836357, -0.08038845658302307, -0.017606206238269806, -0.037928927689790726, 1.8387400615967668e-34, -0.012142039835453033, -0.020483113825321198, 0.0016972486628219485, -0.03595328703522682, -0.010731127113103867, 0.0029424859676510096, 0.014696688391268253, 0.001266610692255199, 0.03442907705903053, -0.0016393378609791398, -0.004524344112724066], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "66d0b138123c34d12a989a632b203a417958b1e415dfdea40c94ed29dfe87a54", "content": "This chunk discusses Charlie Taylor's approach to improving behaviour in schools by emphasizing the importance of simple, consistent practices. It references the effective use of checklists, likening them to a menu for behaviour management. This aligns with the broader themes in the document, which outlines targeted strategies for addressing individual pupil needs.He found that their approach to improving behaviour was similar: do the simple things well with consistency. After reading a book by a surgeon who had implemented simple checklists in hospital to be read aloud prior to surgery to avoid infections, he decided the same could be done for improving behaviour. He refers to the lists as a \u2018menu\u2019 to choose from. See Appendix I.\nBox 7: Charlie Taylor\u2019s checklists\f28\nEducation Endowment Foundation 5\nUse targeted approaches to meet the needs of individuals in your school\nRecommendations 1\u20134 focused on structuring the school and classroom such that unwanted behaviours are less likely to occur. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Improving_behaviour_in_schools_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/18VgDE6jXP5eN_4w_IPbsUVu8tW-V4ep2", "source_id": "7a1d3f44ce494b71f1d9e0fe5b7517c578d4c867369077d76a9ccd359cf1603d", "page_number": 29, "split_id": 66, "split_idx_start": 56585, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "9d0deb