3, 5.4395153711084276e-05, -0.00792399886995554, 0.015792420133948326, 0.035744957625865936, -0.001871096552349627, -0.05736799165606499], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "c2331bfd2afe2a22e55653ed34a33e82c32898fa47a1ea95f86d681b87647c92", "content": "This chunk appears in the section discussing targeted approaches to behavior management in schools, emphasizing the importance of personalized interventions for disruptive pupils that align with overall behavior policies while maintaining high expectations. It addresses the necessity of resilience and understanding in educators to effectively implement these tailored strategies.For pupils who are disruptive, targeted interventions are often most effective when adapted to the needs of the individuals involved.\nHowever, a tailored approach to support an individual\u2019s behaviour should complement the school\u2019s behaviour policy without lowering expectations of any pupil\u2019s behaviour. The research behind Ofsted\u2019s latest inspection framework warns against \u2018expectancy effects\u2019: low expectations communicated from teachers can detrimentally affect pupils\u2019 achievements. Furthermore, children and particularly adolescents have a heightened awareness of fairness: they are unlikely to respond well to differential treatment.\n\u2018A flexible but consistent approach is not a soft option and requires considerable resilience in the adults who are trying to support and teach young people immersed in their own difficulties\u2019 36\nJohn Cornwall, 2015\nT argeted approaches benefit from tailoring\nThere is a wide range of interventions that may improve aspects of school behaviour, including those already covered under other areas of EEF focus\u2014examples include social and emotional learning, parental engagement, and self-regulation (see further reading below). ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Improving_behaviour_in_schools_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/18VgDE6jXP5eN_4w_IPbsUVu8tW-V4ep2", "source_id": "7a1d3f44ce494b71f1d9e0fe5b7517c578d4c867369077d76a9ccd359cf1603d", "page_number": 30, "split_id": 68, "split_idx_start": 57642, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "cac66d6861709c8402019717da96a0fea63ece63e6b7de5573cc6119c4472670", "range": [683, 817]}, {"doc_id": "c2b93d0f76fde8af5bd8227cad5b2e7ff2d2153c6d5d2869ba3ba0fd7a09d6ac", "range": [0, 526]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.09491077810525894, -0.11593811213970184, -0.0010840055765584111, 0.004903826862573624, 0.012410396710038185, 0.041032787412405014, 0.007963642477989197, -0.011579788289964199, 0.0010708071058616042, 0.022512396797537804, 0.06401743739843369, -0.004540719091892242, -0.015675704926252365, -0.018087465316057205, -0.0017547053284943104, -0.08829890191555023, -0.0016812195535749197, 0.010054808109998703, -0.02983012981712818, 0.026019807904958725, 0.0029823763761669397, -0.008790399879217148, -0.015544774010777473, 0.012280112132430077, -0.012235508300364017, -0.020095625892281532, 0.011772120371460915, 0.024231741204857826, 0.001418139087036252, -0.04377381503582001, 0.0926097109913826, 0.0387834757566452, -0.009432323276996613, -0.03298350051045418, 2.0179761577310273e-06, 0.0030617034062743187, -0.007614258211106062, -0.017949769273400307, 0.023991798982024193, 0.0015845675952732563, -0.030845263972878456, -0.04395363852381706, 0.006815157365053892, 0.04234151542186737, 0.008039590902626514, 0.044022589921951294, 0.013670211657881737, 0.017836779356002808, 0.013284744694828987, -0.021460073068737984, 0.003199285827577114, -0.018238719552755356, -0.02707294002175331, -0.016947943717241287, 0.07194874435663223, -0.0290523674339056, 0.0025022223126143217, -0.07373256981372833, -0.003965337760746479, -0.024895846843719482, 0.00204445980489254, -0.02108296938240528, -0.02494315430521965, -0.027106396853923798, -0.016067447140812874, -0.04954887181520462, -0.04251328110694885, -0.06739422678947449, -0.014544127508997917, 0.005714184604585171, 0.0660