3042691871523857, 0.0188306737691164, 0.00853962916880846, -0.03781920671463013, -0.04849847033619881], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "9bbc69c6f5c4b6b234f70fb45768c7afccaa1e760f03bf4fbc559374a47bc772", "content": "This chunk is situated within the section discussing targeted approaches to behaviour management in schools. It emphasizes the importance of understanding disruptive behaviours and implementing individualised strategies, such as daily report cards, to support students with high behaviour needs and improve their classroom conduct effectively.For example, an assessment by the teacher might conclude that a particularly disruptive child\u2019s behaviour is attention-seeking. A school counsellor could speak with the student to confirm. The school team could then decide how to change the attention the pupil gets in class to encourage better behaviour. While effective, this approach can be time-consuming, but the individual techniques may be useful in isolation: identify the challenging behaviours, the triggers and the previous strategies used; decide a positive behavioural strategy to adopt; set goals, benchmarks and responsibilities; implement and monitor progress; summarize and evaluate outcomes. Daily report cards are usually completed once or twice daily by the class teacher in a primary school, or carried to each class and completed by all class teachers in secondary. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Improving_behaviour_in_schools_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/18VgDE6jXP5eN_4w_IPbsUVu8tW-V4ep2", "source_id": "7a1d3f44ce494b71f1d9e0fe5b7517c578d4c867369077d76a9ccd359cf1603d", "page_number": 31, "split_id": 71, "split_idx_start": 60235, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "07108ef3e53977f07cbf8a8671799cdb4de04fcf8bd611c01d56b567756517da", "range": [760, 888]}, {"doc_id": "3251908af811b80f140b23542f62c4e6067f135a0392ef4d36de32c204359aae", "range": [0, 178]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.051130905747413635,