a79437eb3ab93f571896acb0c474fd4", "content": "This chunk is part of the \"Consistency is key\" section, discussing the inconsistency in the application of behaviour policies among teachers in schools. It emphasizes the need for school leaders to support their staff in implementing strategies to enhance uniformity in behaviour management across classrooms, referencing Tom Bennett's insights on cultivating a positive school culture.The same report revealed that only a quarter of secondary and half of primary teachers agreed that the behaviour policy in their school was applied consistently by all staff. Only a third of all teachers said the headteacher provided support in managing behaviour. School leaders can support their teachers and middle leaders to achieve better consistency between the classrooms in their school, and any new strategies should aim towards this.\n\u201cIt is reasonably straightforward to identify what a good culture might look like, but like a diet, the difficulty lies in embedding and maintaining it. This includes staff training, effective use of consequences, data monitoring, staff and student surveys and maintaining standards.\u201d Tom Bennett, 2017\n\u201cOnly a quarter of secondary and half of primary teachers agreed that the behaviour policy in their school was applied consistently.\u201d\f33Improving behaviour in schools\nZero tolerance behaviour policies, sometimes described as \u201cno excuses\u201d, aim to create a strict and clear whole-\nschool approach to discipline. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Improving_behaviour_in_schools_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/18VgDE6jXP5eN_4w_IPbsUVu8tW-V4ep2", "source_id": "7a1d3f44ce494b71f1d9e0fe5b7517c578d4c867369077d76a9ccd359cf1603d", "page_number": 34, "split_id": 83, "split_idx_start": 69436, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "0e3feb9619325267c2c45784363f63053e3e09bc932340666575ae8c40b1af93", "range": [641, 816]}, {"doc_id": "031531b4ee691cb9a