92117405, -0.01458729151636362, 0.029674431309103966, 0.019013142213225365, -0.027754079550504684, -0.010453525930643082, -0.0026678170543164015, 2.0073418821425547e-34, 0.0010646627051755786, -0.04131581261754036, 0.02026575803756714, -0.08087284117937088, -0.01607937179505825, 0.013514986261725426, 0.020096154883503914, -0.0174051932990551, 0.03830703720450401, -0.006715124007314444, -0.009961508214473724], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "0da40085ea0cc402b335ce7e691175b8f0a249eb0de74a6e6d9aa99522fc2ac0", "content": "This chunk is located within the section discussing the effectiveness of various behaviour management strategies in schools, specifically addressing the contentious nature of zero tolerance policies. It highlights criticisms and the need for robust evaluations while suggesting alternatives like consistent training and adaptable approaches to improve pupil behaviour overall.41 On the other hand, some have criticised the use of zero tolerance, with MPs on the Education Select Committee in England expressing concern that such policies may be linked unnecessary exclusions. 42\nTo date, very few robust studies have assessed the impact of zero tolerance policies on pupil outcomes, and no high-quality studies have been completed in English schools. It would be valuable to conduct more rigorous evaluations. However, in the absence of such studies, the wider evidence base may help schools considering whether to change any aspect of their behaviour policy. For example, providing training for teachers in classroom management and having a consistent approach across the school that can be adapted for some pupils with specific needs are likely to improve behaviour within zero tolerance or alternative approaches.\n", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Improving_behaviour_in_schools_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/18VgDE6jXP5eN_4w_IPbsUVu8tW-V4ep2", "source_id": "7a1d3f44ce494b71f1d9e0fe5b7517c578d4c867369077d76a9ccd359cf1603d", "page_number": 35, "split_id": 85, "split_idx_start": 71025, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "031531b4ee691cb9ad0ced833b6a9e062485f8cbf6e60b55e21e6125750e986d", "range": [992, 1192]}, {"doc_id": "cc8cfc989400d3587be4f2090a7eb13683625acd0c3df4ce6f636a1d1be09e5e", "range": [0, 257]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.05371495708823204, -0.0764782652258873, 0.01690681092441082, 0.020052308216691017, -0.019189374521374702, 0.02691798284649849, 0.059720974415540695, 0.011843510903418064, -0.030631428584456444, 0.0004374161653686315, 0.04250551387667656, 0.023607874289155006, -0.02006210945546627, -0.0009591207490302622, 0.0066857244819402695, -0.05246791988611221, -0.011205573566257954, 0.012987637892365456, -0.06254034489393234, 0.028589844703674316, 0.006962161976844072, -0.024006647989153862, -0.009863871149718761, 0.015889311209321022, -0.0245321337133646, -0.02293066680431366, -0.00448228046298027, -0.00014460015518125147, 0.00707273930311203, -0.0385478250682354, 0.06126729026436806, 0.0011767229298129678, -0.014923580922186375, -0.009484275244176388, 1.9675933344842633e-06, 0.015835514292120934, 0.0024444088339805603, -0.011900312267243862, 0.027507249265909195, -0.013494214043021202, -0.025153670459985733, -0.023566337302327156, 0.017959970980882645, 0.05573936924338341, -0.009832588024437428, 0.038495391607284546, 0.01083532627671957, 0.009222651831805706, -0.012310474179685116, -0.0272392425686121, 0.0011297327000647783, 0.020357733592391014, -0.06532150506973267, -0.032575007528066635, 0.09005095809698105, -0.01822800375521183, 0.026221394538879395, -0.05054487660527229, 0.015154987573623657, -0.04335540533065796, -0.022093001753091812, 0.008354974910616875, -0.013876368291676044, -0.058017898350954056, -0.005794776603579521, -0.006342185661196709, -0.014458137564361095, -0.06700176000595093, 0.016276486217975616, -0.02374638058245182, 0.051280148327350616, 0.04015070199966431, 0.031626760959625244, -0.0072538829408586025, -0.0035963719710707664, 0.0179