9662618637, -0.06763381510972977, -0.027578601613640785, 0.03435305505990982, -0.020809287205338478, 0.04482002183794975, 0.03806095942854881, 0.03979753330349922, 0.010004311800003052, 0.05318692699074745, -0.051243431866168976, -0.007270283531397581, -0.02840356156229973, -0.0021019650157541037, 0.023925038054585457, -0.021932803094387054, 0.02005891315639019, -0.03995887190103531, 0.008063309825956821, -0.018405595794320107, -0.03642505779862404, 0.0273894090205431, 0.018964428454637527, 0.04954136162996292, -0.03127354010939598, -0.04290545731782913, -0.002882053144276142, -0.02655860036611557, 0.056076522916555405, 0.020922774448990822, 0.027366558089852333, 0.026077883318066597, -0.005148110445588827, -0.039985597133636475, 0.026796933263540268, 0.03078801929950714, 0.00323586817830801, -0.009922715835273266, 0.005421540234237909, -0.040384646505117416, -0.04012187197804451, 0.0019251530757173896, 0.07876963168382645, -0.04607406631112099, -0.017056768760085106, -0.035655368119478226, 0.011448202654719353, 0.020641053095459938, -0.0688944086432457, -0.041842058300971985, -0.01696554385125637, 2.3340602054602917e-34, 0.006721317768096924, -0.0494280643761158, 0.0006344809080474079, -0.10993312299251556, -0.005584829021245241, 0.0026908633299171925, 0.030851082876324654, 0.0005411583697423339, 0.009489679709076881, -0.02321789599955082, -0.037275638431310654], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "2cd5f1940f47dacd8ef21e2aceffb80027a696c66209379e11864fc56c208bb0", "content": "This chunk is part of the section discussing the importance of comprehensive whole-school behaviour strategies in improving school culture and outcomes. It highlights the potential benefits of such strategies on attendance and wellbeing, and references Appendix II, which outlines a high-level implementation logic model for successfully enacting behaviour policy changes in schools.Whole-\nschool approaches can promote inclusion, improve school culture, engender positive role models, and clarify organisational principles, ultimately improving the school for staff as well as pupils; thus a comprehensive behaviour strategy might, in time, expect to see an impact on wide-ranging measures such as pupil and staff attendance and wellbeing. 43\nAppendix II (Implementation logic model) demonstrates the high-level implementation logic model a school might construct when implementing changes to their behaviour policy. In this logic model, you can see a process spanning an academic year involving the whole school to ensure school staff and pupils take ownership of the new policy. This is a complex change, but even relatively simple changes such as a change to mobile phone policy are likely to benefit from careful implementation, wide consultation, and room for adaptation through iterative feedback. Any change to a behaviour policy in your school is, by its nature, calling for behavioural change, whether in a targeted group of pupils, all pupils, staff, or a combination of these.\n", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Improving_behaviour_in_schools_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/18VgDE6jXP5eN_4w_IPbsUVu8tW-V4ep2", "source_id": "7a1d3f44ce494b71f1d9e0fe5b7517c578d4c867369077d76a9ccd359cf1603d", "page_number": 37, "split_id": 89, "split_idx_start": 74812, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "92162664b2d95393842c4213ec8f044f65c684b628420a1243907dcb32f605ca", "range": [660, 1018]}, {"doc_id": "140bf63e07c64e0b08eed8f1d7232e612529b4601eabbab0ba731afc60dc6157", "range": [0, 184]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.0667668804526329, -0.06946931779384613, -0.0005964543088339269, -0.02367277815937996, -0.044908393174409866, 0.02472160942852497, 0.019061541184782982, -0.00531012425199151, -0.005020329728722572, -0.004737474489957094, 0.09001362323760986, 0.011241219006478786, 0.004770365543663502, 0.014894966967403889, 0.0219207052141428, -0.03992004692554474, 0.014705647714436054, 0.007175031583756208, -0.05325515568256378, 0.019843524321