104257106781006, 0.012810667045414448, -0.03129643201828003, -0.006464781239628792, 0.005220983177423477, 0.06949269026517868, -0.02020943909883499, 0.012537024915218353, -0.023457590490579605, 0.025933796539902687, 0.02206842601299286, -0.05902930349111557, -0.021363969892263412, -0.01546810194849968, 2.0439757503261985e-34, 0.0013568124268203974, -0.018735984340310097, 0.008951154537498951, -0.07354334741830826, -0.0022746247705072165, -0.016246844083070755, 0.04879120737314224, -0.005064030643552542, 0.008125797845423222, -0.03016885556280613, -0.02020801045000553], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "140bf63e07c64e0b08eed8f1d7232e612529b4601eabbab0ba731afc60dc6157", "content": "This chunk is located in the section discussing the implementation of school behavior policies, emphasizing the complexity of enacting changes that require collective behavioral shifts among students and staff. It highlights the need for strategic planning and consistency in applying new approaches, referencing a logic model in Appendix II to support effective implementation and evaluation.Any change to a behaviour policy in your school is, by its nature, calling for behavioural change, whether in a targeted group of pupils, all pupils, staff, or a combination of these.\nWhether implementing a new rewards and sanctions system or moving towards using a redistributive justice approach to respond to behaviour incidents, teacher and pupil behaviours become ingrained and can be difficult to change, so most whole-school behaviour policy or practice change will likely take more than a school term to demonstrate impact. The logic model demonstrated in Appendix II would provide a discussion framework for senior leaders. It would be supplemented with individual plans and even logic models for components of this complex whole-school change.\n\u201cEven relatively simple changes are likely to benefit from careful implementation, wide consultation and iterative feedback.\u201d\f36\nEducation Endowment Foundation An ethos and environment that promotes respect and values adversity\nCurriculum teaching and learning to promote resilience and support social and emotional learning\nLeadership and management\nthat supports and champions efforts to promote emotional health and wellbeing\nEnabling student voice to influence decisions\nStaff development to support their own wellbeing and that of students\nIdentifying need and monitoring impact of interventions\nWorking with parents/carers\nTargeted support\nand appropriate referral\nFigure 8: Elements of a whole-school approach for wellbeing interventions (PHE 2015) 44\nEnhance the effects of universal, whole-school approaches by considering how these interact with targeted approaches you employ.\n", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Improving_behaviour_in_schools_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/18VgDE6jXP5eN_4w_IPbsUVu8tW-V4ep2", "source_id": "7a1d3f44ce494b71f1d9e0fe5b7517c578d4c867369077d76a9ccd359cf1603d", "page_number": 37, "split_id": 90, "split_idx_start": 75734, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "2cd5f1940f47dacd8ef21e2aceffb80027a696c66209379e11864fc56c208bb0", "range": [922, 1106]}, {"doc_id": "206f3e8115420aaee0a9f94460dc1b7bf7994ebb5e3af4d783603570ac105e57", "range": [0, 888]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.09103947877883911, -0.050969790667295456, 0.005839237943291664, -0.0024740954395383596, -0.025040924549102783, 0.036032214760780334, 0.010784853249788284, -0.002622016007080674, 0.010316023603081703, -0.008033107966184616, 0.08194273710250854, 0.013988380320370197, -0.005834081210196018, 0.008418663404881954, 0.02430805377662182, -0.08992870897054672, 0.016245292499661446, 0.026141056790947914, -0.026241878047585487, 0.03003538027405739, 0.006296511739492416, 0.006118606775999069, -0.00921161100268364, 0.010580555535852909, -0.003708413103595376, -0.009926273487508297, -0.018726294860243797, 0.046757157891988754, 0.007800265680998564, -0.050803426653146744, 0.073