5, 0.0270853228867054, -0.05977893993258476, 0.01663467288017273, 0.0171956904232502, -0.047968026250600815, -0.008936035446822643, -0.00271605490706861, 1.4646488567634108e-34, -0.004423021804541349, 0.024466771632432938, 0.01955926977097988, -0.042434338480234146, 0.014084325172007084, -0.0329248420894146, 0.04557911679148674, -0.030727524310350418, 0.018563320860266685, -0.02950664795935154, -0.00597292510792613], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "af51fc67447b74ce2f0e1fe5a8602e55178db353c5f859f5612f2bfa946474a3", "content": "This chunk is part of the discussion on mobile phone usage in schools, highlighting research findings that suggest a mobile phone ban can lead to improved academic performance, particularly among lower-attaining students. It emphasizes the importance of proper implementation and caution in interpreting the results within the guidance on improving behavior in schools.They found that pupils performed better in high-stakes exams following a mobile phone ban within a school, with improvements particularly seen from the lowest-attaining pupils, as long as pupils were compliant with the ban. The results suggest restricting mobile phone use could improve attainment results. However, they should be interpreted with some caution. Firstly, the data is correlational and doesn\u2019t show a mobile phone ban caused the change in attainment. Secondly, high compliance with a mobile phone ban implies a well-implemented policy. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Improving_behaviour_in_schools_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/18VgDE6jXP5eN_4w_IPbsUVu8tW-V4ep2", "source_id": "7a1d3f44ce494b71f1d9e0fe5b7517c578d4c867369077d76a9ccd359cf1603d", "page_number": 40, "split_id": 95, "split_idx_start": 79675, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "76e54686780c628d668fc068bda5dc3478c2f3c78cf8d3e6ec93b57bd9a4a965", "range": [466, 690]}, {"doc_id": "4c8c0d254888f57e9830f77540b35f7b4b67ad00c52c17d047dc036234ac7df6", "range": [0, 85]