lated policy changes. It emphasizes the importance of understanding the context of exclusions, particularly for disadvantaged pupils, and includes works addressing student wellbeing and mobile phone policies in schools.The Campbell systematic review (2018) on exclusions exposes the differential rates at which children and young people aged 4-18 from different backgrounds are excluded from schools and reviews evidence for various interventions schools may use to reduce their exclusion rates. The more recent Timpson review focuses in on practice in schools in England, the reasons behind it and policy changes that could improve conditions for pupils at risk of exclusion, including a new government commitment to address poor behaviour in schools.\nLavis & Robson (2015), \u2018Promoting children and young people\u2019s emotional health and wellbeing: A whole school and college approach\u2019, Public Health England https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/government/uploads/system/uploads/attachment_data/file/414908/Final_EHWB_\ndraft_20_03_15.pdf Public Health England\u2019s publication incorporates the model above and guidance for school leaders.\nCat Scutt (2019), \u2018Banning Mobile Phones in Schools: Reflecting on the Debate\u2019 https://impact.chartered.college/article/banning-mobile-phones-schools-reflecting-debate/ Cat Scutt considers the evidence on mobile phone usage in a blog for the Chartered College of Teaching. This may provide a useful springboard for discussion for school stakeholders contemplating a change in policy.\n", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Improving_behaviour_in_schools_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/18VgDE6jXP5eN_4w_IPbsUVu8tW-V4ep2", "source_id": "7a1d3f44ce494b71f1d9e0fe5b7517c578d4c867369077d76a9ccd359cf1603d", "page_number": 41, "split_id": 98, "split_idx_start": 81833, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "eb10a44af5895180850cd32344ee029f81e290561e83a821f098f902ea80879b", "range": [890, 1167]