0.005181097891181707, 0.007274653762578964, 0.002847850089892745, 0.0023368732072412968, 0.0212705098092556, -0.035122666507959366], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "02c995e58e70e2f0fdd12eb66075a7774762611ac5c33aa019ef36b6b151ae7e", "content": "This chunk is situated in the introductory sections of the document, specifically following the advisory panel's acknowledgment and thanks to contributors involved in the creation of the guidance report on improving behavior in schools. It emphasizes collaboration with researchers and practitioners, underscoring the collective effort in shaping effective behavior strategies.The advisory panel included John d\u2019Abbro OBE (New Rush Hall Group), Jane Bateman (Underwood West Academy), Prof Neil Humphrey (University of Manchester), Dr Alex Sutherland (RAND) and Jenny Thompson (Dixons Trinity Academy).\nWe would like to thank the many researchers and practitioners who provided support and feedback at the scoping stage and on drafts of this guidance. These include Tom Bennett (researchED), Phil Brown (St Mary\u2019s Catholic Academy), Simon Cox (Blackpool Research School), Dr Louise Denne (Warwick University), Lisa Finnegan (Ash Grove Academy), Prof Philip Garner (University of Northampton), Terry Haydn (University of East Anglia), Lisa Hesmondhalgh (Peover Superior Endowed Primary School), Anita Kerwin-Nye (Every Child Should), Terry Leighton (Dixons Manningham Academy), Neil Miley (Dixons Kings Academy), Pauline Myers (Department for Education), Shirley Watson (Dixons City Academy), Rob Webster (UCL Institute of Education), Steve Wheeldon (Wilbraham Primary School).\fAPPENDIX I: CHARLIE TAYLOR\u2019S CHECKLISTS\nFor senior leadership team:\nPolicy:Ensure absolute clarity about the expected standard of pupils\u2019 behaviour.\nEnsure that behaviour policy is clearly understood by all staff, parents, and pupils.Display school rules clearly in classes and around the building; staff and pupils should know what they are.\n", "data