89937502145767, 1.7710671732441643e-34, -0.003286996390670538, -0.05526136979460716, 0.007899243384599686, -0.0725119337439537, 0.011041614226996899, -0.016841763630509377, 0.020907489582896233, 0.008113945834338665, 0.02316880412399769, 0.0008470896282233298, -0.040316175669431686], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "da32ea49ddc243f57900468fab4228a00a8b79541c2cfbe7fb7ba436fc0e081e", "content": "This chunk is found in the section discussing \"Active ingredients\" within the framework for implementing effective behavior policies in schools. It emphasizes the importance of support from subject and pastoral leads in ensuring students understand behavior expectations and the role of senior leaders in monitoring and communicating behavior-related information.\u2022 Subject and pastoral leads effectively support behaviour across departments.\nActive ingredient four\nSupported students:\n\u2022 Students are clear about the expectations regarding behaviour. They know the consequences if they do not meet these.\n\u2022 Senior leaders, class teachers and pastoral team have precise and accurate information about pupils\u2019 behaviour, based on survey data and information from other stakeholders e.g. school nurse.\n", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Improving_behaviour_in_schools_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/18VgDE6jXP5eN_4w_IPbsUVu8tW-V4ep2", "source_id": "7a1d3f44ce494b71f1d9e0fe5b7517c578d4c867369077d76a9ccd359cf1603d", "page_number": 49, "split_id": 145, "split_idx_start": 103036, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "c46b5b715ae33513a9ea1edb8621e859d1d4f7d9c0a22919b45336b994715938", "range": [495, 574]}, {"doc_id": "9e7620e64e568fa853dcf0ae9a38be962ba7c6eb4ce9fc8fcaebd9d7b382dc07", "range": [0, 14]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.07416656613349915, -0.06289022415876389, -0.0007285707979463041, -0.018724950030446053, -0.015472589991986752, 0.06645800173282623, -0.004502067342400551, -0.0246210228651762, -0.003927014302462339,