-0.017177946865558624, -0.003085116157308221, 0.006522289011627436, -0.055572234094142914, -0.03303107991814613, 0.013106537982821465, 2.3531978045046555e-34, -0.007643654476851225, -0.0189205352216959, 5.819587750011124e-05, -0.08129188418388367, 0.01518260594457388, -0.009775587357580662, -0.047355253249406815, 0.019322477281093597, 0.03901875391602516, 0.02632872387766838, 0.02036461792886257], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "70e845a8f9ce506d05c2a14f3aada6ad7ebffeec3af2bc271452a4f569de6876", "content": "This chunk is located in the Foreword section of the document and discusses the importance of social and emotional skills for children's development and learning outcomes. It highlights the challenges schools face in prioritizing these skills amid pressure to improve academic attainment and notes the lack of support for teachers in integrating these skills into their teaching practices.With the right support, children learn to articulate and manage their emotions, deal with conflict, solve problems, understand things from another person\u2019s perspective, and communicate in appropriate ways. These \u2018social and emotional skills\u2019 are essential for children\u2019s development, support effective learning, and are linked to positive outcomes in later life.\nHowever, many schools feel that there\u2019s little time for developing such skills, given the pressure to improve attainment. Although all schools are expected to deliver Personal, Social, and Health Education (PSHE), it has not been a statutory requirement in the primary phase and in practice is often squeezed out. Few teachers receive support on how they can develop social and emotional skills in their mainstream teaching. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Social_and_Emotional_Learning.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ux2ZVY-H62jpu0xzPvuWIaW6mMGtPxRN", "source_id": "e8d637c88ad0e99d399f844be26e40bf15211fc84f01bf87ff947205064caf09", "page_number": 4, "split_i