\"Introduction\" section of the document, immediately following an explanation of the guidance's target audience. It outlines the range of stakeholders who may benefit from the recommendations and emphasizes the importance of acting on the guidance to enhance social and emotional development in primary schools.Further audiences who may find the guidance relevant include other staff within schools who are responsible for children\u2019s social and emotional development (for example, PSHE coordinators or inclusion leads), local authorities, multi-academy trusts, governors, parents, programme developers, and educational researchers.\nActing on the guidance\nThe recommendations in this guidance report provide a starting point for school leaders to critically review how they support children\u2019s social and emotional development. This could include auditing their current approach to PSHE or Relationships and Health education and how it links to classroom teaching, their behaviour management or anti-bullying policies, or their training and support for staff. If schools have bought a SEL programme, it might prompt them to consider if it is as promising as hoped, and how it might be implemented more effectively\u2014 or replaced.\nAdditional resources to support the implementation of the recommendations made in this report will be developed. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Social_and_Emotional_Learning.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ux2ZVY-H62jpu0xzPvuWIaW6mMGtPxRN", "source_id": "e8d637c88ad0e99d399f844be26e40bf15211fc84f01bf87ff947205064caf09", "page_number": 5, "split_id": 10, "split_idx_start": 7578, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "a261fb5b69351155b2fdc392e58b069385228fd8e46d3d69cef77b70461ede30", "range": [393, 714]}, {"doc_id": "70ae383203ea5dbb79538e12b80f7137588e68fc41e36c6f88da7e20c0aef9a2", "range": [0, 113]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.06433429569005966, -0.08337775617837906, 0.01094367541372776, -0.0051718172