563957214, 0.007689279038459063, -0.013800839893519878, 2.3774168911401813e-34, 0.005181258078664541, -0.03241896629333496, 0.007364047225564718, -0.06599686294794083, 0.01103079691529274, -0.01606915146112442, -0.014956186525523663, 0.03142596036195755, 0.025740303099155426, 0.06723760068416595, -0.03539025038480759], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "333ef56522982e5cb7e1340da91e667726e0f70579b6e873a41dd325450d065a", "content": "This chunk is from the recommendations section of the guidance report on improving social and emotional learning (SEL) in primary schools. It emphasizes the integration of SEL skills into core subject areas and the importance of using structured lesson plans and evidence-based programs for effective teaching.\u2022 Embed SEL teaching across a range of subject areas: literacy, history, drama and PE all provide good opportunities to link to SEL.\n\u2022 Use simple ground-rules in groupwork and classroom discussion to reinforce SEL skills. Page 18\nIntegrate and model SEL skills through everyday teaching\n2\n\u2022 Use a planned series of lessons to teach skills in dedicated time.\n\u2022 Adopting an evidence-based programme is likely to be a better bet than developing your own from scratch.\n\u2022 Explore and prepare carefully before adopting a programme\u2014\nreview what is required to deliver it, and whether it is suitable for your needs and context.\n", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Social_and_Emotional_Learning.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ux2ZVY-H62jpu0xzPvuWIaW6mMGtPxRN", "source_id": "e8d637c88ad0e99d399f844be26e40bf15211fc84f01bf87ff947205064caf09", "page_number": 10, "split_id": 30, "split_idx_start": 23480, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "379be27e3f41b8059c5f2b8e4be369c963206b4152a1c7137d4a1c68afb769b3", "range": [313, 446]}, {"doc_id": "b5aa475bfe07f1891119e6e3d9656808dbf3074343187e19f5fe806c8f8e1b35", "range": [0, 155]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.04671735316514969, -0.087475