664958477, 0.0513279065489769, -0.05865130200982094, 0.0227471012622118, 0.002889773575589061, 0.008210510015487671, 0.03129418194293976, -0.04275256767868996, -0.03189286217093468, 0.020615041255950928, 1.4425557618639425e-34, 0.003077944042161107, -0.02601730078458786, -0.021191833540797234, -0.07883930206298828, 0.01876726746559143, -0.013458163477480412, -0.05299638584256172, 0.00028767745243385434, -0.006199630443006754, 0.028396442532539368, -0.007853983901441097], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "7a3e4ab6904c4f0855f54940e94bba14798f01023e3978ebe49865ba505f922d", "content": "This chunk is found in the section discussing strategies for enhancing children's self-awareness as part of explicit teaching in social and emotional learning (SEL). It includes examples of activities that teachers can use to help children recognize and express emotions, emphasizing the importance of vocabulary development and practical applications through interactive methods.20 Two areas that teachers can support are children\u2019s knowledge of emotions, and ability to express emotions.\nExamples of activities used to develop children\u2019s self-awareness\nKnowledge of emotions\nTeachers can help children label and recognise emotions through explicit vocabulary teaching (\u2018putting feelings into words\u2019), and activities that give children the opportunity to practise using this language in real contexts through games, stories, and other activities. For example: \u2022 using story books to discuss how characters feel and why;\n\u2022 using games to develop children\u2019s vocabulary e.g. miming activities where children guess a feeling that is being portrayed (\u2018emotional charades\u2019); and\n\u2022 using mirrors, photographs and pictures to talk about what happens to people\u2019s faces and bodies when they are experiencing particular emotions: for example, children might match photographs displaying different emotions with emotion labels and scenario labels.\f11Improving social and emotional learning in primary schools\nEmotional expression\nChildren\u2019s ability to recognise and express emotions can be supported with a class display, which is regularly referenced (see Box 1). ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Social_and_Emotional_Learning.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ux2ZVY-H62jpu0xzPvuWIaW6mMGtPxRN", "source_id": "e8d637c88ad0e99d399f844be26e40bf15211fc84f01bf87ff947205064caf09", "page_number": 12, "split_id": 39, "split_idx_start": 28050, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "7154647507f4f51e1b7d8b4e8a6a630da241923b2a08f22e134861ff1d5306af", "range": [455, 565]}, {"doc_id": "4e42aa90694f41d9f0ffda841f80124130d7dfdeb7a7de51446580ccaaac55a6", "range": [0, 217]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.038010939955711365, -0.0849524736404419, -0.022183436900377274, 0.04607297107577324, -0.021379156038165092, 0.05910201370716095, 0.07483149319887161, -0.013526729308068752, 0.03209527209401131, -0.01710609160363674, 0.008999164216220379, -0.000731601205188781, 0.02742166444659233, -0.01656663604080677, 0.03935560584068298, -0.11548320204019547, 0.0001704319438431412, 0.057976651936769485, 0.0025130894500762224, 0.01778046227991581, -0.008075063116848469, 0.018145104870200157, -0.03853234276175499, 0.01473813597112894, -0.042459238320589066, -0.039688121527433395, -0.016987070441246033, -0.0021291521843522787, -0.014617947861552238, -0.04399121180176735, 0.03138234093785286, 0.003072805004194379, 0.014788123778998852, -0.09370075911283493, 1.9340541257406585e-06, -0.029702508822083473, 0.010215128771960735, -0.035923030227422714, 0.023439273238182068, 0.015657074749469757, 0.0862601175904274, 0.02321673557162285, 0.05176401883363724, 0.00826927088201046, 0.004878829699009657, -0.00861347746104002, 0.057198576629161835, 0.06483140587806702, 0.03518160060048103, 0.0010888796532526612, 0.008368298411369324, 0.02881496399641037, -0.050845686346292496, -0.030796969309449196, 0.09407840669155121, 0.04602440