2532005, 0.07330260425806046, 0.03514230251312256, -0.0611959807574749, 0.029463564977049828, 0.025042258203029633, -0.0031956518068909645, 0.02877240814268589, 0.08485855907201767, -0.03699018061161041, 0.03032955527305603, 0.006957684177905321, 0.0039912620559334755, -0.041123293340206146, 0.017905347049236298, 0.009600980207324028, 0.02864878997206688, 0.026539700105786324, 0.031831614673137665, 0.03487419709563255, 0.039285995066165924, 0.02484261989593506, 0.031069733202457428, 0.02050490491092205, -0.0033960205037146807, -0.022987019270658493, -0.002674743067473173, -0.05708593130111694, 0.01827487349510193, -0.016654111444950104, -0.01925036683678627, -0.0018394782673567533, 0.0183143001049757, 0.00038105787825770676, -0.001141697051934898, -0.015299061313271523, 0.021160390228033066, 0.03142290562391281, 0.012322086840867996, 0.06522870063781738, -0.03362556919455528, 0.03687801584601402, -0.08174055069684982, -0.01991887390613556, -0.0015578988241031766, -0.0324271060526371, 0.014246285893023014, 0.03409847989678383, -0.006222242955118418, 0.0023856426123529673, 0.01151180174201727, 0.004053635988384485, -0.055338069796562195, 0.018095627427101135, -0.008586370386183262, -0.057329561561346054, 0.0040701306425035, -0.001584806595928967, 0.03553274646401405, -0.07436589896678925, -0.03360079228878021, 0.010472244583070278, -0.0020698809530586004, 0.044359102845191956, -0.04549052566289902, -0.009518402628600597, -0.01713150553405285, 1.8898371020703327e-34, -0.0035258629359304905, -0.024876097217202187, -0.007848942652344704, -0.07018771767616272, 0.010453845374286175, -0.0021154158748686314, 0.019104545935988426, 0.017251867800951004, 0.03352770954370499, 0.006888208910822868, -0.013140003196895123], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "4e42aa90694f41d9f0ffda841f80124130d7dfdeb7a7de51446580ccaaac55a6", "content": "This chunk is located in the section discussing strategies for enhancing children's self-management and emotional expression within the broader context of teaching social and emotional learning (SEL) skills in primary schools. It emphasizes the importance of helping children recognize and articulate their emotions through specific techniques and prompts in the classroom.11Improving social and emotional learning in primary schools\nEmotional expression\nChildren\u2019s ability to recognise and express emotions can be supported with a class display, which is regularly referenced (see Box 1). Teachers can also develop children\u2019s ability to tell others how they are feeling by, for example:\n\u2022 teaching them to use \u2018I\u2019 messages (articulating how you feel and why): \u2018I feel x because\u2026\u2019\n\u2022 providing supportive prompts to children who have difficulty talking about their emotions, such as: \u2018It looks like you might be feeling sad, can you tell me what happened?\u2019. The simple act of naming the emotion can help children understand it more clearly.\n\u2022 explaining to children that all feelings are okay, but the behaviours they lead to may not be okay. It is okay to feel angry, for example, but not okay to act in ways that hurt others.\n", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Social_and_Emotional_Learning.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ux2ZVY-H62jpu0xzPvuWIaW6mMGtPxRN", "source_id": "e8d637c88ad0e99d399f844be26e40bf15211fc84f01bf87ff947205064caf09", "page_number": 13, "split_id": 40, "split_idx_start": 29007, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "7a3e4ab6904c4f0855f54940e94bba14798f01023e3978ebe49865ba505f922d", "range": [957, 1174]}, {"doc_id": "63b82c8888c85cfff0aadcbaec2210e3c12542708ae481cc493b5f842203de8b", "range": [0, 85]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.06113552674651146, -0.09279133379459381, -0.008314676582813263, 0.005692934617400169, -0.01080209668725729, 0.059934522956609726, 0.08728627860546112, -0.028369834646582603, 0.043960195034742355, -0.0012007916811853647, 0.012554120272397995, 0.0004185317375231534, 0.02676095813512802, 