06, 0.04390671104192734, -0.036674149334430695, 0.037148136645555496, 0.019708814099431038, -0.03059706650674343, 0.022016149014234543, 0.07373101264238358, -0.01390998438000679, 0.005277339369058609, 5.29561257280875e-05, 0.007671582978218794, -0.021039152517914772, -0.0026168846525251865, 0.011859117075800896, 0.024204809218645096, 0.008191311731934547, 0.047289155423641205, 0.03487326577305794, 0.07180685549974442, 0.014565580524504185, 0.04066235572099686, 0.011728726327419281, 0.005455317907035351, 0.0048270458355546, 0.003690812736749649, -0.07994332909584045, 0.03441446274518967, -0.038454219698905945, 0.004844977054744959, -0.045819368213415146, 0.03730505332350731, -0.04053870588541031, 0.024616094306111336, -0.02941899187862873, -0.0032500578090548515, 0.029695002362132072, 0.020088117569684982, 0.07967229932546616, -0.022573748603463173, 0.0428706631064415, -0.07245717197656631, 0.01748058572411537, -0.00748203881084919, -0.0662376806139946, 0.020269671455025673, 0.0387648306787014, 0.005871690809726715, -0.006806541234254837, 0.029363760724663734, -0.0008844182593747973, -0.08307969570159912, 0.025704529136419296, 0.00805562175810337, -0.06113307178020477, -0.005057358182966709, 0.0010125460103154182, 0.038603559136390686, -0.04664519429206848, -0.017158836126327515, 0.025629810988903046, -0.011548224836587906, 0.07825328409671783, -0.04807843267917633, 0.018819093704223633, -0.024331100285053253, 1.995398675867208e-34, 0.003988201729953289, -0.022541198879480362, -0.010506301186978817, -0.05285206064581871, 0.003541405778378248, 0.004103188868612051, 0.04546552896499634, 0.0038714390248060226, 0.024592624977231026, 0.02245386876165867, -0.025461822748184204], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "4d214bd0695cdf2bee2e44467682ef4937eaaa0e40e88d52835f49847075521e", "content": "This chunk is situated within a section discussing strategies to enhance children's self-management and emotional self-regulation in the guidance report on improving social and emotional learning in primary schools. It specifically references the use of a feelings display to help children identify and express their emotions effectively.Can you think of a time you might feel frustrated?\u2019\nTeachers can also use the display to support emotional expression, for example, by having children place their name or photo on a relevant emotion within the feelings display to indicate how they are feeling when they come into the classroom in the morning, or at the start of the afternoon. 21 Teachers may also make reference to the display in describing their own emotions: \u2018This is beginning to make me frustrated, because you\u2019re talking whilst I am trying to explain something important to you.\u2019\nEnergetic\nBouncy\nEcstatic\nNervous\nWarm-hearted\nKind\nSympathetic\nLoving\nFurious\nFrustrated\nUpset\nIrritated\nMopey\nBlue\nDepressedDown\nPleased\nSatisfiedContentFulfilled\nJittery\nAnxiousTerrified\nTense\nExcited\nTender\nScared\nAngry\nSad\nHappy\nBox 1: Feelings display\nFeelings wheel: to support explicit teaching and practice of new vocabulary\f12\nEducation Endowment Foundation B. Strategies to enhance children\u2019s self-management\nWhat do we mean by self-management and emotional self-regulation?\nThe terms \u2018self-management\u2019 and \u2018self-regulation\u2019 refer to the ability to understand and regulate our emotions, thoughts, and behaviours in different situations. It includes being able to:\n\u2022 regulate or manage reactions to emotions like frustration, anxiety, or excitement;\n\u2022 calm down after something exciting or upsetting;\n\u2022 focus on a task; and \u2022 control impulses.\n", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Social_and_Emotional_Learning.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ux2ZVY-H62jpu0xzPvuWIaW6mMGtPxRN", "source_id": "e8d637c88ad0e99d399f844be26e40bf15211fc84f01bf87ff947205064caf09", "page_number": 13, "split_id": 42, "split_idx_start": 30330, "_split_overlap": [{"