 -0.023556148633360863, 0.07482299953699112, 0.002330315299332142, -0.004236327484250069, 0.035567961633205414, -0.028071172535419464], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "7bcb54726987022874e16c5085bb0444f3b30393a1b14520902630494e921a78", "content": "This chunk is situated within the section discussing strategies to enhance children's self-management and emotional self-regulation in the guidance report on improving social and emotional learning in primary schools. It describes the \"Turtle Technique,\" a method for helping children calm themselves during moments of anger through visualization and breathing exercises.The aim is to help children calm themselves when they feel angry. It is based on empirical observations of the relationship among physiological measures, behaviour, and children\u2019s emotional states. 23 It involves the teacher explaining to children how a turtle can retreat into its shell, and suggest they can do this too when they are angry (perhaps putting their head down, or holding themselves). In their shell, they can take three deep breaths and think calming thoughts, such as: \u2018I can calm down.\u2019 They come out of their shell when calm and ready to think of solutions to the problem. An adaptation for older children (Key Stage 2) has been called \u2018Fingers Linked, Time to Think\u2019.\n", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Social_and_Emotional_Learning.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ux2ZVY-H62jpu0xzPvuWIaW6mMGtPxRN", "source_id": "e8d637c88ad0e99d399f844be26e40bf15211fc84f01bf87ff947205064caf09", "page_number": 14, "split_id": 44, "split_idx_start": 32310, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "4285c6988bf4c09e276a469ba28b978b1c586b7ec28150fe0c62e519c0b1e8ac", "range": [779, 845]}, {"doc_id": "f26f4e922ab65062475ca06d251477ef2953843f8d4da15b14454dd049c4bc31", "range": [0, 96]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.021522564813494682, -0.07325693964958191, -0.018318844959139824, -0.004329799208790064, -0.0093