93], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "e12ace194b3a0fe35c78e2ad8100c076ed91d54397ef7f7cbcda92b8b8b39180", "content": "This chunk is part of the section on self-management and emotional self-regulation within the guidance report on improving social and emotional learning (SEL) in primary schools. It discusses strategies for helping children recognize physiological signs of strong emotions and provides teaching methods, such as using images and metaphors, to enhance their understanding and response to emotions.\u2022 Recognise body cues. Help children to become aware of early physiological signs of strong emotions and encourage them to talk about how they are feeling. Examples of physiological signs include: heart beating, face getting hot, sweaty palms, knees or hands shaking, tone of voice, facial expression, and rapid breathing. \u2022 Use images and metaphors to help children\u2019s understanding (see Box 2). For example, children might watch a balloon blown up until it bursts, or learn about volcanic eruptions, then discuss how they might notice angry feelings building up inside themselves, and make a class poster of steps they can take to avoid an \u2018anger explosion\u2019.\f", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Social_and_Emotional_Learning.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ux2ZVY-H62jpu0xzPvuWIaW6mMGtPxRN", "source_id": "e8d637c88ad0e99d399f844be26e40bf15211fc84f01bf87ff947205064caf09", "page_number": 14, "split_id": 47, "split_idx_start": 33858, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "22f04e5030370282a5b48911d6d5a114eaa36803848c4b10346373d5805f8d51", "range": [422, 445]}, {"doc_id": "7cc5e37d9da7933092eebd31722cc1c16dba8935fe1cf56010c7b06810d52f61", "range": [0, 264]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.0032345233485102654, -0.0648767277598381, -0.011838052421808243, 0.011787144467234612, 0.05400978401303291, 0.03582920879125595, -0.013644865714013577, -0.014798101037740707, 0.08004786819219589, -0.00382958329282701, -0.004890519