0599, -0.02516118623316288, 0.02411823533475399, 0.006685239262878895, -0.05977541580796242, 0.02941506914794445, 0.01662105694413185, 0.02044367976486683, -0.054327916353940964, -0.002363440115004778, -0.03484327346086502, 0.012374423444271088, 0.010567991062998772, -0.0446740947663784, 0.0044519929215312, -0.02455347403883934, 2.1373299760019025e-34, -0.013682913035154343, -0.02304457686841488, -0.0036467299796640873, -0.06404101848602295, 0.050208378583192825, -0.006120022386312485, -0.02416377328336239, 0.016052061691880226, 0.0042597269639372826, 0.02917601726949215, -0.029042934998869896], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "bf19f1e1fbd8606d9d8a67e1fc3edfbb6006c4f830748c6a681e07423463677a", "content": "This chunk is part of the section discussing activities and strategies to enhance children's social awareness within the context of social and emotional learning (SEL). Specifically, it provides examples of how to use discussions and role-playing activities, such as the use of 'hot seating' and circle time, to help children understand emotions and perspectives, thereby promoting empathy.To draw connections with other subjects, in a follow-up history lesson, they could consider what it means to be an ally to another nation, and how this is different or similar to being an ally to someone in school. This example is drawn from the Second Step programme. 28\nBox 3: Discuss what it means to be an ally\nExamples of activities and strategies used to develop children\u2019s social awareness\nIdentifying another person\u2019s emotions and perspective\n\u2022 Use \u2018hot seating\u2019, in which one child plays a character and the rest of the class asks them questions about their feelings and how those feelings influence the choices they make.\n\u2022 Circle time. Use everyday classroom scenarios during circle time to explore how awareness of the feelings of others can help to develop children\u2019s empathy skills e.g. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_p