004375521093606949, 0.0237873625010252, -0.022700175642967224, -0.026911117136478424, -0.0043478296138346195, -0.024489693343639374, 1.9090874291862305e-34, 0.016564389690756798, -0.025947006419301033, 0.007895435206592083, -0.06772483885288239, 0.009853004477918148, -0.019171999767422676, 0.0075987051241099834, -0.036827437579631805, 0.009213914163410664, 0.0036001757252961397, -0.02013791725039482], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "2e340cdfb502376d7582762a13bf58b51ade13da106e492ed6969e5d8d579330", "content": "This chunk is from the section discussing strategies to enhance children's relationship skills within the context of social and emotional learning (SEL). It emphasizes the importance of self-reflective questioning as a metacognitive technique that aids in evaluating actions and promoting self-reflection, which are crucial for effective interpersonal interactions and emotional regulation.\u2022 Encourage self-reflective questioning\u2014such as, \u2018What would I have done in that situation?\u2019 This is a metacognitive technique that allows children to evaluate actions, promotes self-reflection, and develops their use of self-talk. This is commonly taught in evidence-based SEL programmes. 27\f14\nEducation Endowment Foundation D. Strategies to enhance children\u2019s relationship skills\nWhat do we mean by relationship skills?\nRelationship skills are concerned with our ability to interact positively with peers and adults, and to effectively navigate social situations. 29 It is important that children learn to recognise, express and regulate their emotions before they can be expected to interact successfully with others. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Social_and_Emotional_Learning.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ux2ZVY-H62jpu0xzPvuWIaW6mMGtPxRN", "source_id": "e8d637c88ad0e99d399f844be26e40bf15211fc84f01bf87ff947205064caf09", "page_number": 15, "split_id": 52, "split_idx_start": 36915, "_split_overlap": [{"do