405746, -0.020184693858027458, 0.0011695405701175332, 0.007030712906271219, 0.05598744377493858, 0.011914827860891819, 0.0013737375847995281, -0.0032655822578817606, -0.021894635632634163, -0.012511005625128746, 0.030031440779566765, 0.052007559686899185, -0.02924182638525963, -0.031214887276291847, -0.015476814471185207, 0.012951061129570007, -0.003840346122160554, -0.0635569840669632, -0.03419433906674385, -0.04416132718324661, 2.6089314218895924e-34, -0.010064474307000637, 0.010764297097921371, 0.011659360490739346, -0.0651966854929924, 0.009635337628424168, -0.02017306350171566, 0.03927314653992653, 0.050287529826164246, 0.04589406028389931, 0.05462462827563286, -0.0567016564309597], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "9c0082d16c09449e20ad092e77eb00d4e637a50c98fc8e52828803bc50d21ba8", "content": "This chunk appears in the section discussing Recommendation 2, which emphasizes integrating and modeling social and emotional learning (SEL) skills through everyday teaching. It highlights the importance of teachers modeling appropriate behaviors and thought processes, demonstrating effective decision-making and interactions to enhance students' SEL development in the classroom.38 Modelling\u2014or demonstrating appropriate behaviours\u2014teaches and enhances children\u2019s social and emotional skills. 39 How teachers and other adults in school speak to each other, to parents and to children\u2014demonstrating attentive listening, for example, or how to speak to someone who is upset\u2014\ncan model a respectful relationship. The EEF\u2019s guidance report on Metacognition and Self-Regulated Learning provides examples of how teachers can use modelling to reveal the thinking processes of an expert learner. Similarly, teachers can model their own thinking and feelings explicitly as they approach a task or respond to a situation to reveal the processes that support effective decision-\nmaking and interaction. For example, when faced with a problem\u2014classroom IT that is needed for an activity but isn\u2019t functioning correctly\u2014the teacher might model aloud the immediate response of frustration, and demonstrate the problem-solving approach, staying calm, taking a deep breathe, thinking through the options aloud, and coming up with a solution for a new activity.\n", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Social_and_Emotional_Learning.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ux2ZVY-H62jpu0xzPvuWIaW6mMGtPxRN", "source_id": "e8d637c88ad0e99d399f844be26e40bf15211fc84f01bf87ff947205064caf09", "page_number": 20, "split_id": 68, "split_idx_start": 45997, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "87cbb75db71a1ee43cabe80f8bc5996bdf867ffd42f792bc2b8d2512f93f96bf", "range": [965, 1079]}, {"doc_id": "1195220341b645aa95461f20f136cdb206118851b31b07d50ef31e5728af782f", "range": [0, 353]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.07045884430408478, -0.10416961461305618, -0.028773900121450424, 0.011465078219771385, -0.011788113974034786, 0.05925878509879112, 0.05626639351248741, -0.030310072004795074, -0.010687600821256638, -0.00957498699426651, 0.01110792625695467, 0.032285917550325394, 0.010548297315835953, 0.003659909823909402, 0.040003951638936996, -0.09314918518066406, 0.014328055083751678, 0.007890352047979832, -0.008815030567348003, 0.015097053721547127, 0.0007787290960550308, 0.006998496130108833, -0.03567826747894287, 0.037974365055561066, -0.015097418799996376, -0.03744801878929138, -0.013171037659049034, 0.00897833239287138, -0.02788376435637474, -0.01530348602682352, 0.03670351207256317, 0.0332530215382576, -0.001823086873628199, -0.08922582119703293, 2.2953622647037264e-06, -0.004878595005720854, -0.02679239585995674, -0.02834441512823105, -0.006930894684046507, -0.010527780279517174, 0.06451354920864105, 0.036822643131017685, 0.030045682564377785, 0.028770916163921356, -0.01646525412797928, 0.03163573518395424, 0.014627907425165176, 0.064732126891613, -0.0010336306877434254, -0.012527701444923878, 0.009732912294566631, 