43620482087135315, 0.11602503061294556, 0.020844407379627228, 0.03867851570248604, -0.002135958056896925, 6.590349948965013e-05, -0.01625506393611431, 0.04014890268445015, -0.06341590732336044, -0.034979261457920074, -0.05912487953901291, 0.04843675717711449, 0.0012845415621995926, 0.014583025127649307, 0.007777619641274214, 0.027174774557352066, 0.026401028037071228, 0.002479723421856761, 0.0016129040159285069, -0.010312603786587715, -0.0445212684571743, 0.00219789519906044, 0.0007508131675422192, -0.0026054151821881533, -0.017169633880257607, -0.0022553452290594578, 0.0024281078949570656, 0.013895709067583084, -0.001269734581001103, -0.03286239504814148, 0.0833163633942604, 0.025822404772043228, 0.07197056710720062, -0.019420567899942398, 0.021501272916793823, -0.0559634231030941, -0.039975639432668686, 0.006498159375041723, -0.08408138900995255, 0.008862035349011421, 0.030883897095918655, -0.010688865557312965, -0.0015793241327628493, -0.007945629768073559, 0.0321047380566597, -0.005466633941978216, 0.02061912603676319, 0.00961584597826004, -0.05549716204404831, 0.016396084800362587, 0.01000002771615982, 0.03860221803188324, -0.008777517825365067, -0.004797349683940411, 0.0007270810310728848, 0.022479034960269928, 0.007269329857081175, -0.05818859860301018, -0.005924418568611145, -0.019772613421082497, 1.9298060910913243e-34, -0.00225435639731586, -0.010024108923971653, 0.01429909560829401, -0.07211960852146149, 0.020013809204101562, -0.012723304331302643, -4.414405793795595e-06, 0.02100226655602455, 0.025089126080274582, 0.07785336673259735, -0.035005442798137665], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "bbeea279a51efaeab433f020cd7536f5401f0c8d7cb60196069bb0117bbc898f", "content": "This chunk appears in the section discussing the integration and modeling of social and emotional learning (SEL) skills through everyday teaching. It emphasizes the importance of not only addressing SEL skills during crisis moments but also proactively teaching these skills in a preventative manner to enhance children's ability to navigate social situations effectively.42\nDo not rely on \u2018crisis moments\u2019 for teaching skills\nIn busy schools we often do not think explicitly about children\u2019s social and emotional skills until a problem arises (such as an incident of bullying). While such problems can provide important contexts for teaching and reinforcing skills, if they are the only time that skills are discussed, children may perceive skills to be solely about avoiding poor behaviour. Such individual incidents can also be sensitive and difficult for communicating broader lessons.\nAlthough it can be difficult to make time, taking a preventative approach\u2014discussing issues such as bullying before they arise, and providing children with strategies they can draw on \u2014is likely to make crisis moments easier to deal with when they do arise, and lead to more effective resolutions.\f20\nEducation Endowment Foundation Provide support for effective group work and classroom talk\nThe effects of SEL work can be amplified through classrooms that help children to practise their skills in their everyday interactions.44 For example, collaborative (or cooperative) learning approaches may be particularly beneficial for supporting pupils\u2019 peer interactions.45 Such approaches typically involve pupils working together on activities or learning tasks in groups small enough for everyone to participate on a collective task that has been clearly assigned. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Social_and_Emotional_Learning.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ux2ZVY-H62jpu0xzPvuWIaW6mMGtPxRN", "source_id": "e8d637c88ad0e99d399f844be26e40bf15211fc84f01bf87ff947205064caf09", "page_number": 21, "split_id": 72, "split_idx_start": 49228, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "456131ce90631ed23f3f18fb65c3641357916dc1ee23c72eda5d257c2055e374", "range": [914, 1121]}, {"doc_id": "273e32c3b6b1fa4f6c5824299bd819ea05a774d9a085aaaab2f