.02443612366914749, 0.008956331759691238, 0.009476213715970516, -0.012822232209146023, 0.007739120628684759, -0.0493461973965168, -0.0422837994992733, 0.021882938221096992, 2.206715825946592e-34, -0.0015517728170379996, -0.03436831384897232, -0.027269911020994186, -0.02272273600101471, 0.03910083323717117, 0.0012908921344205737, -0.020516512915492058, 0.015564694069325924, 0.05169244110584259, 0.03838491439819336, -0.0076992386020720005], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "c115ca206e158d43725bb5ba4fce4f16aa2ab7eca249c33e5430080c8d5b9d9f", "content": "This chunk is part of Recommendation 2, \"Integrate and model SEL skills through everyday teaching,\" which discusses the importance of using literature and storytelling in classrooms to enhance children's social and emotional learning (SEL) skills. It highlights strategies for reflecting on emotions and scenarios to facilitate discussion and understanding of others' perspectives.Children can reflect upon and share similar experiences. As an extension, children could then rewrite the story to show how they would have reacted to the problem. This example is based on a range of evidence-based programmes that use stories and scenarios to prompt reflection and discussion.\nBox 6: Identifying others\u2019 emotions and perspectives\u2014linking SEL and reading comprehension\nIn Philosophy for Children (P4C), structured group discussions are prompted by a stimulus (for example, a story or a video) and are based around a concept such as \u2018truth\u2019, \u2018fairness\u2019, or \u2018bullying\u2019. The aim of P4C is to help children become more willing and able to ask questions, construct arguments, and engage in reasoned discussion. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Social_and_Emotional_Learning.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ux2ZVY-H62jpu0xzPvuWIaW6mMGtPxRN", "source_id": "e8d637c88ad0e99d399f844be26e40bf15211fc84f01bf87ff947205064caf09", "page_number": 23, "split_id": 77, "spli