0.013047530315816402, -0.021543078124523163, 0.06003417819738388, -0.02296692319214344, -0.041620075702667236, -0.027992041781544685, 0.012950807809829712, -0.027803098782896996, -0.036420129239559174, 0.00629012193530798, 0.0009575957665219903, 0.013511663302779198, -0.02069586142897606, -0.04509393870830536, -0.015129282139241695, 0.03442491218447685, -0.010537807829678059, -0.0026467374991625547, 0.05062362551689148, -0.05229009687900543, -0.06526810675859451, 0.006033707410097122, 0.00027278473135083914, 0.00852743349969387, 0.0678698718547821, 0.008977808058261871, 0.012986740097403526, 0.0022154017351567745, 0.03780609369277954, -0.043329816311597824, 0.04637706279754639, 0.006365894805639982, -0.0631231740117073, 0.053775858134031296, -0.006353424862027168, 0.009402109310030937, -0.05884167179465294, -0.011981727555394173, 0.00730169890448451, 0.0016544017707929015, -0.01984092965722084, -0.036561645567417145, -0.03911749646067619, 0.0058717806823551655, 2.363548812865363e-34, 0.0022711288183927536, -0.005441021639853716, -0.03391735255718231, -0.019065095111727715, 0.008953811600804329, 0.018958164379000664, -0.009174929931759834, -0.005471113603562117, 0.02416064590215683, 0.05862300843000412, -0.020826099440455437], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "f984adb55a766d1d0297d5049c4a9267a606a434c930e135e2bd69c799f97cbc", "content": "This chunk is part of the discussion on implementing effective Social and Emotional Learning (SEL) programmes in primary schools. Specifically, it highlights the Philosophy for Children (P4C) approach, which, while not a traditional SEL programme, aligns with SEL goals by enhancing children's skills in reasoning, communication, and collaboration, thereby supporting their overall development.The aim of P4C is to help children become more willing and able to ask questions, construct arguments, and engage in reasoned discussion. P4C is not normally thought of as a \u2018SEL programme\u2019, however many of the discussion topics are very relevant to SEL, and teachers and pupils involved have reported that it has had a beneficial impact on outcomes such as pupils\u2019 confidence to speak, patience when listening to others, self-\nesteem, communication and teamwork. 48 There is also evidence that P4C has an impact on academic outcomes, including reading and maths.49 This is currently being tested through a large EEF evaluation in primary schools.\nBox 7: Philosophy for Children\f22\nEducation Endowment Foundation 3\nPlan carefully for adopting a SEL programme\nUse a planned series of lessons to teach skills in dedicated time\nTypically, \u2018social and emotional learning programmes\u2019 are delivered to all children in a classroom, usually by the class teachers, and consist of a series of lessons on topics such as identifying and labelling feelings, controlling impulses, and understanding the perspective of others. They are often introduced in the context of specific topics, such as: bullying, friendship, health education, and staying safe (see Box 8 for an example). ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Social_and_Emotional_Learning.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ux2ZVY-H62jpu0xzPvuWIaW6mMGtPxRN", "source_id": "e8d637c88ad0e99d399f844be26e40bf15211fc84f01bf87ff947205064caf09", "page_number": 23, "split_id": 78, "split_idx_start": 54744, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "c115ca206e158d43725bb5ba4fce4f16aa2ab7eca249c33e5430080c8d5b9d9f", "range": [584, 722]}, {"doc_id": "a3081924ef1bef1833df1d2f88cb44cef739adc10719c3c655ec599035979666", "range": [0, 155]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.04774688556790352, -0.037744250148534775, 0.01243550144135952, 0.044108908623456955, -0.007625665981322527, 0.04557614400982857, 0.032249245792627335, -0.07270485162734985, -0.011918782256543636, 0.014384609647095203, 0.009934035129845142, 0.005862068384885788, 0.017565328627824783, -0.04487721249461174, -0.004630482755601406, -0.09389163553714752, -0.012350277043879032, 0.03