81197548, 0.00972103700041771, 0.02483181282877922, -0.08359688520431519, 0.03583906963467598, 0.00014422283857129514, 0.05832740664482117, 0.015426604077219963, 0.0140231903642416, 0.006885661277920008, 0.006702869664877653, 0.03371613472700119, -0.04336169734597206, -0.024200936779379845, -0.04760663956403732, 2.3116771399817045e-34, 0.011218181811273098, -0.05158647149801254, -0.022567881271243095, -0.1008264422416687, 0.002215016633272171, -0.024591242894530296, -0.005104820244014263, -0.012182742357254028, -0.015906421467661858, 0.020598946139216423, -0.0313359834253788], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "5babdc490a19b5342267371c0fdc385ca5fb8fba7eaa90b1a342527504a7e7a6", "content": "This chunk is found in the section discussing structured Social and Emotional Learning (SEL) programs aimed at improving children's emotional and social skills. It specifically highlights the Promoting Alternative Thinking Strategies (PATHS) program, detailing its curriculum, focus areas, training for teachers, and the evidence supporting its effectiveness in primary education.Promoting Alternative Thinking Strategies (PATHS) aims to develop self-control, emotional understanding, positive self-esteem, relationships and interpersonal problem-solving among children aged 4\u201311. This is primarily delivered through a taught curriculum by the class teacher (approximately twice a week throughout the year), with lessons on topics such as identifying and labelling feelings, controlling impulses and understanding the perspectives of others. Teachers receive one and a half days\u2019 training plus ongoing support. The curriculum is supplemented by activities that help children apply the lessons throughout the day, and provides links to the home environment.\nOverall, PATHS has a strong international evidence base, including over ten RCTs. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Social_and_Emotional_Learning.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ux2ZVY-H62jp