96954823, -0.016552599146962166, -0.03347405046224594, -0.0011533143697306514, 0.007523923180997372, 0.0194605253636837, -0.010590176098048687], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "9541bf68f773d396fd372cfd71bd43e4032de2c61658088afdb0da41518d0d9b", "content": "This chunk discusses the challenges of implementing the PATHS (Promoting Alternative Thinking Strategies) programme for social and emotional learning (SEL) in UK primary schools. It highlights concerns regarding the adequacy of lesson delivery and its impact on student outcomes, emphasizing the importance of quality implementation amidst busy school schedules.Implementation was a challenge: teachers struggled to fit all the PATHS lessons into their timetables, delivering lessons at half the recommended frequency. There was evidence that higher quality delivery of the programme was associated with improved tests scores at Key Stage 2 (though this could have been due to better quality teaching overall rather than to PATHS). 56\nIn summary: the international evidence base for PATHS is strong, but in UK primary schools it is more tentative\u2014quality of implementation appears important for outcomes, but is challenging to achieve given busy timetables. See Table 2 and Recommendation 6 for responses to this challenge.\nBox 8: The promise and challenge of SEL programmes \u2014 PATHS\f25Improving social and emotional learning in primary schools\nM T W T F\nWhat to do Questions to ask\n\u2022 Identify your own aims and needs as a school.\n", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Social_and_Emotional_Learning.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ux2ZVY-H62jpu0xzPvuWIaW6mMGtPxRN", "source_id": "e8d637c88ad0e99d399f844be26e40bf15211fc84f01bf87ff947205064caf09", "page_number": 26, "split_id": 86, "split_idx_start": 61585, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "79bde9aa367991d719dedf4879dcfd769cca8ed56731feb28d81dfd23ac2f5a9", "range": [615, 772]}, {"doc_id": "812ec284f51799b08828bdad1999799d293216c5