833288192749, 0.009224181063473225, -0.02175845019519329, -0.021851796656847, 0.04434126615524292, 0.03679489344358444, 0.04607981815934181, -0.03265337273478508], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "33bc685924ebad6983072fd35304bd88732e5b127d854ef1c047246e27a8cd99", "content": "This chunk is part of Recommendation 4, which emphasizes the importance of using a SAFE curriculum for teaching social and emotional learning (SEL) in primary schools. It discusses the necessity of identifying specific skills to be taught purposefully and highlights the significance of sequencing lessons to facilitate progressive skill development across different year groups.Programmes should identify specific skills that they want children to develop, and teach these purposefully, rather than having a more general approach. See Recommendation 1 for more detail on specific skills.\nBox 9: SAFE principles\f27Improving social and emotional learning in primary schools\nS\nA\nF\nE\nequential\nctive\nocussed\nxplicit\nSequence lessons to build skills progressively\nAs with any learning, new knowledge and more complicated skills need to be broken down into smaller steps and sequentially mastered. Schools\u2019 teaching of SEL should therefore aim to develop skills that are developmentally appropriate for each year group, in a coordinated and connected way. \u2018Sequencing\u2019 refers both to the development of content within each year, and across year groups. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Social_and_Emotional_Learning.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ux2ZVY-H62jpu0xzPvuWIaW6mMGtPxRN", "source_id": "e8d637c88ad0e99d399f844be26e40bf15211fc84f01bf87ff947205064caf09", "page_number": 28, "split_id": 99, "split_idx_start": 65734, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "155e40adc336443c326be797374e174a11aa9f810ec413634f655b308f3e21a8", "range": [224, 377]}, {"doc_id": "06db156950a7feae651cccaebf45dee10c0d48b121bed0b1e05eeddb0d529aa9", "range": [0, 97]}]}, "score": n