0.00665587093681097, 0.02931700088083744, 0.04265597462654114, -0.02117026597261429, 0.046774689108133316, -0.025277020409703255, -0.030145935714244843, 0.014856216497719288, -0.033821944147348404, -0.01586192287504673, 0.012268728576600552, 1.9848059105628644e-34, 0.01199808344244957, -0.0455051027238369, -0.013645586557686329, -0.06989026814699173, -0.010767820291221142, -0.012479521334171295, -0.012712047435343266, 0.04208774119615555, 0.025487706065177917, 0.016178801655769348, -0.016558321192860603], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "8014f4ef5476c963ca5839ebb73f061f15309015122840b6f6951e7a4e64b623", "content": "This chunk is part of the recommendations on how to effectively implement Social and Emotional Learning (SEL) in primary schools, specifically discussing the duration and frequency of SEL lessons. It emphasizes the importance of running SEL programmes for a year to ensure depth and ongoing development, while noting that quality of delivery is more crucial than merely increasing the amount of teaching time.There is little evidence to support very brief SEL interventions. Most evidence suggests SEL lessons should run for a year, with embedding, repetition, and increasing challenge in subsequent year groups. An exception to this rule is that some positive effects have been demonstrated in programmes operating for 8-10 weeks for specific skills or for specific groups of pupils. 61 \u2022 Quality matters more than quantity. Increasing the quantity of explicit SEL teaching beyond one hour per week is unlikely to be feasible for most teachers. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Social_and_Emotional_Learning.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ux2ZVY-H62jpu0xzPvuWIaW6mMGtPxRN", "source_id": "e8d637c88ad0e99d399f844be26e40bf15211fc84f01bf87ff947205064caf09", "page_number": 30, "split_id": 104, "split_idx_start": 69016, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "f53cba923384624c3652187d58131d1c2d39f0782c8bbcb407e50fe9fbc4d580", "range":