4891039337962866, 0.03425787389278412, 0.11552465707063675, 0.04262487590312958, 0.028568701818585396, -0.00527923833578825, 0.0067937555722892284, -0.04387741535902023, 0.029824312776327133, -0.020097043365240097, -0.017829176038503647, -0.05612347647547722, 0.035344209522008896, 0.006395268719643354, -0.026651723310351372, 0.02200792171061039, 0.02914658561348915, 0.026403961703181267, 0.012583556585013866, 0.02537059411406517, -0.005409504286944866, -0.08305326104164124, -0.03640046715736389, -0.013089246116578579, 0.029056234285235405, -0.023182539269328117, 0.02236785925924778, -0.007518670987337828, -0.01706535555422306, -0.024697912856936455, -0.034572966396808624, 0.06595566123723984, 0.015074383467435837, 0.056776586920022964, -0.017533352598547935, 0.02823699824512005, -0.06654813140630722, -0.060355622321367264, 0.009985142387449741, -0.016501471400260925, 0.005375095177441835, 0.01804397441446781, -0.05109129473567009, -0.0018659388879314065, 0.009446907788515091, 0.032199401408433914, -0.009137698449194431, 0.012775836512446404, 0.004735623486340046, -0.05718698352575302, 0.006961243227124214, 0.007535174023360014, 0.062404077500104904, -0.005352172069251537, 0.025798190385103226, -0.012580114416778088, 0.014426658861339092, 0.0013230418553575873, -0.02391659840941429, -0.0022651543840765953, -0.01007166225463152, 2.3994838129232446e-34, 0.0185752771794796, -0.03823236748576164, 0.011156701482832432, -0.09531352669000626, 0.007415772415697575, -0.011281585320830345, -0.03199348598718643, 0.018392588943243027, 0.002387438900768757, 0.023291761055588722, -0.047481097280979156], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "4f6147d2be7593bf085e35e09d31ec2d07130d147eea5b17318b0e7fb046f101", "content": "This chunk is located in the section discussing the effectiveness of whole-school approaches to social and emotional learning (SEL). It emphasizes that while classroom-based SEL interventions show stronger evidence of effectiveness, whole-school practices can still be beneficial if implemented thoughtfully, highlighting the role of student involvement in shaping school culture.67 However, the evidence underpinning the effectiveness of whole school approaches to SEL is less strong than the adoption of classroom-based SEL interventions; 68 they are also more challenging to implement due to the number and complexity of school systems that need to be addressed. Nonetheless, whole school practices can be developed incrementally, with commitment from senior leadership, clarity about aims, starting small and with realistic expectations. 69\nRecommendation 1 provided the example of \u2018class rules\u2019\u2014with evidence that rules are more likely to be followed when children have been involved in developing them. Similarly, at a whole-school level, involving children in \u2018school councils\u2019 or \u2018action groups\u2019 with representatives from each class could be an effective way of creating shared values across the school, and identifying and addressing issues that affect socio-emotional development.\nThere has been little evaluation of school councils in primary schools to date. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Social_and_Emotional_Learning.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ux2ZVY-H62jpu0xzPvuWIaW6mMGtPxRN", "source_id": "e8d637c88ad0e99d399f844be26e40bf15211fc84f01bf87ff947205064caf09", "page_number": 32, "split_id": 109, "split_idx_start": 72784, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "6ccb1e9b66560550558a1140dd27561eef371e9ee56a8e117d7799de44b2edbe", "range": [711, 997]}, {"doc_id": "57d35251ff3353d412a95be7c23c95099daf68844eb52b6f535d2d2e6fc12b36", "range": [0, 80]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.06380040943622589, -0.05487724021077156, 0.008705065585672855, 0.028453532606363297, -0.004108027555048466, 0.045766644179821014, 0.03656278923153877, -0.049035876989364624, 0.0038844032678753138, 0.009494414553046227, 0.04662202671170235, -0.003847818821668625, 0.011736216023564339, 0.004971