56228160858, -0.008414830081164837, -0.0005116155953146517, 0.007606480736285448, 0.036575522273778915, -0.018932556733489037, -0.012392374686896801, 0.03445257991552353, -0.07360968738794327, 0.020441511645913124, 0.019885171204805374, 0.040228404104709625, -0.029529670253396034, 0.013885455206036568, 0.002871584380045533, 0.02051691710948944, 0.003660110756754875, -0.0422668419778347, 0.0007211225456558168, -0.008161406964063644, 1.9269015639365874e-34, -0.01781305857002735, -0.008263546973466873, 0.017679477110505104, -0.09385958313941956, 0.003890217747539282, 0.0038615346420556307, -0.008412402123212814, 0.04027193784713745, 0.0051735276356339455, 0.026366766542196274, -0.022763654589653015], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "525c07ad2118ef98fdf67ed9aa59a3e15740e6f232f2fcc8a41b7825ff0dd943", "content": "This chunk is located in the section discussing the integration of social and emotional learning (SEL) with behavior management strategies. It emphasizes the importance of developing SEL skills as foundational for promoting positive behavior among students. It also introduces the KiVa program as a practical example of a whole-school approach to reducing bullying and fostering empathy.For example, Figure 2 provides a framework for developing a consistent, school-wide approach to integrating social and emotional development with behaviour management, illustrating SEL skill-development as the foundation for good behaviour. As this report shows, the social and emotional skills in this pyramid can be developed actively through teaching and whole school efforts.\f32\nEducation Endowment Foundation Use selectively:\n\u2022 Loss of privileges\n\u2022 Timeout\n\u2022 Call parent\n\u2022 Work chore\nUse Liberally\nUse Liberally\nBenefits for child Teacher skills and strategies\nDisruptive behaviours\nNon-disruptive behaviours\nResponsibility\nPredictability\nObedience\nSocial skills\nAcademic skills\nMotivation\nProblem Solving\nCooperation\nSelf-esteem Empathy Attention and involvement\nPraise\nEncouragment\nClear limits\nClassroom structures\nNon verbal cues\nPositive verbal redirect\nConsequences\nReminder of expected behaviour\nWarning of consequences\nRe-engagment strategies\nDistractions\nIncentives\nCelebrations\nPlay Problem solving\nListening Talking\nKiVa is a whole-school approach aimed at reducing bullying, first developed in Finland (where it is used by 90% of schools) and now used in several other countries. It places particular emphasis on the role of the bystander in intervening when bullying takes place. Classroom lessons aim to: (1) raise awareness among children of the role that the group plays in bullying; (2) increase empathy towards victims of bullying; and (3) promote children\u2019s skills in supporting victims. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Social_and_Emotional_Learning.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ux2ZVY-H62jpu0xzPvuWIaW6mMGtPxRN", "source_id": "e8d637c88ad0e99d399f844be26e40bf15211fc84f01bf87ff947205064caf09", "page_number": 33, "split_id": 113, "split_idx_start": 76869, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "8690e9b9070f06bde64d54985ea8cd5bc674c8f6b089c16df711dfe243305f2b", "range": [1194, 1435]}, {"doc_id": "049456959a7ed8de8a3710feb774076ea4f010523b2c68e1e84060c3a1bf53b7", "range": [0, 214]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.10713431984186172, -0.11039499938488007, 0.003115379251539707, 0.013912428170442581, 0.015447711572051048, 0.06000448763370514, 0.05389201268553734, -0.005609885323792696, 0.007352406624704599, -0.003550227265805006, 0.052099309861660004, 0.00916235987097025, -0.0053201960399746895, 0.007854493334889412, 0.025069380179047585, -0.09266267716884613, 0.003212685463950038, 0.03494512662291527, 0.025525972247123718, 0.014674839563667774, 0.013750175014138222, 0.02033921703696251, -0.013410631567239761, 0.021178804337978363, -0.016453631222248077, -0.037547074258327484, 0.0029775616712868214, 0.006604891736060381, 0.021137403324246407, -0.039646