4991412163, 0.03532087430357933, -0.00941442046314478, 1.995514388592367e-34, -0.029818112030625343, -0.00947643257677555, 0.034380149096250534, -0.10682367533445358, 0.0028314453084021807, 0.0056637730449438095, -0.0063557373359799385, 0.034054532647132874, -0.0040332903154194355, 0.05508197098970413, -0.02683393470942974], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "049456959a7ed8de8a3710feb774076ea4f010523b2c68e1e84060c3a1bf53b7", "content": "This chunk is situated within the section discussing the reinforcement of social and emotional learning (SEL) practices in schools, specifically highlighting the KiVa program's focus on bullying prevention. It emphasizes the importance of group dynamics, empathy towards victims, and parental engagement, aligning with the document's recommendations for effective SEL implementation.Classroom lessons aim to: (1) raise awareness among children of the role that the group plays in bullying; (2) increase empathy towards victims of bullying; and (3) promote children\u2019s skills in supporting victims. Lessons follow the SAFE practices (set out in Recommendation 4) and are reinforced through the school environment (assemblies, posters and so on), and information to parents. KiVa has had positive results in several trials internationally. 75 UK evidence is limited so far, though future evaluation is planned.\nBox 11: The role of bystanders in reducing bullying\nFigure 2: The incredible years teaching pyramid \u00ae\nAdapted from Webster-Stratton, C. and Reid, M. J. (2001) Incredible Years Teacher Training Program: Content, Methods and Processes (Facilitator Manual), Seattle.\f", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Social_and_Emotional_Learning.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ux2ZVY-H62jpu0xzPvuWIaW6mMGtPxRN", "source_id": "e8d637c88ad0e99d399f844be26e40bf15211fc84f01bf87ff947205064caf09", "page_number": 34, "split_id": 114, "split_idx_start": 78167, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "525c07ad2118ef98fdf67ed9