5367676913738, -0.06146782636642456, 0.03348608687520027, -0.02373087964951992, 0.09435144066810608, -0.01730472594499588, -0.0047793262638151646, 0.005355975590646267, -0.004352996125817299, 0.008953175507485867, -0.021573582664132118, -0.051803503185510635, -0.012292497791349888, 2.4564289377921514e-34, -0.004028293304145336, -0.037818361073732376, 0.00717484625056386, -0.0981801226735115, -0.0076949684880673885, -0.007440752815455198, 0.002703861566260457, -0.030426442623138428, -0.01200843509286642, -0.010959241539239883, -0.0475851371884346], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "6af90c9cd663fbf209e89b6336b8bd071813bbe07997d945894bf75b901ae6c9", "content": "This chunk is from the \"Foreword\" section of the guidance report by the Education Endowment Foundation. It highlights the organization's commitment to supporting education professionals in providing equitable opportunities for all students, particularly those with Special Educational Needs and Disabilities (SEND), emphasizing the significant achievement gap faced by these pupils compared to their peers.41\f2\nEducation Endowment Foundation Education professionals are usually driven by the desire to provide each and every young person with the best chance to succeed in life, no matter who they are or where they come from. Our aim at the EEF is to support professionals by arming them with the tools needed to make the biggest possible impact in achieving this. This is especially crucial for those pupils that need the most support.\nPupils with Special Educational Needs and Disability (SEND) have the greatest need for excellent teaching and are entitled to provision that supports achievement at, and enjoyment of, school. The attainment gap between pupils with SEND and their peers is twice as big as the gap between pupils eligible for free school meals and their peers. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Special_Educational_Needs_in_Mainstream_Schools_Guidance_Report.pdf", "url": "https: