 0.002310727024450898, -0.03850362077355385], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "60dd9e37c6d6aaed294a7b0df03650f11bb2080753e2a5f43896801d5aa2c2d3", "content": "This chunk is located in the \"Introduction\" section of the guidance report, where it outlines the focus of the review conducted by CEDAR on research related to pupils aged 5\u201316. It explains the intended audience for the guidance, emphasizing that supporting pupils with Special Educational Needs and Disabilities (SEND) is a collective responsibility among school leaders and teachers, not solely the SENCo.The review focused on research related to pupils aged 5\u201316. It was undertaken by the Centre for Educational Development, Appraisal and Research (CEDAR) at the University of Warwick.1 Who is this guidance for? This guidance is for school leaders, including the Special Educational Needs Coordinator (SENCo), and classroom teachers across mainstream primary and secondary schools. This guidance challenges the idea that responsibility for Special Educational Needs is solely the job of the SENCo. The SENCo has an important role in the development of a school\u2019s approach, but ensuring all pupils achieve\u2014including those with SEND\u2014is everyone\u2019s responsibility. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Special_Educational_Needs_in_Mainstream_Schools_Guidance_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1JdqrM1riD57oBlBO2rxsYWM-BECtkBOI", "source_id": "74ab0c4b037724e2e6a15bac6c92262e5c8cee040eab7233c78628898f0a7eb6", "page_number": 5, "split_id": 11, "split_idx_start": 6928, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "a4d5f180efa2e9bc03f5acdae816af0cabc20267cbb0418dda6e27826c17f441", "range": [470, 530]}, {"doc_id": "10b14adab5ad0ac834eb5a27e1b5571d9afc9839354531314a5856837656f5d2", "range": [0, 163]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.0730857104063034, -0.0924757719039917, 0.0019603839609771967, 0.004316446837037802, 0.02115434780716896, 0.03191149979829788, 0.04812527820467