 provided by the Education Endowment Foundation (EEF). It highlights the support available through Research Schools and regional teams to foster partnerships and improve educational practices for pupils with Special Educational Needs and Disabilities (SEND).Schools may also want to seek support from our Research Schools at r esearchschool.org.uk a national network of schools funded by the EEF and the Institute for Effective Education to support teachers to implement the evidence-based recommendations contained in our guidance reports and keep them in touch with the latest research. In addition, the EEF has six regional teams across the country that help foster and coordinate school improvement partnerships with local authorities, multi-academy trusts, Teaching School Alliances, and informal groups of schools. The EEF is always looking for practical examples that help bring evidence-based recommendations to life. If you have examples of a recommendation that has been effectively implemented in your school, then please get in touch: info@eefoundation.org.uk\nAn introduction to SEND\nThe starting points for educating all pupils are the same: an acceptance of diversity, pupils\u2019 rights, and the knowledge that all pupils can learn if they receive good teaching. All pupils have a right to effective teaching and full participation in the community of a school as set out in international agreements ( the UN Convention on the Rights of the Child, 1989) and education law in England (the Equality Act, 2010 and the Children and Families Act, 2014). ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Special_Educational_Needs_in_Mainstream_Schools_Guidance_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1JdqrM1riD57oBlBO2rxsYWM-BECtkBOI", "source_id": "74ab0c4b037724e2e6a15bac6c92262e5c8cee040eab7233c78628898f0a7eb6", "page_number": 6, "split_id": 15, "split_idx_start": 9273, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "83785fe85eb80f0c5383c676a1228dcbcb4216305fbd4c7ca58be