830503821373, -0.008998998440802097, -0.0080450139939785, 0.008622976019978523, -0.030154207721352577, 0.00541237136349082, -0.04847245290875435, 0.050182241946458817, -0.04495718702673912, -0.030494339764118195, 0.013182580471038818, -0.011122127063572407, 0.03765236213803291, 0.012410400435328484, -0.033146586269140244, -0.026432063430547714, 2.2794940791193577e-34, 0.005854927934706211, 0.028908327221870422, 0.009184340015053749, -0.10881926119327545, -0.003955497406423092, -0.04573914781212807, 0.02774663455784321, 0.018509717658162117, 0.009591277688741684, 0.023416336625814438, -0.046940818428993225], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "7cdfb7b423cabf09699057fd7bd83d179f8a038e816ca907c26ee367f78a0790", "content": "This chunk is part of the section discussing the variability of special educational needs and disabilities (SEND) over time, highlighting that many pupils classified as having SEND may not retain those needs as they progress in their education. It emphasizes the importance of understanding how individual development and interactions within the school environment influence learning and the role of effective teaching practices.Others who are considered to have SEND at the beginning of their lives may no longer have these needs later in life. Recent research has indicated that this change happens for significant proportions of pupils with SEND.7 By the end of Year 11, 44% of pupils had been classified as having SEND at some point in their schooling but only around 15% of pupils are considered to have had SEND at any one time.9\nSupporting pupils with SEND\nEvery pupils\u2019 development is driven by the progressively more complex everyday activities and interactions with the people, objects, and symbols they experience in their immediate environment (\u2018processes\u2019). At school, teachers routinely plan and organise these \u2018processes\u2019 to enhance development for their classes. This happens, for example, through direct teaching, group or individual learning, peer-to-\npeer activities, reading, learning a new skill, and so on. The quality of teachers\u2019 planning and of their delivery of teaching and learning therefore has a major impact on every pupil\u2019s development. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Special_Educational_Needs_in_Mainstream_Schools_Guidance_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1JdqrM1riD57oBlBO2rxsYWM-BECtkBOI", "source_id": "74ab0c4b037724e2e6a15bac6c92262e5c8cee040eab7233c78628898f0a7eb6", "page_number": 7, "split_id": 21, "split_idx_start": 14086, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "9f31cdf38a6c3da451e9a35d4d973da0db938fb6af577122ef117a66428aafdf", "range": [371, 488]}, {"doc_id": "26aba9a7a0de6bd5ea109b5298230b5687c1a9ea7504d66d6bca14a0c9da5c7a", "range": [0, 140]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.01423886138945818, -0.05979215353727341, -0.015601607970893383, -0.006581153720617294, 0.012433142401278019, 0.01567399874329567, 0.023229438811540604, -0.047049928456544876, -0.0014211867237463593, 0.0014217719435691833, 0.05669161677360535, -0.02989644929766655, 0.0386822372674942, -0.011918456293642521, -0.0035619684495031834, -0.11759326606988907, 0.0021218708716332912, 0.009114534594118595, -0.07653181254863739, 0.029313266277313232, -0.020482469350099564, 0.00877524446696043, -0.019958671182394028, 0.0017875718185678124, 0.007309809792786837, -0.05048499256372452, 0.0022772245574742556, -0.00929736066609621, 0.03858564421534538, 0.0037511209957301617, 0.03766825795173645, 0.07382309436798096, 0.01277833990752697, -0.02218218706548214, 2.415789140286506e-06, 0.06318973004817963, 0.04468173533678055, -0.04113757237792015, -0.0390193909406662, -0.015481905080378056, -0.06585846096277237, -0.0012297393986955285, 0.02090681903064251, 0.020240141078829765, -0.001646106713451445, 0.057088252156972885, 0.0184794794768095, -0.05097290500998497, 0.005177980754524469, -0.05422290787100792, 0.0016711951466277242, 0.005658152978867292, -0.016902947798