112836718559, 0.06489048153162003, -0.06741096824407578, 2.9837596571269387e-07, 0.004635237623006105, 0.03831188380718231, 0.008986590430140495, -0.033085811883211136, -0.0046754805371165276, -0.012945665046572685, -0.007159646134823561, 0.015111343935132027, 0.041244026273489, 0.0885927751660347, 0.020345358178019524, -0.011569472961127758, 0.008774235844612122, -0.038602475076913834, 0.03748276084661484, -0.03144054859876633, -0.029282405972480774, 0.014905229210853577, 0.002078999299556017, 0.032035671174526215, 0.025073619559407234, 0.013688803650438786, -0.0023818467743694782, 0.006082567386329174, -0.009267400950193405, 0.00440661096945405, -0.053397081792354584, 0.023149574175477028, 0.05621277913451195, -0.01738644950091839, 0.03703168407082558, -0.0019346445333212614, 0.021456263959407806, 0.012001028284430504, -0.05727480351924896, -0.0039259823970496655, -0.019791487604379654, 0.005780564621090889, -0.0016275153029710054, 0.10145705193281174, -0.008245039731264114, 0.019520338624715805, 0.00397014245390892, 0.0031585944816470146, -0.0065485527738928795, -0.022292999550700188, 0.06860686093568802, 0.09392842650413513, -0.025102922692894936, 0.009984975680708885, 0.025767063722014427, 0.04880106449127197, 0.017734035849571228, -0.01264888234436512, -0.005036861635744572, -0.04533040523529053, 0.01413832139223814, -0.040964361280202866, 0.03486892208456993, -0.03152518719434738, -0.03299092873930931, 0.011308401823043823, -0.020849762484431267, 0.017636822536587715, 0.009937695227563381, -0.021669529378414154, -0.03805548697710037, 2.0138566922400026e-34, -0.005459503270685673, 0.04011132940649986, 0.010127988643944263, -0.07717613130807877, -0.012140023522078991, -0.030133787542581558, 0.039635397493839264, 0.0215985756367445, 0.025357449427247047, 0.03951834887266159, -0.03569746017456055], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "26aba9a7a0de6bd5ea109b5298230b5687c1a9ea7504d66d6bca14a0c9da5c7a", "content": "This chunk is part of the \"Introduction\" section of the guidance report on Special Educational Needs in Mainstream Schools. It emphasizes the importance of teachers' planning and instructional delivery in shaping pupils' development, while also noting the influence of personal characteristics and environmental factors on learning. The Bronfenbrenner diagram is referenced to illustrate these interactions.The quality of teachers\u2019 planning and of their delivery of teaching and learning therefore has a major impact on every pupil\u2019s development. However, individual pupils\u2019 development is also influenced by the interaction of \u2018what happens in class every day\u2019 with their personal characteristics, wider environmental influences, and time10 (see Figure 2: The Bronfenbrenner diagram). This approach helps to highlight that special educational needs are not something the pupil needs to change about themselves; rather, the school needs to consider how to change the quality of what happens in the immediate environment to best support the pupil\u2019s learning, taking into account the individual, the home and wider community, and time.\f6\nEducation Endowment Foundation Figure 2: Bronfenbrenner diagram\nFigure 1: The key influences that interactively affect pupils\u2019 learning and development 9\nleading todrive\ninfluence\nEVERYDAY ACTIVITIES AND INTERACTIONS\nDifferent\ndevelopmental OUTCOMES\nENVIRONMENT\ne.g. class, school, local area\nDEVELOPMENTe.g. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Special_Educational_Needs_in_Mainstream_Schools_Guidance_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1JdqrM1riD57oBlBO2rxsYWM-BECtkBOI", "source_id": "74ab0c4b037724e2e6a15bac6c92262e5c8cee040eab7233c78628898f0a7eb6", "page_number": 7, "split_id": 22, "split_idx_start": 14986, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "7cdfb7b423cabf09699057fd7bd83d179f8a038e816ca907c26ee367f78a0790", "range": [900, 1040]}, {"doc_id": "dbb7409ce02fe0f75534978215e22aee3fcdeb066dff476294f5160cfc0329f6", "range": [