004135326307732612, -0.001476271077990532, -0.0940108597278595, -0.007832947187125683, -0.03167484328150749, -0.03921970725059509, 0.017240092158317566, 0.03425171598792076, 0.04656137153506279, -0.062019798904657364], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "8d4e991de23fd091e856c5c39beb051bc19444f7095e6dc50770b21ee16b699e", "content": "This chunk is part of the \"Introduction\" section, which discusses the impact of environmental factors and personal characteristics on pupils' educational experiences and needs. It emphasizes the importance of considering both the school environment and individual traits in understanding and supporting students with Special Educational Needs and Disabilities (SEND).Environment\nPupils\u2019 needs will change as they move through different environments and life experiences. \u2018Environment\u2019 does not just mean the physical environment in the school; it refers to the activities that pupils take part in and the interactions they have with staff and other pupils. Environmental factors can play an important role in creating barriers that compromise a pupil\u2019s experience at school.\nPersonal Characteristics\nPupils have different personal characteristics that lead them to react differently to the same environment. Personal characteristics alone do not determine the presence, type, or complexity of special educational needs.\f", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Special_Educational_Needs_in_Mainstream_Schools_Guidance_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1JdqrM1riD57oBlBO2rxsYWM-BECtkBOI", "source_id": "74ab0c4b037724e2e6a15bac6c92262e5c8cee040eab7233c78628898f0a7eb6", "page_number": 8, "split_id": 25, "split_idx_start": 16713, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "fb1acccd3ecd44b31d22895c6fcc5e045360d796a7f476e2e59cd10358ad8c66", "range": [399, 503]}, {"doc_id": "9203db6c5ef51fd41767f4ce1e86717a112affe80cbcfa748a601e767014d473", "range": [0, 112]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.017013434320688248,