25276566, -0.027285631746053696, 0.00899491086602211, 0.026518037542700768, 0.05759337171912193, 0.019080964848399162, 0.08871106803417206, -0.0038549199234694242, -0.009722419083118439, 0.004181430209428072, -0.058276861906051636, 0.06513195484876633, -0.011473556980490685, 0.0005679384921677411, 0.022353265434503555, -0.018122587352991104, 0.021635403856635094, -0.012630075216293335, -0.0018963823094964027, -0.009678308852016926, -0.0039194864220917225, 0.0048159705474972725, 0.006802277639508247, -0.041386961936950684, 0.014834939502179623, 0.06454452872276306, -0.014882874675095081, 0.07393331825733185, -0.004718895070254803, 0.02847854420542717, 0.009650439023971558, -0.05136600509285927, 0.014141891151666641, -0.00783617328852415, 0.014174534939229488, -0.013599749654531479, 0.06951829791069031, -0.01800205186009407, 0.0004091732553206384, -0.014295482076704502, -0.017600590363144875, -0.018748732283711433, 0.008714782074093819, 0.03932354599237442, 0.07073268294334412, -0.0018728111172094941, -0.001890794956125319, 0.0013655776856467128, 0.04728621244430542, 0.0062502422370016575, -0.002823583548888564, 0.028042104095220566, -0.07400117069482803, 0.025416197255253792, -0.04618764668703079, 0.058588191866874695, 0.0028629975859075785, -0.026627572253346443, 0.04148764908313751, -0.0359860397875309, 0.012943999841809273, 0.012690634466707706, -0.05239366367459297, -0.02586531639099121, 1.8059209251291557e-34, -0.02615876868367195, 0.015491830185055733, -0.010068221017718315, -0.08006121218204498, -0.0010968272108584642, -0.04294231906533241, 0.0365978442132473, 0.01565360836684704, 0.02889774926006794, 0.0022611322347074747, -0.024283919483423233], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "fc95460340cac9beaa61946b067bd06cc70e6cca85f0068ee25556f368380768", "content": "This chunk is part of the \"Introduction\" and \"Summary of Recommendations\" sections in the guidance report on supporting pupils with Special Educational Needs and Disabilities (SEND) in mainstream schools. It emphasizes the importance of understanding the barriers to learning for these pupils and introduces five strategies aimed at facilitating their inclusion and success within the educational environment.It means understanding the specific barriers pupils face to learning and what they need in order to thrive so that they can be included in all that the school has to offer.\nWe now consider five strategies aimed at supporting pupils with SEND.\nCognition\nand learning\nSensory and/or physical needs\nSocial,\nemotional and mental health\nCommunication and interaction\f8\nEducation Endowment Foundation SUMMARY OF RECOMMENDATIONS\n\u2022 An inclusive school removes barriers to learning and participation, provides an education that is appropriate to pupils\u2019 needs, and promotes high standards and the fulfilment of potential for all pupils. Schools should: \u2014 promote positive relationships, active engagement, and wellbeing for all pupils; \u2014 ensure all pupils can access the best possible teaching; and \u2014 adopt a positive and proactive approach to behaviour, as described in the EEF\u2019s Improving Behaviour in Schools guidance report. \u2022 Schools should aim to understand individual pupil\u2019s learning needs using the graduated approach of the \u2018assess, plan, do, review\u2019 approach. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Special_Educational_Needs_in_Mainstream_Schools_Guidance_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1JdqrM1riD57oBlBO2rxsYWM-BECtkBOI", "source_id": "74ab0c4b037724e2e6a15bac6c92262e5c8cee040eab7233c78628898f0a7eb6", "page_number": 9, "split_id": 29, "split_idx_start": 19313, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "6280c189f34a08435b2647a25a9c1062c9a1ee2505535fdbfd7272c99a601a45", "range": [635, 808]}, {"doc_id": "97bf4db6bc3578d94c5229db6a786246274a1fdf8d63997477748d2a5cc4f304", "range": [0, 142]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.023336386308073997, -0.12715376913547516, -0.006532211787998676, 0.