0865365117788, 2.1929877968034147e-34, -0.02586737647652626, 0.017291411757469177, 0.0052735633216798306, -0.10702764242887497, -0.014047693461179733, -0.020964456722140312, 0.026502491906285286, 0.014201183803379536, 0.04709581658244133, 0.012035371735692024, -0.03802429884672165], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "43a2e99b0f990b227e20e407db5d43f08c51e694a669bedf033a3d6c67b91aa3", "content": "This chunk is situated in the section discussing the need for teachers to identify effective interventions and teaching strategies for pupils with Special Educational Needs (SEND). It highlights the challenges faced by a newly qualified teacher, Binda, as she navigates the landscape of pedagogical approaches related to various diagnoses and considers their applicability in her classroom.Binda starts exploring particular interventions and strategies commonly associated with different diagnostic labels but it is difficult to work out what is likely to be effective. There seem to be many strategies recommended for common conditions, like dyslexia or autism, but it is not clear how much these should be emphasised relative to strategies Binda is already using to teach all the pupils in her class. Discussion questions:\nIs there a special pedagogy for special educational needs? Is \u2018what works\u2019 for one pupil likely to be applicable to other pupils with the same identified need?\nWhat other key factors should be taken into consideration when selecting a strategy to try?\f", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Special_Educational_Needs_in_Mainstream_Schools_Guidance_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1JdqrM1riD57oBlBO2rxsYWM-BECtkBOI", "source_id": "74ab0c4b037724e2e6a15bac6c92262e5c8cee040eab7233c78628898f0a7eb6", "page_number": 22, "split_id": 76, "split_idx_start": 51749, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "048a01e9127780041022174526ac3034149cac1eab6eea3487759b82106ec6d0", "range": [524, 704]}, {"doc_id": "7cae75c5a0864b3967b9fc54d1efa