5638, -0.023485183715820312, 0.03308495506644249, -0.008946847170591354, -0.05033024400472641, 0.004401220008730888, -0.010406329296529293, 0.051984988152980804, 0.00044790495303459466, -0.041352078318595886, -0.039545245468616486, 1.7952452782895296e-34, 0.007433087565004826, 0.019149472936987877, 0.022279050201177597, -0.10038093477487564, -0.004017227794975042, -0.015301527455449104, 0.05950571596622467, 0.0008933445787988603, 0.017844442278146744, 0.04491959512233734, -0.025582483038306236], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "e96491c5730f53984dea164157002267b570b4962f12725bd92d91f29455f1a6", "content": "This chunk is situated in the section discussing the importance of high-quality teaching for pupils with Special Educational Needs and Disabilities (SEND). It emphasizes that interventions should not replace effective teaching but should enhance it, aligning with the guidance that high-quality, differentiated instruction is essential for supporting students with SEND.There is a risk that we respond with flimsy interventions (they provide a nice paper trail) but don\u2019t stop to consider the quality of our teaching. Any additional interventions and strategies must supplement high quality teaching and learning and not replace it. The Code of Practice is clear on this: high quality teaching, differentiated for individual pupils, is the first step in responding to pupils who have or may have SEND. Additional support cannot compensate for a lack of good teaching. As a SENCo, I have embraced the current interest in cognitive science and how we learn. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Special_Educational_Needs_in_Mainstream_Schools_Guidance_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1JdqrM1riD57oBlBO2rxsYWM-BECtkBOI", "source_id": "74ab0c4b037724e2e6a15bac6c92262e5c8cee040eab7233c78628898f0a7eb6", "page_number": 23, "split_id": 79, "split_idx_start": 53340, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "4a6b31578ccac0519bcffc78ae0a0a7610ee9b1405