650810242], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "db0300163b3b5f00187340b8398550b0f480661e6494b8f7d7104b6b3833506d", "content": "This chunk appears in the section discussing the importance of high-quality teaching for all pupils, emphasizing that effective teaching strategies beneficial for students with Special Educational Needs and Disability (SEND) are also advantageous for all learners. It highlights cognitive and metacognitive strategies as essential components of effective teaching.Good teaching for children with SEND is good teaching for all.\u2019\nBox 6: A view from a SENCo\u2026 \u201c \u201c\f22\nEducation Endowment Foundation Cognitive and metacognitive strategies Cognition is the mental process involved in knowing, understanding, and learning. Cognitive strategies are skills like memorisation techniques or subject-specific strategies like methods to solve problems in maths. Cognitive strategies are fundamental to learning and are the \u2018bread and butter\u2019 of effective teaching. 29 Metacognition refers to the ways in which pupils monitor and purposefully direct their thinking and learning. Metacognitive strategies are strategies we use to monitor or control our cognition, such as checking whether our approach to solving a mathematics problem worked or considering which cognitive strategy is the best fit for a task. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Special_Educational_Needs_in_Mainstream_Schools_Guidance_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1JdqrM1riD57oBlBO2rxsYWM-BECtkBOI", "source_id": "74ab0c4b037724e2e6a15bac6c92262e5c8cee040eab7233c78628898f0a7eb6", "page_number": 23, "split_id": 81, "split_idx_start": 54311, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "1fa16c8ed38daa8c5abf49e152d8d4999eb036e99b581385ff2cc2577ba47f98", "range": [473, 724]}, {"doc_id": "2163769a4cfb7bd4c0eddd363b970a186a9b72e3cb311854e59625cd5b420eaa", "range": [0, 230]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.017857594415545464, -0.11463836580514908, -0