93251037598, 0.02005254477262497, 0.014733108691871166, -0.05843810737133026, -0.027683764696121216, -0.06785766035318375, -0.03764645755290985, 0.008740629069507122, 0.03377753868699074, 0.02920050173997879, -0.04464845359325409], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "5b0c7260485c761df9dc31fa95fe334cd4eaef9516c5faacd0bdb752610f42fc", "content": "This chunk is situated in the section on \"Ensure all pupils have access to high quality teaching.\" It discusses the importance of forming flexible small groups for targeted instruction and collaborative learning, emphasizing strategies to enhance pupils' understanding and skills, particularly through metacognitive knowledge.Such groups can be formed for an explicit purpose and disbanded when that purpose is met. It may be that a small group of pupils share the need for more explicit instruction to independently carry out a skill, remember a fact, or understand a concept. Allocating pupils to temporary groups can also allow teachers to set up opportunities for collaborative learning; research has indicated, for example, that collaborative learning can be effective in helping pupils to read history texts.28 Teachers may also set up groups for pupils to work collaboratively on tasks such as reviewing background information, reading and analysing source documents, and completing graphic organisers.\n1. Teachers should acquire the professional understanding and skills to develop their pupils\u2019 metacognitive knowledge.\n", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Special_Educational_Needs_in_Mainstream_Schools_Guidance_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1JdqrM1riD57oBlBO2rxsYWM-BECtkBOI", "source_id": "74ab0c4b037724e2e6a15bac6c92262e5c8cee040eab7233c78628898f0a7eb6", "page_number": 24, "split_id": 83, "split_idx_start": 56031, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "2163769a4cfb7bd4c0eddd363b970a186a9b72e3cb311854e59625cd5b420eaa", "range": [1120, 1210]}, {"doc_id": "753ae3bd10d32caea128d704fb65906