51612687111, 0.017126573249697685, -0.011735585518181324, -0.013271910138428211, -0.054147057235240936, 0.008360326290130615, -0.012991856783628464, 0.044135041534900665, 0.028350912034511566, -0.031369514763355255, 0.007706102449446917, -0.02897590957581997, 0.006923372391611338, -0.016425572335720062, -0.08833852410316467, -0.05844290554523468, 2.7042470241744907e-34, -0.02317669242620468, -0.04399368539452553, 0.0003740997635759413, -0.022135499864816666, -0.04427118971943855, -0.035956911742687225, 0.05400390550494194, 0.028482753783464432, 0.037801697850227356, 0.024291926994919777, -0.04000250622630119], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "e87e341d55c9f0add819af702ea4776ca470333136991ea36032ef4a4e46be4e", "content": "This chunk is situated in the section discussing \"Explicit Instruction\" under Recommendation 3, \"Ensure all pupils have access to high quality teaching.\" It provides a practical example of how a teacher can use explicit instruction techniques to teach summarization skills, emphasizing the importance of modeling and anticipating misconceptions in the learning process.Example: summarising a paragraph A teacher might teach a pupil a strategy for summarising a paragraph by breaking up the strategy into small steps.33 The teacher would initially \u2018think aloud\u2019 while identifying the topic of the paragraph to model this process to the pupil. They would then give the pupil the opportunity to practice this skill, perhaps giving the pupil one paragraph at a time to support them to focus on the information that is essential to the task. Then the teacher would model the skill of finding the main idea in a paragraph and guide the pupil to practise finding both the topic and the main idea. The teacher could anticipate potential misconceptions\u2014\nthat, for example, paragraphs always contain the main idea in the first sentence\u2014by providing counter-\nexamples: in this case, paragraphs that contain the main idea at different points. Box 8 and Box 9 show further examples of explicit instruction.\n", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Special_Educational_Needs_in_Mainstream_Schools_Guidance_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1JdqrM1riD57oBlBO2rxsYWM-BECtkBOI", "source_id": "74ab0c4b037724e2e6a15bac6c92262e5c8cee040eab7233c78628898f0a7eb6", "page_number": 26, "split_id": 93, "split_idx_start": 60380, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "7bfc5501fbfa5a0624661ef98cdb931b316a4bd68577ba5022d796aa034ffb64", "range": [782, 1055]}, {"doc_id": "7f21835ac7aec2a492001d8465becbdf5bca7b8596a0a0da0061755090e430c1", "range": [0, 63]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.04625489562749863, -0.12354385852813721, 0.0030647022649645805, -0.013654840178787708, -0.01952456310391426, 0.0337560772895813, 0.016602320596575737, -0.02958892658352852, -0.038509782403707504, -0.022270862013101578, 0.07628366351127625, 0.0019064709777012467, 0.07336423546075821, -0.0406147837638855, 0.051607538014650345, -0.13189803063869476, 0.03797850385308266, 0.010686540976166725, -0.012064865790307522, 0.02462303638458252, 0.022408615797758102, -0.026940234005451202, -0.03629472106695175, 0.00011735888256225735, 0.02555406652390957, -0.022620344534516335, -0.03187704458832741, -0.00022219159291125834, -0.03757810965180397, -0.03954917564988136, 0.024306930601596832, 0.005381914786994457, 0.02402994967997074, -0.08085646480321884, 2.1632181415043306e-06, -0.010665664449334145, -0.04474537447094917, -0.009500418789684772, 0.012572048231959343, 0.053548090159893036, -0.019347986206412315, -7.387401274172589e-05, 0.035688385367393494, 0.019464969635009766, 0.018280332908034325, 0.06361502408981323, 0.01260329969227314, 0.10346625000238419, 0.0361751914024353, 0.015212801285088062, 0.016274405643343925, -0.010189851745963097, -0.01045486330986023, 0.001239836448803544, 0.05510732904076576, -0.003986096475273371, 0.0045324102975428104, -0.013680788688361645, 0.03543383628129959, 0.04823489114642143, -8.918300591176376e-