hing strategies for pupils with Special Educational Needs and Disabilities (SEND). It emphasizes the importance of addressing misconceptions in mathematics education and suggests that teachers consider the origins of these misunderstandings. It connects to the broader themes of instructional practices that support learning for pupils with SEND.Pupils will often defend their misconceptions, especially if they are based on sound, albeit limited, ideas. In this situation, teachers could think about how a misconception might have arisen and explore with pupils the \u2018partial truth\u2019 that it is built on and the circumstances within which it no longer applies. Pupils may need time and support to develop richer and more robust conceptions. The EEF guidance report, Improving Mathematics in Key Stages 2 and 3, contains further information.\nBox 9: Misconceptions in maths\nUsing technology to support pupils with SEND\nThe evidence review for this guidance report found evidence that, for pupils with SEND, technology can be a useful tool for supporting teaching. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Special_Educational_Needs_in_Mainstream_Schools_Guidance_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1JdqrM1riD57oBlBO2rxsYWM-BECtkBOI", "source_id": "74ab0c4b037724e2e6a15bac6c92262e5c8cee040eab7233c78628898f0a7eb6", "page_number": 27, "split_id": 96, "split_idx_start": 62609, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "d2a3602922998c0d59fe5fbbc2481b75c4134251aecf172fb7cd0569584f8eea", "range": [891, 1000]}, {"doc_id": "85ad4b20ee073bd14d5f40d30c25c6ed02bf77b2723c5300a8fb768af376eae9", "range": [0, 221]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.012440694496035576, -0.0994330570101738, -0.025335460901260376, 0.011597470380365849, -0.03314133360981941, 0.061209194362163544, 0.07031452655792236, -0.03726422041654587, -0.05573708564043045, -0.005848424509167671, 0.03823498636484146, -0.018961699679493904, 0.0836426317691803, -0.016122767701745033, 0.029284544289112