003d5ea638e5ee07daf53637ffddb1ac7b740db8bb8f8b", "content": "This chunk is part of the section discussing effective teaching strategies for pupils with Special Educational Needs and Disabilities (SEND), specifically focusing on the technique of scaffolding. It emphasizes the importance of gradual support from teachers to help pupils achieve independence in their learning, and provides an example of how scaffolding can be implemented in a classroom setting.Initially, a teacher would provide enough support so that pupils can successfully complete tasks that they could not do independently.35 This requires effective assessment to gain a precise understanding of the pupil\u2019s current capabilities. Support could be visual, verbal, or written. The teacher will gradually remove the support (the scaffold) as the pupil becomes able to complete the task independently. If the teacher is supporting a pupil with SEND, that scaffold may be in place for longer to promote confidence and competence that can be sustained once the scaffold is removed.\nExample: using scaffolding\nA teacher might initially scaffold for a whole class by providing a writing frame for all pupils to work from. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Special_Educational_Needs_in_Mainstream_Schools_Guidance_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1JdqrM1riD57oBlBO2rxsYWM-BECtkBOI", "source_id": "74ab0c4b037724e2e6a15bac6c92262e5c8cee040eab7233c78628898f0a7eb6", "page_number": 28, "split_id": 98, "split_idx_start": 63969, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "85ad4b20ee073bd14d5f40d30c25c6ed02bf77b2723c5300a8fb768af376eae9", "range": [866, 1107]}, {"doc_id": "2b57d82e8a0c0188b4203abc9418dc81ee9493cc486c9a782fc5b88a8abce65e", "range": [0, 138]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.009277023375034332, -0.09805876016616821, -0.008637644350528717, -0.03731914237141609, -0.032869696617126465, 0.001095755840651691, 0.060504090040922165, -0.05364121124148369, -0.03759091719985008, 0.0110