s chunk is from the section discussing the implementation of interventions for pupils with Special Educational Needs and Disabilities (SEND) in mainstream schools. It emphasizes the importance of using small-group and one-to-one interventions effectively without compromising overall teaching quality, advocating for a tiered support approach to enhance educational outcomes for these students.Tiers of support\nSmall-group and one-to-one interventions provide the opportunity to apply effective teaching strategies with a more intense focus on a smaller number of learning goals. They can be powerful tools but must be used carefully: they should not replace general efforts to improve the overall quality of teaching in the classroom. It is recommended that schools adopt a strategy that offers tiers of increasingly intense support to pupils as required, as outlined in Figure 6 .38 This approach is also used in the EEF\u2019s Pupil Premium Guide , which recommends a tiered approach to Pupil Premium funding to help schools to balance spending across approaches to improving teaching, targeted academic support and wider strategies.\nEllie, a deputy head, is reviewing the interventions provided by the school. The school has many pupils with SEND and uses an extensive list of interventions that has grown over time. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Special_Educational_Needs_in_Mainstream_Schools_Guidance_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1JdqrM1riD57oBlBO2rxsYWM-BECtkBOI", "source_id": "74ab0c4b037724e2e6a15bac6c92262e5c8cee040eab7233c78628898f0a7eb6", "page_number": 30, "split_id": 105, "split_idx_start": 67869, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "3a0e9fa621a41718a15c383ab71021da3695bf83591c57e3d90b0ad7262a1ff9", "range": [698, 884]}, {"doc_id": "9ff364825ebc3d6b8ad9d01eed9b33e5a8939eb8e4f2c4ab385a1b3578ef22d9", "range": [0, 107]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.057272326201200485, -0.13700707256793976, -0.006689069326967001, 0.000600