058900475502, 0.0006423616432584822, 0.02714478224515915, 0.0004795193381141871, -0.0399472638964653, -0.038206953555345535, 2.493265532833564e-34, -0.004210712853819132, 0.017246339470148087, 0.015104848891496658, -0.0713782012462616, -0.004705536644905806, -0.03479687497019768, 0.018722616136074066, 0.004788908176124096, 0.04751410335302353, -0.013701463118195534, -0.07100223004817963], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "40eac7d61e6d197c6eb1d6afcd70c09e249708ea68fbbdbfafb0dd49a73d13d0", "content": "This chunk is located in the section discussing the importance of structured interventions for students with Special Educational Needs (SEN). It highlights various strategies schools can adopt to enhance collaboration between staff and ensure effective delivery of interventions, emphasizing a tiered approach to support\u2014specialist, targeted, and whole-class teaching.Schools have tried several approaches, such as:\n\u2022 setting aside regular times for staff delivering interventions to meet and plan with main class teachers;\n\u2022 using assembly time to meet and discuss intervention delivery; and\n\u2022 the Senior Leadership Team being clear about how such liaison time is used.\nFigure 6: A tiered approach to educational support\nSpecialist\nTargeted\nWhole-class\nSpecialist support. In addition to \u2018good teaching for pupils with SEN is good teaching for all,\u2019 some pupils will need specialist intervention delivered by a trained professional.\nTargeted interventions. If pupils require additional support beyond what can be offered in whole class teaching, a targeted, one-to-one or small-group intervention could provide the intensive focus required for the pupil to make progress. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Special_Educational_Needs_in_Mainstream_Schools_Guidance_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1JdqrM1riD57oBlBO2rxsYWM-BECtkBOI", "source_id": "74ab0c4b037724e2e6a15bac6c92262e5c8cee040eab7233c78628898f0