756904006004, -0.017631296068429947, -0.02798417955636978, -0.031532637774944305, 2.0581569876429155e-34, -0.0017925178399309516, -0.04030999541282654, 0.03495386615395546, -0.04460672289133072, -0.011317417025566101, -0.03122679516673088, 2.0580975615303032e-05, 0.0019894614815711975, 0.01507455762475729, 0.018672719597816467, -0.04569201543927193], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "e1834491a234ac1237a5dc372b81acd53f8edeb36eeee0490e5277fe4f10a07b", "content": "This chunk is located in the section discussing the effective deployment of teaching assistants (TAs) within mainstream schools. It emphasizes the importance of strategic support from TAs for pupils with Special Educational Needs and Disability (SEND), highlighting potential negative impacts on learning and wellbeing when TAs are not deployed thoughtfully.Unfortunately, where the deployment of TAs is not carefully considered by school leadership, it can have negative impacts on pupils\u2019 learning and wellbeing.43 Box 13 describes some examples of ineffective TA deployment.\nWork effectively with teaching assistants There is a pupil on SEND support and the SENCo, Vishal, is considering how best to support him. Currently, the pupil is mainly supported by a teaching assistant, but Vishal is worried about the amount of time he is spending away from the rest of the class. He is conscious of the need for the pupil to receive high quality, whole-class teaching alongside his peers. Vishal begins to explore the research evidence on how to work with TAs to maximise their effectiveness. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Special_Educational_Needs_in_Mainstream_Schools_Guidance_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1JdqrM1riD57oBlBO2rxsYWM-BECtkBOI", "source_id": "74ab0c4b037724e2e6a15bac6c92262e5c8cee040eab7233c78628898f0a7eb6", "page_number": 36, "split_id": 125, "split_idx_start": 80287, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "3fd151a7f88c5a6446662aaed8e502e2747d202ca99cdc30f36826