01412065140902996, -0.01110137440264225, -0.028620760887861252, 1.7499362847556913e-34, -0.0029305408243089914, -0.004868754651397467, 0.012231376953423023, -0.023951677605509758, -0.0008670839597471058, -0.0311615988612175, 0.0019229172030463815, -0.004351695999503136, 0.05487203598022461, 0.05531864985823631, -0.041895218193531036], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "19c56398f6f50738ac59ce1424ea7e223903d87d075aaf3a590ba880458111f9", "content": "This chunk is located within the section discussing the effective deployment of teaching assistants (TAs) in supporting pupils with Special Educational Needs and Disabilities (SEND). It highlights potential pitfalls in TA interactions that may hinder meaningful learning, emphasizing the need for well-defined roles and collaborative teaching strategies to ensure pupils receive appropriate support.TAs conduct \u2018stereo-teaching\u2019 where their interactions with the pupil cut across the teacher\u2019s whole class delivery. Pupils with SEND receive a very different classroom experience when working with a TA. Tasks can be inappropriately targeted, repetitive, or undemanding. TAs might decide what to do based on what they think the pupil can do or understand. This is well-intentioned but may not provide appropriate challenge. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Special_Educational_Needs_in_Mainstream_Schools_Guidance_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1JdqrM1riD57oBlBO2rxsYWM-BECtkBOI", "source_id": "74ab0c4b037724e2e6a15bac6c92262e5c8cee040eab7233c78628898f0a7eb6", "page_number": 36, "split_id": 128, "split_idx_start": 81583, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "3febd8a055fa5d2b970b6dc2cf2c8fd15599063b4eb185e2c07622deeb20607b", "range": [390, 507]}, {"doc_id": "b14d531f4580f7b3ff5bd88fa2562cc327a9bad4892bcdc8d97e60b6bfb3cc61", "range": [0, 68]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.06947630643844604, -0.12065020948648453, -0.024528274312615395, 0.025597378611564636, 0.00042143205064348876