g": null}, {"id": "53530e006be6253bda240590f74f5f0555667fb3bf83990b6899f4839d646bbf", "content": "This chunk is situated in the section discussing the effective deployment of teaching assistants (TAs) and how schools can support pupils with Special Educational Needs and Disabilities (SEND). It highlights the challenges faced by parents during the transition to a new model of support that promotes student independence, while emphasizing the positive outcomes of reduced reliance on one-to-one assistance.Moving to the new model of support was a challenge for some parents of children with SEND who were concerned that their child would not receive the individualised support they were used to. In response, Heather and her team worked with parents to ensure a smooth transition to the new approach. She reports: \u2018We spoke to them and explained that the support from the TA was still there, but we were encouraging and supporting the children to see what they could do independently.\u2019\nPractice has become consistent across the school and is beginning to show impact. \u2018None of our children with an EHC plan relies on one-to-one support 100% of the time and the change in those children has been dramatic\u2019, says Heather. \u2018Their independence, self-esteem, and confidence is improving greatly. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "EEF_Special_Educational_Needs_in_Mainstream_Schools_Guidance_Report.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1JdqrM1riD57oBlBO2rxsYWM-BECtkBOI", "source_id": "74ab0c4b037724e2e6a15bac6c92262e5c8cee040eab7233c78628898f0a7eb6", "page_number": 37, "split_id": 133, "split_idx_start": 84247, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "54c24f9f782ce22d9dd3bf2bc7b3d2ac1070ea3f6b85cf9c367a934d0a487d58", "range": [584, 774]}, {"doc_id": "a31cf242c356fd8ec5bd64374a9a7d277c443e1399003d32424b40ccd2fab9d4", "range": [0, 71]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.053145214915275574, -0.08959927409887314, -0.014771794900298119, -0.00241935369