129600152373314, -0.03087843395769596, -0.019342869520187378, 0.025275759398937225, 0.04029716178774834, 0.029241090640425682, 0.00489459652453661, 0.02399480529129505, -0.03250749781727791, 0.026933040469884872, -0.03490503877401352, 0.055866315960884094, -0.036123186349868774, -0.014772657305002213, 0.047620829194784164, 0.00920210499316454, -0.01759066991508007, -0.08169520646333694, -0.06795044988393784, 0.028297659009695053, 1.5692056354379474e-34, 0.006127394735813141, 0.004998922813683748, 0.0012781348777934909, -0.0748574510216713, 0.04190036654472351, -0.006899802479892969, -0.06346727162599564, -0.009391684085130692, 0.0005726802046410739, -0.012683706358075142, -0.02339683473110199], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "295b79bf0315813038c7621fa4a783ea0b1e07f8c4835a065cd9d833b7b27e38", "content": "This chunk is part of Case Study A, focusing on Watermore Primary School, which highlights the actions taken to support Tom, an 8-year-old autistic pupil with significant anxiety. It details the implementation of strategies by staff, in collaboration with the local authority autism adviser, to reduce his anxiety and promote independence and social interactions.Actions taken and impact\nWith support from the local authority autism adviser, staff explored the likely causes of Tom\u2019s anxiety and how this might be reduced. Together they implemented strategies across the school and these reduced Tom\u2019s anxiety. Tom is now more able to do things for himself\fGood Autism Practice Guide \u2013 Case Studies\n3 Doc ref: GAP report - case studies_I-S\n( e.g. hanging up his coat and putting his bag away). He has been able to build more positive friendships with peers. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 2, "split_id": 4, "split_idx_start": 182