content": "This chunk is from Case Study A: Watermore Primary School, focusing on strategies implemented to support Tom, an 8-year-old autistic pupil. It discusses the approach of limiting choices to reduce overwhelm and the structured lunchtime model used to promote social interaction and engagement, emphasizing the importance of a supportive environment for autistic children.\u201d.\nThe number of choices Tom is given is limited to two as he finds too much choice overwhelming.\nThis is consistently applied and extends to motivators and incentives ( e.g. the TA might say \u201dYou\u2019ve earned your reward, would you like play dough or drawing?\u201d).\nTom has structured lunchtimes following a 20:20:20 model (20 minutes lunch, 20 minutes free time, 20 minutes social time). ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 4, "split_id": 14, "split_idx_start": 5923, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "51dfaf02695c6b0cb30c67fbd770affd57f93e1532ee111ad9aaf1f0d2912356", "range": [516, 519]}, {"doc_id": "de6be2136972b6d3f022daf1afc1d14472ff6795ac0e95134d4df8e0451de09d", "range": [0, 123]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.06094398722052574, -0.06487936526536942, -0.0137669388204813, -0.03239881619811058, -0.01303906925022602, 0.021921634674072266, 0.03369481861591339, -0.0180853009223938, -0.03220919519662857, 0.03195123374462128, 0.05228513851761818, -0.03485863283276558, -0.003644297830760479, 0.019477620720863342, -0.020098719745874405, -0.10784939676523209, 0.03161179646849632, 0.006573636084794998, -0.022695649415254593, 0.027539411559700966, -0.04981103911995888, -0.0023473857436329126, -0.0069632763043046, 0.03192427381873131, 0.05223453789949417, -0.04678589850664139, 0.042259782552719116, 0.006026246584951878, -0.01794670894742012, -0.03347616270184517,