98341202735901, -0.027283908799290657, -0.034294165670871735, 2.4641353134522493e-34, 0.010457269847393036, -0.005266289226710796, 0.04331611096858978, -0.07746952027082443, -0.023216696456074715, -0.010369856841862202, 0.022224944084882736, 0.012600259855389595, 0.029979191720485687, 0.011998884379863739, -0.04279204457998276], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "de6be2136972b6d3f022daf1afc1d14472ff6795ac0e95134d4df8e0451de09d", "content": "This chunk is part of Case Study A: Watermore Primary School, which focuses on the inclusion strategies for Tom, an 8-year-old autistic pupil with significant anxiety. It illustrates how structured lunchtimes were implemented to help Tom engage socially and manage his anxiety in a supportive environment, emphasizing collaboration with his parents.Tom has structured lunchtimes following a 20:20:20 model (20 minutes lunch, 20 minutes free time, 20 minutes social time). He chooses a friend to have lunch with and sits away from the crowds.\fGood Autism Practice Guide \u2013 Case Studies\n5 Doc ref: GAP report - case studies_I-S\nPositive and effective relationships\nP3 \u2013 Collaboration with parents and carers of autistic children and young people\nSupporting Tom at school involved regular meetings, telephone calls and emails with his parents. His parents were partners in the process, suggesting ideas to help Tom and using ideas from school at home ( e.g. the sensory calming strategies were used just before bedtime to reduce his anxiety and enable him to fall asleep more quickly).\n", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 4, "split_id": 15, "split_idx_start": 6184, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "414540591be969063b9d4ac593b9e28dccfdda85fb16ea0b9356ca80306c44c2", "range": [261, 384]}, {"doc_id": "f3c562729343c38b