-0.003938333597034216, -0.05696126073598862, 0.026039697229862213, -0.014716352336108685, 0.02035447768867016, -0.002279913518577814, 0.03387262299656868, 0.01699724607169628, -0.012041282840073109], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "ee067817b6f48968f8a6a9856faa82d90f2061032ffd9d691deb997859154a9e", "content": "This chunk is from Case Study A about Watermore Primary School, focusing on strategies implemented to support an autistic pupil, Tom. It describes how Tom\u2019s safe haven is designed to be calming and inviting, with sensory elements like Pok\u00e9mon decorations and weighted items to help him manage his anxiety during challenging situations.he had a board of Pok\u00e9mon cards on the wall). The room had a table and chairs and a tent. Tom can invite children into his space and will share it. Black curtains have been used to lower the light and staff hope to install dimmers in the future.\nIf Tom was finding a situation challenging, he was supported to go to the safe haven, given a weighted item, such as a lap pad, and enough time to calm. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 6, "split_id": 26, "split_idx_start": 10588, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "8d67a98809a28eee1428e0e41a414ae5e711aad55ed2890566935fb70fd63414", "range": [456, 502]}, {"doc_id": "bb37a21b7a53401310d3f4ad28b68a4a19fca1759a78f5794f2dd4235580ef2a", "range": [0, 153]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.045145854353904724, -0.12780676782131195, -0.027402319014072418, 0.023932773619890213, -0.04100717976689339, 0.03952872008085251, 0.045268621295690536, -0.019888605922460556, -0.07947608083486557, 0.007695589680224657, 0.037241131067276, -0.03366786986589432, 0.01626342535018921, -0.02017783932387829, -0.008616512641310692, -0.0747573971748352, 0.004465664736926556, 0.032