621189, 2.0709482947252814e-34, -0.015250395983457565, 0.027784070000052452, 0.03758829087018967, -0.03395457565784454, -0.021902361884713173, -0.007492428179830313, 0.04271591827273369, 0.01536780409514904, 0.013250909745693207, -0.016060085967183113, -0.03867271542549133], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "9f9398f2ede1037ff2e3654664c817aaf6a9573dae9a81fb2becd3c2de0d3f3a", "content": "This chunk is part of Case Study A: Watermore Primary School, which focuses on strategies to support an 8-year-old autistic pupil named Tom. It illustrates how personalized learning approaches, such as writing about his interests and using a \u2018pause\u2019 folder for difficult tasks, help Tom engage with the curriculum and develop social skills through group activities.Tom is given the option to write about Minecraft instead of Egypt.)\nHe has produced some wonderful stories with drawings on topic work.\nTom has been given a \u2018pause\u2019 folder so that if he is finding a piece of work difficult or he does not want to do it, it goes in the folder to be completed at another time or day. There is an expectation that the work will still be completed at some point.\nGroup work\nThe TA models and works with Tom on sharing, turn-taking, skipping, football and the beginnings of team games. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 7, "split_id": 31, "split_idx_start": 12232, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "329cf85aa9cb829166d82564135df9973af79b144fc47971696c0ca34615e77e", "range": [239, 307]}, {"doc_id": "71901f15ab1c9a609b5ef71acf14bc5537bc61947d31888f09e6b3623627517f", "range": [0, 122]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.027728930115699768, -0.03994585946202278, -0.03917550668120384, 0.011632098816335201, -0.015571226365864277, 0.011029953137040138, 0.072500728070