6619541049004, 0.01568952016532421, -0.03327934443950653, -0.01942465454339981, 0.007400184404104948, -0.0003649210266303271, 0.042011938989162445, 0.07927447557449341, 0.004575769882649183, 0.01759868487715721, 0.033179398626089096, 0.03807860240340233, 0.040273334830999374, -0.024582544341683388, 0.0028567640110850334, -0.044572535902261734, 0.018849119544029236, -0.015583104453980923, 0.041715383529663086, -0.034736424684524536, -0.06506706029176712, 0.014473979361355305, -0.02954951487481594, -0.022590765729546547, -0.03417395427823067, -0.04724351316690445, 0.02637064829468727, 1.94552121078262e-34, -0.02471178025007248, 0.012987842783331871, 0.02422448806464672, -0.032074861228466034, -0.003940470051020384, -0.018682438880205154, 0.016064541414380074, 0.02284184843301773, 0.05417513847351074, 0.02780255302786827, -0.03288498893380165], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "71901f15ab1c9a609b5ef71acf14bc5537bc61947d31888f09e6b3623627517f", "content": "This chunk is located within Case Study A: Watermore Primary School, focusing on strategies to support an autistic pupil named Tom. It highlights the collaborative approach of the teaching assistant (TA) in facilitating group work, addressing Tom's challenges with social interactions, and promoting skills such as sharing and teamwork through paired activities. The following section outlines transferable practices that can be implemented in similar settings to support autistic children.Group work\nThe TA models and works with Tom on sharing, turn-taking, skipping, football and the beginnings of team games. This is mostly paired work as Tom finds being part of a group of children challenging. Using a peer to model activities works well for him.\fGood Autism Practice Guide \u2013 Case Studies\n8 Doc ref: GAP report - case studies_I-S\nTransferable practice \u2013 actions you could try in your setting\nHere are some ideas you could try in your setting that worked for this child:\n\u2022 Create a safe space indoors for the child to use\n\u2022 Create a safe space outdoors\n\u2022 Give alternative topics for writing activities which capitalise on the child\u2019s interests\n\u2022 Assess the child\u2019s sensory issues by completing the AET Sensory Checklist with the staff and the child\u2019s parents\n\u2022 Use a scale to assess a child\u2019s emotional state and take appropriate actions\n\u2022 Give enough time for a pupil to calm with an activity they enjoy,( e.g. time on iPad, crafts, walk outside)\n\u2022 Ensure partnership working with parents as they are the experts on their child\n\u2022 Use a \u2018pause folder\u2019 with alternative activities. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 7, "split_id": 32, "split_idx_start": 12624, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "9f9398f2ede1037ff2e3654664c817aaf6a9573dae9a81fb2becd3c2de0d3f3a", "range": [392, 514]}, {"doc_id": "5dc10295a0321271a2041e3b826544e52dbe4feb7b60c069b7739658dfe2282b", "range": [0, 169]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.02977914549410343, -0.09412376582622528, -0.019022243097424507, 0.031414926052093506, -0.006455509457737207, 0.007418311666697264, 0.05701961740851402, -0.04129994660615921, -0.06487423926591873, 0.004586957860738039, 0.018957387655973434, -0.05661996081471443, 0.03351382538676262, 0.002764409175142646, -0.01836780644953251, -0.09197099506855011, 0.013609396293759346, 0.027201339602470398, -0.03634447976946831, 0.00704544922336936, 0.020790686830878258, 0.009393892250955105, 0.0009372492204420269, 0.035689827054739, 0.02445468306541443, -0.03740253672003746, 0.003019084921106696, -0.013694003224372864, 0.02305583842098713, -0.01774802803993225, 0.03981907665729523, 0.01749987155199051, -0.018598079681396484, 0.01219506748020649, 2.119966211466817e-06, 0.04285476729273796, 0.0274737