65038400888443, -0.032163314521312714, 0.07426288723945618, -0.04126238077878952, -0.03053206019103527, 0.022053442895412445, 0.014026809483766556, -0.008912256918847561, -0.03934729844331741, -0.008052281104028225, 0.024940479546785355, 1.5369517464491887e-34, -0.0010590270394459367, 0.0040872301906347275, 0.009837623685598373, -0.07251077145338058, 0.048050060868263245, -0.0018766578286886215, -0.07462697476148605, -0.007524434011429548, 0.011220637708902359, 0.003820714307948947, -0.030483011156320572], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "a7f0ce28a80ed9bcd00fd84cff20c19c7a5fd6b8543a2bfcbc759d112b37c542", "content": "This chunk is situated in the case study of Oasis Academy Connaught, a nursery and primary school in the South West of England. The focus is on understanding the needs of autistic children, developing targeted support, and adapting the curriculum to enhance wellbeing and success, particularly for those with speech and language challenges.Targeted support and measuring progress of children and young people on the autism spectrum\n8. Adapting the curriculum, teaching and learning to promote wellbeing and success for autistic children and young people\nContext\nThe academy has a small number of children diagnosed with autism but a larger number whom staff consider are autistic, but not diagnosed. The nursery caters for children aged between three and four years. There are a number of children who enter the school without being able to talk and so speech, language and communication are priorities for the academy. The academy is also in an area where there is high deprivation and staff run a food bank for the community. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 9, "split_id": 36, "split_idx_start": 16370, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "39