43008423, -0.03390633314847946, -0.014919417910277843], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "4d538d05fc95cbc967a5ff8c1b25d74c202053e2d05bc32e059d0abf162f5d14", "content": "This chunk is from Case Study B, which focuses on the strategies employed by Oasis Academy Connaught to support autistic children. It details the implementation of autism-based interventions such as Social Stories, visual timetables, and the Five Point scale, as well as the Time to Talk program to enhance communication and social skills. This information contributes to insights on workforce development and the understanding of special educational needs (SEND) within the school context.These include use of Social Stories (Gray, 2015), the use of visual timetables and the Five Point scale developed by Buron and Curtis (2003).\fGood Autism Practice Guide \u2013 Case Studies\n11 Doc ref: GAP report - case studies_I-S\nThe school operate an autism-based intervention with all children called Time to Talk (Shroeder, 2001) as well as Talk Boost (developed by The Communication Trust and I CAN, with support from the Every Child a Chance Trust). This is part of A Chance to Talk (ACTT) which is a social skills group intervention which focuses on things like turn-taking. There is a dedicated Learning Support Assistant for Speech and Language.\nPositive and effective relationships\nP4 \u2013 Workforce development to support autistic children and young people\nSenior leaders have an in-depth understanding of SEND and the needs of autistic children and this informs how practice develops and is embedded across the school.\n", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 10, "split_id": 42, "split_idx_start": 18991, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "ce84b2907fcecdb3b7f1d53335ab50392d8f6d6fdc7b9c470b886223b8