720618635416, -0.017689064145088196, 0.05294302850961685, -0.023923469707369804, -0.08086484670639038, 0.04054980352520943, 0.011306965723633766, 0.007432238664478064, -0.07172902673482895, -0.014152653515338898, -0.04639603570103645, 2.0785756918586833e-34, 0.01573590189218521, -0.06390847265720367, 0.017274880781769753, -0.08894247561693192, 0.010002671740949154, 0.01553456112742424, 0.004984193481504917, 0.0016790900845080614, 0.015406115911900997, -0.004955213982611895, -0.042812280356884], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "f1d519655401efa33f6c0e7477d3588340c5a3f93201c2736b90fcb703a2623c", "content": "This chunk is part of Case Study C: Hinderton Special School, which focuses on the implementation of interventions like visual supports to aid emotional regulation for autistic children. The use of character-based visuals from the film \"Inside Out\" is highlighted as a strategy to help students understand and discuss their emotions, alongside personal workstations equipped with 'now and next' signs to support learning and self-regulation.Visuals are displayed to encourage self-regulation by giving the children a sequence of actions to do when they are angry.\nCharacters are used to represent emotions so that these can be discussed with the children. These are based around the characters in the film Inside Out (Pixar, 2015) and are shown in visuals. Each emotion is personified by a different character which is thought to help children put a visual to an emotion.\nSeparate, personal work stations have been set up with \u2018now and next\u2019 signs. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 13, "split_id": 51, "split_idx_start": 23657, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "0a6be240d7f1f807e9da7335cfeb6ab408517e75d5e9e07afa5a17374004e6b6", "range": [371,