, 0.0457635261118412, 0.029686400666832924, 0.03955306485295296, -0.026962559670209885, 0.004496050998568535, -0.033869627863168716, 0.00853001419454813, -0.0023694918490946293, 0.019828150048851967, -0.04717195779085159, -0.06717809289693832, 0.006127673201262951, 0.008458126336336136, -0.029441410675644875, -0.09497113525867462, -0.011752153746783733, -0.018546203151345253, 1.8911318677717902e-34, 0.027934860438108444, -0.0254422165453434, 0.016957992687821388, -0.04408136382699013, -0.01356961764395237, -0.0027411722112447023, -0.00825128611177206, 0.007495047990232706, 0.04090738296508789, -0.027428224682807922, -0.009509201161563396], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "ea6c8605cd7243184ff45b389b79adf6c90dd7e9451a9f085a9c8c2ac92b5332", "content": "This chunk is part of the \"Good Autism Practice Guide \u2013 Case Studies\" document, specifically focusing on Case Study H regarding St. John the Baptist Church of England Primary School. It discusses strategies for adapting the curriculum and teaching methods in early years settings to enhance the learning and developmental outcomes for autistic children, emphasizing thematic planning and progressive expectations.Good Autism Practice Guide \u2013 Case Studies\n14 Doc ref: GAP report - case studies_I-S\nP8 \u2013 Adapting the curriculum, teaching and learning to promote wellbeing and success for autistic children and young people\nEarly years continuous provision is looked at and adapted on a daily basis and there are high expectations for progress. An activity will start the week at one level of expectation and build throughout the week to become more challenging. For example, a Maths activity that the children can choose may start with the numbers up to 5 and then move on to numbers up to 10 by the end of the week.\nThe Early Years curriculum has been adapted to have an emphasis on developing speech and vocabulary. Provision is planned linked to a theme ( e.g. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path