d further resources related to autism education practices. It lists key texts and materials that inform effective strategies for supporting autistic children and young people, alongside resources provided by the Autism Education Trust, aimed at enhancing staff competencies and parental involvement.References\nBuron, KD and Curtis, M (2012) The Incredible 5-Point Scale (2nd. Edition) AAPC publishing\nGray, C. (2015) The New Social Story Book , Arlington, Texas: Future Horizons\nGreenland, SK (2009) The mindful child; how to help your kid manage stress and become happier, kinder and more compassionate, New York: Simon and Schuster\fGood Autism Practice Guide \u2013 Case Studies\n16 Doc ref: GAP report - case studies_I-S\nFurther resources\nAutism Education Trust ( http://www.autismeducationtrust.org.uk/)\nNational Standards for Schools 5-16 which sets out key elements of good autism practice in settings for autistic children and young people aged 5 to 16 years\nCompetency Framework for Staff 5-16 which sets out knowledge and skills required for teaching staff working with autistic children and young people aged 5 to 16 years\nAET Parent/Carers Guide: What to look for in your child\u2019s school : a document for parents/\ncarers to complete identifying what they deem to be important for their autistic son or daughter \u2013 free to download from the AET website.\nAET Tools for Teachers\nAET Progression Framework \u2013 a means of recording progress specifically for autistic children and young people and for setting learning intentions.\nThe AET also has a number of autism training hubs in different parts of England which offer training on autism at different levels and for the different age phases ( i.e. Early Years, 5 to 16 and 16 to 25 years) \u2013 see the AET website ( http://www.autismeducationtrust.org.uk /) for details of the training hub closest to you\fGood Autism Practice Guide \u2013 Case Studies\n17 Doc ref: GAP report - case studies_I-S\nCase Study C: Hinderton Special School\nType of school Community special school for autistic students at all levels of ability in the North West of England\nSchool website hinderton.cheshire.sch.uk\nNumber on roll 44\nAge range 3 to 11 years\nFocus of the case study\nYoga, Forest schools and Outdoor Learning, Colourful Semantics\nPrinciples illustrated\n1. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 15, "split_id": 58, "split_idx_start": 27536, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "f7b3a9342542ab2d10ceaea89170f619d3c24ad5792f9dd3b011c9ee944dfe54", "range": [1087, 1164]}, {"doc_id": "fd29891c247676609af3101238a2419d7704553feac344a95dd113257941d9c4", "range": [0, 589]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.06034872308373451, -0.12540562450885773, -0.02236970141530037, 0.01735965721309185, -0.0012673925375565886, 0.059710320085287094, 0.04803970083594322, -0.03711568936705589, -0.045809660106897354, -0.015930140390992165, 0.04254084825515747, -0.056474778801202774, 0.039431676268577576, -0.026074813678860664, 0.015413188375532627, -0.05226801708340645, 0.034607961773872375, 0.04496295750141144, -0.03246211260557175, 0.008729886263608932, 0.017251502722501755, 0.0329187847673893, -0.019494015723466873, 0.04150338098406792, 0.03623002767562866, -0.0022162573877722025, 0.020568229258060455, 0.0009351480402983725, 0.022605573758482933, -0.09056928753852844, 0.03315088897943497, -0.029329318553209305, -0.01229600328952074, -0.03260767087340355, 2.35888774113846e-06, 0.014137293212115765, 0.007291814778000116, -0.0018080949084833264, -0.01677076518535614, -0.013262595050036907, -0.046224769204854965, 0.011451092548668385, 0.029786892235279083, 0.05464097112417221, 0.008327005431056023, -0.02168434113264084, 0.027580002322793007, 0.08801791816949844, -0.0015749192098155618, 0.01353740505