151128068566322, 0.060994233936071396, 0.0007305446779355407, -0.011421414092183113, -0.07375448197126389, -0.0036364728584885597, -0.019025424495339394, 1.678394596538693e-34, 0.008788783103227615, -0.007825333625078201, 0.03812364116311073, -0.09396440535783768, 0.0414295420050621, 0.0062129744328558445, -0.05090036243200302, -0.008359240368008614, -0.014424622058868408, -0.0396977923810482, -0.06251753121614456], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "645204b59a548f8f9d8eb3ee21ce5e117b95d9bbc2247d75a078fa8f6e39523c", "content": "This chunk is part of Case Study C, focusing on Hinderton Special School, which highlights the incorporation of yoga as a therapeutic intervention for autistic students. It discusses how physical and mindfulness elements are integrated into the school's timetable to support emotional regulation and reduce anxiety among the pupils.The physical and mindfulness elements of yoga are\fGood Autism Practice Guide \u2013 Case Studies\n18 Doc ref: GAP report - case studies_I-S\nincorporated into the timetable. The yoga teacher works for one day a week at the school and prioritises pupils where she feels she can have the most impact. The sessions last for 15 minutes with the older pupils and about 5 minutes with the younger pupils in Reception. The Yoga teacher has trialled different group sizes and found that working 1:1 and 1:2 works better than a larger group, as some children do not participate when in a larger group.\nSome basic resources are required. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 17, "split_id": 63, "split_idx_start": 30875, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "72457144ff72d5ec04072ba353adc95d2e1487eaf36392f0fca68207cb507ba4", "range": [333, 500]}, {"doc_id": "191268b36c2407fff51e5e35fe45d0e4985c9dfacc2fb5cadd6335fb96c9e