2455, -0.02236206829547882, -0.052618399262428284, 0.022463195025920868, 0.014338032342493534, 0.06357082724571228, -0.05476510524749756, -0.07807444781064987, 0.05362699553370476, -0.0008328317198902369, -0.0025621303357183933, -0.03870423883199692, -0.002544488525018096, -0.04334306716918945, 1.909295895762509e-34, -0.0008287360542453825, -0.008091852068901062, 0.03720754757523537, -0.10174810141324997, 0.02778887189924717, -0.014633175916969776, -0.004006523173302412, 0.023424219340085983, 0.04105960205197334, -0.02171168476343155, -0.06980136781930923], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "fc7aaa65a1f2002634812605d100d70004ff2a6aff563a0f4ec867859db0d95d", "content": "This chunk is part of Case Study C from Hinderton Special School, which explores the implementation of yoga as an intervention for autistic pupils. It discusses the yoga teacher's approach to adapting activities based on individual children's needs, emphasizing the calming effects of yoga on anxious autistic students and the importance of flexibility in teaching methods.She felt a visual timetable might be a barrier to activities and she needs to use a flexible structure that is not too prescriptive so that she can be responsive to the children\u2019s needs on that day. She also uses a very individualised approach with clear beginnings and ends to the session indicated in ways each child enjoys and understands.\nShe feels that the use of yoga to support children with autism works well because autistic children are often anxious and yoga is designed to work with the sympathetic nervous system. She believes autistic children just \u201cget it\u201d. It does not need to be made fun in the same way as is necessary for a neurotypical child. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 18, "