356, -0.005670018028467894, -0.015651576220989227, 1.5506903366744242e-34, 0.0036313505843281746, -0.005315143149346113, 0.05210161954164505, -0.09663863480091095, 0.041864603757858276, 0.0033802087418735027, -0.021556038409471512, 0.019483022391796112, 0.027442604303359985, -0.03064855933189392, -0.06389360874891281], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "75ea8787e42b8dbceea330b2ff33985fe5dae9482c19b4a6d0f9bae61965be6d", "content": "This chunk is located in Case Study C: Hinderton Special School, which details various interventions for autistic students, particularly focusing on the implementation of yoga as a calming strategy. It highlights the collaborative relationship between the yoga teacher and students, emphasizing the insights gained into individual needs and the emotional impact of yoga sessions on the children.This close involvement between the yoga teacher and the child has provided insights that staff would never have had into the child\u2019s needs ( e.g. one child does reverse breathing when he is very anxious). The yoga teacher checks to see how the pupil has coped with the yoga session emotionally and talks to the class teacher straight after the session to let them know what she has worked on. She writes up each session and videos sessions to share with the staff and parents.\fGood Autism Practice Guide \u2013 Case Studies\n19 Doc ref: GAP report - case studies_I-S\nAfter doing yoga for a while, the staff have found that children begin to use the techniques themselves, independently. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 18, "split_id": 70, "split_idx_start": 33787, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "858590ca48675ab5a117998840d86283ea30d2f23993f135c0322a14328d6d00", "range": [351, 497]}, {"doc_id": "b7bf5b222c72345435144a5034ad6f030