, 0.004721066914498806, -0.009837435558438301, 0.04268727824091911, -0.10443402826786041, 0.041885487735271454, 0.0015995486173778772, -0.02568671479821205, 0.0016081177163869143, 0.024558184668421745, -0.030515259131789207, -0.04854624718427658], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "dd44fed7c0eb740c210abd52a7e7c41068e7c18a0966064c5e277c77f4c4fbfe", "content": "This chunk is part of Case Study C: Hinderton Special School, which focuses on interventions for autistic pupils, highlighting the implementation of yoga and outdoor learning activities. Specifically, it discusses the positive effects of yoga on children, particularly those with complex needs, and introduces the concept of Forest Schools as an effective outdoor learning intervention.Yoga has been used with all the children and the yoga teacher feels all can benefit but some respond better than others. Teachers have observed the most impact with more complex children.\nForest schools and outdoor learning\nThis intervention arose from staff seeing mainstream primary pupils in a school next door participating in an assault course. School staff saw the potential this might have for their children. This was led by an external provider who delivered military-style outdoor activities. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 19, "split_id": 73, "split_idx_start": 35147, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "c1403e7ab0805fdb225469761dd594699c49e867e5eb480405accea747d7b2f6", "range": [459, 580]}, {"doc_id": "455ba04a478574a4721221c7060595d0d68f88f0b5c58987852b92689e49447d", "range": [0, 86]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.008551834151148796, -0.055756423622369766, -0.016258450224995613, 0.003564116545021534, 0.03488560765981674, -0.0016198661178350449, 0.01689780503511429, -0.026930812746286392, -0.02520