986207962, -0.05188766494393349, 0.054742712527513504, -0.02245446667075157, 0.052156463265419006, 0.06514869630336761, -0.016470149159431458, 0.011116232722997665, 0.028860455378890038, 0.027317136526107788, 0.04230128228664398, -0.007062315009534359, 0.005257484503090382, -0.04195920377969742, 0.05675911158323288, 0.0067473300732672215, 0.004963303916156292, -0.07699380069971085, -0.05310327932238579, 0.05772379785776138, -0.022109536454081535, 0.03429539129137993, -0.05000323802232742, -0.0063902344554662704, -0.03223971277475357, 1.458278228394931e-34, 0.01807854324579239, -0.013291348703205585, 0.012783238664269447, -0.12145426869392395, 0.023572664707899094, 0.003945938777178526, 0.0030488234478980303, -0.019763316959142685, 0.016948744654655457, -0.021992532536387444, -0.023011857643723488], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "455ba04a478574a4721221c7060595d0d68f88f0b5c58987852b92689e49447d", "content": "This chunk is part of Case Study C: Hinderton Special School, which discusses the implementation of an outdoor learning program. It highlights how an external provider, experienced in military-style activities, collaborated with school staff to adapt their approach, ensuring the needs of autistic students were met through flexible and differentiated practices.This was led by an external provider who delivered military-style outdoor activities. The providers were all ex-military, some having an educational background.\nSchool staff felt that this approach was a risk but has been one that has really paid off. The providers had a measured and clear approach and were willing to be flexible and invest their own time in planning. The providers operate appropriate differentiation, listening to what the school staff say and then changing their practice to accommodate the needs of the children. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_