5475, 0.01192338578402996, -0.00039534192183054984, 0.0307419802993536, 0.04012864828109741, -0.023639868944883347], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "1e141b341a07def87590fb1746290aa41609b2d209af453508709c43eff1b27f", "content": "This chunk is part of the case study for Hinderton Special School, which highlights their successful implementation of outdoor learning initiatives led by external providers. It discusses how these providers tailor their sessions to address specific issues faced by students, fostering positive outcomes in social interaction and learning throughout the primary age range.The providers are responsive to issues arising with the children in their planning. For example, one child had issues over fire safety at home so the providers presented a session around this.\nThe school invited the team in for a free trial and this resulted in very positive feedback from staff through staff questionnaires. The team works with all the children from Reception through to Year 6 and uses a space outside, designated as the outdoor learning classroom. The children are organised into three groups with similar levels of social interaction and need.\n", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 19, "split_id": 76, "split_idx_start": 36472, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "ff947555c8d58b09e1645046d0ec08a8773123a1d5ea31a25451b666c461e0d9", "range": [537, 621]}, {"doc_id": "07499a552889e59e0a21ca8fe0a025a42caa019ace37ab48165db42272106d69", "range": [0, 97]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [-0.0029442445375025272, -0.05798724293708801, -0.008206558413803577, 0.01232824195176363, 0.021377671509981155, -0.006987748201936483, 0.013489394448697567, -0.07182786613702774, 0.011112880893051624, 0.014076432213187218, 0.0015764960553497076, 0.038241300731897354, 0.