5323, -0.017415763810276985, -0.009354888461530209], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "de44f508b11eb466dd4a3ed24b0730a41cea161325cb8a0621194fb118c46341", "content": "This chunk is part of the case study for Hinderton Special School, which explores the implementation of outdoor learning activities designed to engage autistic pupils. It highlights the use of creative resources and the importance of partnerships with external providers to enhance learning experiences and foster positive relationships.Providers started with a taster session which was based on a story Little People and the Dragons. Resources can be simple such as coloured threads to make a fire, big canopies, parachutes and hammocks. They used new materials that the children had not worked with before and that engaged the children\u2019s interest. One member of staff commented that \u201call of a sudden it became exciting to sit on a piece of tarpaulin\u201d.\nThe school felt that relationships with the provider and their flexibility have been key to making the project a success and a real partnership has developed. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 20, "split_id": 78, "split_idx_start": 38044, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "07499a552889e59e0a21ca8fe0a025a42caa019ace37ab48165db42272106d69", "range": [1104, 1202]}, {"doc_id": "7cf053a4034140733bd409d419cdbc50703abda4cf76909b9175a6b3714c3712", "range": [0, 159]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.015068236738443375, -0.056455522775650024, -0.02149130217730999, 0.013180878013372421, 0.004027176182717085, -0.0065642367117106915, -0.0050009810365736485, -0.05355861410498619, -0.045241255313158035, -0.009660627692937851, -0.015884049236774445, 0.014736035838723183, 0.04456058889627457, 0.017549997195601463, -0.017143787816166878, -0.