642393052578, 0.00607438525184989, 0.02568628452718258, -0.029938852414488792, 0.0021631254348903894, -0.007070159539580345, 0.02515897899866104, -0.044048164039850235, 0.02213606983423233, -0.039167776703834534, -0.061788491904735565, 0.03737290948629379, -0.011755545623600483, 0.01758520118892193, -0.06662225723266602, -0.032535407692193985, -0.0002700166660360992, 1.8443095400556384e-34, 0.005656764376908541, -0.026731064543128014, -0.005341282580047846, -0.06160468980669975, 0.0351293683052063, -0.015409794636070728, 0.03823505714535713, -0.025806764140725136, 0.04968521371483803, -0.006882033310830593, -0.003171039978042245], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "7cf053a4034140733bd409d419cdbc50703abda4cf76909b9175a6b3714c3712", "content": "This excerpt is from the case study on Hinderton Special School, which discusses the implementation of outdoor learning initiatives. It highlights the importance of collaboration between the school and external providers in developing effective programs that cater to the specific needs of autistic students and foster partnerships with other schools for enhanced educational outcomes.The school felt that relationships with the provider and their flexibility have been key to making the project a success and a real partnership has developed. The project required a large initial investment of time with providers although they now adapt the activities themselves on the strength of knowing the individual pupils. There have been additional meetings with the providers to develop further links with the PE curriculum.\nThis work has also benefited from joint working with other schools ( e.g. a joint curriculum project with all the local mainstream schools linked to a topic on habitats).\n", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page