505394898355007, -0.006290023680776358, -0.01553389336913824, -0.041608776897192, -0.015624498948454857, -0.01192806102335453, 2.2484954665687085e-34, 0.005256691016256809, 0.0033941506408154964, 0.036671265959739685, -0.09909364581108093, 0.02035069279372692, -0.0014206471387296915, 0.09403199702501297, -0.034406911581754684, 0.03128606081008911, -0.007251210045069456, -0.011473593302071095], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "f4ff33c8722d05c313fb8f5a0a5a3af8d9dce98f0422b1c0a6601bc62448af8c", "content": "This chunk is part of Case Study C: Hinderton Special School, which focuses on innovative teaching practices for autistic students. It highlights the use of collaborative storytelling and presentations to enhance communication skills and engagement among pupils, demonstrating the school's commitment to fostering creativity and confidence in their students.He worked with a staff member to make a slide show and chose pictures and wrote a commentary on a whiteboard first. He presented this to the whole school in assembly. This has also been used to do a class retelling of a story. This class wrote a story together called The not so Haunted House. This had a great impact and one child went home and was able to retell the story word-for-word to a parent. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 21, "split_id": 88, "split_idx_start": 42723, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "202b7505ac12fc10c6e8ee1ab80cbda5e2141abe971784f564509003d2115754", "range": [375, 491]}, {"doc_id": "f5171bbaec4c09ab8db6e2c001247bdd39be7331b526f38d93f152a30a679cda", "range": [0, 108]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.006839293986558914, -0.06159442663192749, -0.01627236232161522, 0.0014581753639504313, -0.04795665666460991, 0.01761556789278984, 0.03857000917196274, -0.043587233871