9, 0.0007501684594899416, -0.030745845288038254, -0.011881052516400814, 0.027640700340270996, 0.039355698972940445, -0.027675924822688103, -0.038920626044273376], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "2eb94ee58d283f8753fc62610ed88a6cf99cd95d9e7a07104abcae4572fa30b1", "content": "This chunk pertains to effective teaching strategies for engaging autistic students. It emphasizes the importance of routine, anticipation, and structured activities in maintaining attention and fostering group participation. This approach is part of the broader discussion on adapting the curriculum and learning methods to enhance the well-being and success of autistic children, as illustrated in Case Study C regarding outdoor learning.\u2022 Using repetition of a routine and anticipation of their turn helps to maintain the pupils\u2019 attention.\n\u2022 There is a clear start, middle and end to each activity.\nThis way of working develops over time from an observed activity led by an adult, with involvement of the children who wish to become involved, to a demonstration of an activity with a partner which the children then do; and working towards small group engagement.\fGood Autism Practice Guide \u2013 Case Studies\n24 Doc ref: GAP report - case studies_I-S\nP8 \u2013 Adapting the curriculum, teaching and learning to promote wellbeing and success for autistic children and young people\nThe impact of outdoor learning by an external provider has resulted in this being extended into teaching across the curriculum. A visual timetable for the outdoor lesson is written on a mini whiteboard.\n", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 23, "split_id": 96, "split_idx_start": 46999, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "d1b6fcec39845e92722950b0d212bed8758ae5bd2b4e7b9732993e33d290c5bd", "ra