, 0.05817783251404762, 0.024660751223564148, 0.01158843096345663], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "54b2f6083456fc72f40a25d0be6aa336740a5f7bfd29edc53509bf30a98a34d6", "content": "This chunk is from Case Study C, Hinderton Special School, which focuses on the use of outdoor learning and engaging students with autism through hands-on activities. It highlights how a teacher used a visual and interactive method, involving large clock faces and sticks, to teach children how to tell time, fostering familiarity, comfort, and understanding in the learning process.The children seemed used to the game and were comfortable with it. Repetitive activities such as this provide familiarity and security and develop understanding.\n\u2022 The teacher demonstrated telling the time to the group using a big clock face drawn on the playground and using sticks (big and little) to mark the hands.\n\u2022 The children then had their own clock face drawn inside hoops on the playground to do their own questions. Children were supported to move the sticks or they did this independently according to need. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 24, "split_id": 99, "split_idx_start": 48568, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "da5616ada657d0c4c8443047a2f65b6ddee4107da22a06b6f204c60b97f650ff", "range": [394, 461]}, {"doc_id": "1db8b66cb495d74f6ff1ba8ede6e5ac57952f555e3060b88e3593bb288702b2b", "range": [0, 93]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.022880565375089645, -0.13664978742599487, -0.039946578443050385, 0.04559282213449478, -0.044312361627817154, 0.030479738488793373, 0.07926823943853378, -0.03255618363618851, -0.03839394822716713, 0.021606942638754845, 0.03488602116703987, -0.013238568790256977, 0.06331321597099304, -0.03216319531202316, -0.0348377525806427, -0.090183600783