", "content": "This chunk is from Case Study C: Hinderton Special School, which discusses the environment and strategies used to support autistic students with various needs. It highlights the school's approach to allowing students to self-regulate by moving away when dysregulated, while also emphasizing a supportive and understanding staff response to facilitate their reintegration into activities.Children who were dysregulated for whatever reason, were allowed to move away without comment. Staff moved alongside the pupil without judgment, used reduced language and then gradually supported the student to return to the group.\fGood Autism Practice Guide \u2013 Case Studies\n25 Doc ref: GAP report - case studies_I-S\nThe outside space was organised into areas such as gardening, Forest school, playground, enclosed activity area, climbing, and sensory equipment. Playground rules were displayed which were simple and easy to remember ( e.g. kind hands, kind words, kind feet).\n", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 24, "split_id": 102, "split_idx_start": 49903, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "a238bdcf405614ec6fb08a291ec17ddfe6175513e93e6cc2bc68bef2e89eb1c8", "range": [450, 545]}, {"doc_id": "6638dcb1e356efc6f48804bba41214d79281b780151723f3ed26a357d3f286e2", "range": [0, 36]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.053111378103494644, -0.1107536181807518, -0.02150799334049225, 0.004828684031963348, -0.013074420392513275, 0.011560320854187012, 0.053004950284957886, -0.04364883899688721, -0.040271058678627014, -0.005027359817177057, 0.03259947896003723, -0.03596736490726471, 0.013080312870442867, -0.012989898212254047, -0.006970935966819525, -0.06644716113805771, 0.029651211574673653, 0.008329028263688087, -0.08578681200742722, 0.014614282175898552, -0.022704