3, -0.028689570724964142, 0.010055775754153728, -0.025610359385609627, 0.05136697366833687, -0.007038547191768885, -0.05021795630455017, 0.0330309234559536, 0.015792492777109146, 0.02083158679306507, -0.04539022967219353, -0.029184959828853607, -0.016569674015045166, 1.8048232613613273e-34, 0.011794177815318108, -0.013220785185694695, 0.03309677913784981, -0.10153546929359436, 0.005967946723103523, -0.01674201898276806, 0.020116649568080902, 0.012568463571369648, -0.016487183049321175, 0.007851611822843552, -0.0036802138201892376], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "6638dcb1e356efc6f48804bba41214d79281b780151723f3ed26a357d3f286e2", "content": "This chunk is found within Case Study C: Hinderton Special School, which discusses various interventions and strategies used to support autistic pupils. Specifically, it highlights the implementation of Colourful Semantics by a speech and language therapist to enhance students' writing skills and vocabulary development, showcasing effective practices in autism education.kind hands, kind words, kind feet).\nColourful Semantics ( http://www.integratedtreatments.co.uk )\nThe school uses colourful semantics which some mainstream schools also use. The speech and language therapist delivered a training session two years ago on this. One teacher has adapted this approach to use with her class to develop writing and this has had a massive impact and been a turning point for their success at written work. She uses it to provide a bank of vocabulary as examples to extend the children\u2019s vocabulary as well as to develop grammatical structure and sentences. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 25, "split_id": 103, "split_idx_start": 50443, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "02e0f674100bf06c38e478ab73d08fbe297a3aba2