576972335577011, 0.014161471277475357, 0.01587625779211521, 1.4200362058791905e-34, 0.00479886494576931, 0.004118311684578657, 0.010843728668987751, -0.08304709941148758, 0.03369680047035217, -0.007562427315860987, -0.07478585839271545, -0.013891763053834438, 0.014124245382845402, 0.003537986893206835, -0.03882133588194847], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "806ed6098ea50a09f2a6d41f98e60733f0202b2625730c45f345a5dd8b020fe9", "content": "This chunk is part of Case Study D: The Garden School, which focuses on the tailored educational strategies for autistic students, emphasizing the importance of targeted support, individualized teaching, and curriculum adaptations to foster progress and well-being for children on the autism spectrum.Targeted support and measuring progress of children and young people on the autism spectrum\n8. Adapting the curriculum, teaching and learning to promote wellbeing and success for autistic children and young people\nContext\nThe criteria for a place at the school are to have a diagnosis of autism and to be at Level P4 or above. The majority of students have severe learning difficulties (SLD) and a few students have moderate learning difficulties (MLD). There are 7 to 8 students per class, with 5 Teaching Assistants (TAs) in the primary classes and 4 TAs in the secondary classes.\fGood Autism Practice Guide \u2013 Case Studies\n28 Doc ref: GAP report - case studies_I-S\nActions taken and impact\nThe school moved to its own newly designed site and the staff had a great deal of input into the design to make it a low arousal setting. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 27, "split_id": 109, "split_idx_start": 55295, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "c529b5cf5409a00e27930ea2cc4f3378761c44cc97ae752092aaadc184c1a714", "range