573622763157], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "95c337e2f5ed4eaf5a5001ec4ed6666d7fdc7544b9c1758c16a5dff0de25086e", "content": "This chunk is located in the case study for Hinderton Special School, which details the use of the SCERTS model to support students with autism. It explains the components of SCERTS\u2014social communication, emotional regulation, and transactional support\u2014and discusses how staff assess students to implement this approach effectively., 2006). Students are social partners (functional users of a picture or symbol system), language partners or conversation partners.\nSCERTS stands for:\n\u2022 SC \u2013 social communication \u2013 the development of spontaneous, functional communication, emotional expression and secure and trusting relationships between children and adults\n\u2022 ER \u2013 emotional regulation \u2013 the development of the ability to maintain a well-regulated emotional state to cope with everyday stress and to be most available for learning and interacting\n\u2022 TS \u2013 transactional support \u2013 to help staff respond to the child\u2019s needs and interests, modify the environment and provide tools to enhance learning ( e.g. visuals, sensory support).\nTo start out on the approach, the staff make a full assessment of each student which takes four days per student. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 28, "split_id": 111, "split_idx_start": 56679, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "b6bbb21c27454a873dcf4a5f992735e7f3b9119d692ea3fab1502a62294ac6b7", "range": [801, 810]}, {"doc_id": "faf7c5e7fd9488802cec5a3e9194a71ef8f54cf4314278ce7b7b05d2f5d12d3e", "range": [0, 114]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.025578003376722336, -0.13909374177455902, -0.02163803204894066, 0.0065114982426166534, -0.02325933