3057208061218, -0.03308939188718796, 0.026893341913819313, -0.04686366021633148, -0.011231022886931896, 0.007945324294269085, 0.022453926503658295, 0.021521728485822678, 0.023254500702023506, 0.011216184124350548, 0.0067238942719995975], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "a897397bfbbc775baf37dc3fd1ab8ff8ade2cd16930e20270c2490dd6e198b95", "content": "This chunk is part of Case Study C: Hinderton Special School, which focuses on various strategies employed to support autistic students, particularly in communication and emotional regulation. It highlights the use of Augmented Aids for Communication, such as iPads, and techniques for helping students recognize and manage their emotional states through tools associated with the Zones of Regulation framework.Others can make a sentence using symbols to express this. Some students are working on the tools they can use to move back into the green zone when they are in amber or red.\nThe school uses Augmented Aids for Communication ( e.g. a mini iPad provides visuals of key vocabulary). Software such as Clicker 7 has been used and the staff find that students are motivated to create longer sentences when using the iPad than when using PECS.\f", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 28, "split_id": 114, "split_idx_start": 58491, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "bbd927b6f95d216d3a4093fe4890e2b45f4b62991bd4ce151a18858ecbfca4dd", "range": [560, 618]}, {"doc_id": "f39ae499b431d3c709279468f32218b336698fc48cc35de814b60b5f76472cdc", "range": [0, 157]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.05799344927072525, -0.08056328445672989, -0.03290705010294914, -0.04571450129151344, -0.024114549160003662, 0.008910185657441616, 0.0662330836057663, -0.021853243932127953, -0.005873134359717369, -0.02542843297123909, 0.016166239976882