63837818056345, 0.01927774026989937, 0.016144633293151855, -0.0002991005021613091, -0.06299247592687607], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "797ea221f6db02906baac26f1cc0807f18446740472842d146b6b3f0b9064069", "content": "This chunk is located in Case Study C, which discusses the approaches used at Hinderton Special School to support autistic students. It highlights the use of visual aids, such as a green and red board for activity transitions, and emphasizes the importance of prevention strategies over physical interventions in managing behavior effectively.A green and red board shows the steps in activities, where the steps start off on the green side of the board and, as these are completed, the students move them to the red side.\nAudible cues as well as visual cues are used to signal transition from one activity to the next.\nPrevention of challenge is key, and restricted physical intervention is rare. If required, staff use Approach created by Bill Thorpe. This is based on the natural movement of the body. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 29, "split_id": 118, "split_idx_start": 59756, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "7c775ba7fa1300c64db702205c681e9e752bae6d837ebe56c73793e387487959", "range": [296, 475]}, {"doc_id": "0caaffb6b233ca05ab6e8f0973a8e5fad3cb2b9b9afc05bdad2bddb71ec3e72b", "range": [0, 51]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.04563097655773163, -0.07908312976360321, -0.0142467450350523, -0.0035340571776032448, -0.015642564743757248, 0.015564629808068275, 0.04427177831530571, -0.028921663761138916, -0.03958236053586006, -0.005568735767155886, 0.030734125524759293, -0.028705017641186714, -0.020072130486369133, 0.01473750825971365, -0.017637981101870537, -0.06995467096567154, 0.013996463268995285, -0.0013884262880310416, -0.05123055