6266193985939, 0.006826112512499094, 0.004145234823226929, -0.016328848898410797, 0.004584119655191898, -0.008377015590667725, 0.06351765990257263, -0.0026642405427992344, -0.04631655663251877, 0.04514209181070328, 0.012973062694072723, 0.005653069820255041, -0.0743979662656784, -0.02573556825518608, -0.028339261189103127, 1.7550729884389183e-34, -0.0014801035868003964, -0.004100807476788759, 0.04538945108652115, -0.0846560150384903, 0.01968511752784252, -0.01778961718082428, 0.011890114285051823, 0.02563118003308773, 0.05060727149248123, -0.004501162096858025, -0.03489137440919876], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "0caaffb6b233ca05ab6e8f0973a8e5fad3cb2b9b9afc05bdad2bddb71ec3e72b", "content": "This chunk is situated within a case study of Hinderton Special School, which outlines various strategies to support autistic students, particularly focusing on enabling their voices in decision-making. It describes the school's implementation of an independent living flat for practical skill development, highlighting the importance of movement in supporting these students.This is based on the natural movement of the body. When supporting students, an element of movement is always involved.\fGood Autism Practice Guide \u2013 Case Studies\n30 Doc ref: GAP report - case studies_I-S\nP2 \u2013 Enabling the voice of the autistic child and young person to contribute to and influence decisions\nThe school has allocated a space within the building as an independent living flat. The flat has a kitchen/living room, bathroom and bedroom so that young people can develop their skills in household tasks. The occupational therapist (OT) consulted secondary students to ascertain what they wanted in the flat. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 29, "split_id": 119, 