013249164447188377, 0.031858161091804504, -0.02725851535797119, -0.03479975461959839, 0.008706407621502876, 0.028206463903188705, 1.6704704559583305e-34, -0.011427794583141804, 0.0014923226553946733, 0.024858402088284492, -0.042806416749954224, 0.03322560340166092, 0.0021070395596325397, -0.06608107686042786, -0.01741483062505722, 0.007827311754226685, -0.015552934259176254, -0.031833238899707794], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "41f3465c668965288c8d84eaf2068ce7a6d8cb5532d989df5bb24cae2a229e43", "content": "This chunk is part of Case Study F, which focuses on the Phoenix School's commitment to effective collaboration with parents through various training workshops. It highlights the role of the Family Support Worker in engaging with 'hard to reach' parents and emphasizes the school's dedication to workforce development to support autistic children and young people.They focus on topics that particularly link to life at home\nand share strategies used at school. The \u2018hard to reach\u2019 parents are approached by the Family Support Worker.\nP4 \u2013 Workforce development to support autistic children and young people\nThere is a real commitment to whole school training and to having a highly trained staff who then train staff across the school. For example, all staff have been trained in Attention Autism developed by Gina Davies. There is now an accredited trainer on the staff who continues to train new staff as they join the school. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 30, "split_id": 122, "split_idx_start": 61537, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "20b1a1a3249f493eef54b283dfbc603ddb74a1c2755bb7103f5a892ea14e7b1b", "range": [492, 589]}, {"doc_id": "f76adbbc48dd8bfd93d0bedaed6f470d9ac99f9de82e51e976dbc8f8de5ff5ad", "range": [0, 106]}]}, "sco