ts for autistic students. It describes the layout and features of outdoor and sensory spaces designed to support the emotional regulation and learning of pupils with autism.This is a fenced area with a gate leading to the playground which can be kept open or locked and provides a secure, outside area. Additional break-out spaces can be used flexibly as there are a variety of sizes, many of these with covered outside space.\fGood Autism Practice Guide \u2013 Case Studies\n32 Doc ref: GAP report - case studies_I-S\nThere is a connecting withdrawal room between each of the primary classrooms. There is a range of sensory rooms ( e.g. soft play with audio and visual stimuli, a light room with two Omi projectors and an outside space). ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 31, "split_id": 131, "split_idx_start": 65475, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "985bff0fd7f2898bcc49a1d0f85188a94c1e4c84ad4a67df412d8b58fa34be34", "range": [405, 535]}, {"doc_id": "375189edd770998b8da705fb33cdbaea1cd4c49730bea23ca6fb08e037542d30", "range": [0, 101]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.024159854277968407, -0.08851585537195206, -0.014830073341727257, 0.021361175924539566, -0.025027958676218987, -0.00857609137892723, 0.053829126060009, -0.06572900712490082, 0.0019411983666941524, 0.029521428048610687, -0.014724223874509335, -0.012958970852196217, 0.015970593318343163, 0.022624539211392403, -0.014529273845255375, -0.0827028900384903, -0.009896178729832172, 0.0033921045251190662, -0.08335987478494644, 0.03136983513832092, -0.04274938628077507, 0.01966261677443981, -0.03139955922961235, 0.024277813732624054, 0.0019527154508978128, -0.046010419726371765, -0.020058022812008858, -0.005547467153519392, 0.04999803751707077, -0.03378082811832428, 0.022064819931983948, -0.008854504