271889177383855, -0.06444934755563736, 1.662430602652809e-34, 0.013076218776404858, -0.0173246618360281, -0.0029125940054655075, -0.0720505341887474, 0.014349790289998055, -0.013194628059864044, -0.005410726647824049, -0.009310728870332241, 0.015394061803817749, 0.008756664581596851, 0.0003214316093362868], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "375189edd770998b8da705fb33cdbaea1cd4c49730bea23ca6fb08e037542d30", "content": "This chunk describes facilities at Hinderton Special School, a community special school for autistic students. It highlights various sensory resources such as a splash pool, light room, and soft play area designed to support sensory regulation, independence skills, and overall well-being for students with autism.soft play with audio and visual stimuli, a light room with two Omi projectors and an outside space). There is a splash pool that is used for sensory regulation and play. Classes have timetabled access to this space.\nThis is also used for students to work on independence skills. It has an adjoining changing area and shower where students need to get ready for swimming, look after their belongings and get dressed afterwards. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 32, "split_id": 132, "split_idx_start": 65932, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "fef4271085cd978d24a8e80f2805d53146a0a8ca069fe47b93e5277b6781ca18", "range": [457, 558]}, {"doc_id": "3dc4e181471f4c194bf2931b881fde04f209f61c2399c0072b48eb0e7228c969", "range": [0, 148]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.01587175950407982, -0.07342923432588577, -0.037657465785741806, -0.01590918004512787, -0.008324476890265942, 0.013934891670942307, 0.050040103495121, -0.04679546132683754, -0.015698177739977837, -0.0014262270415201783, -0.037676937878131866, -0.07159623503684998, 0.02547