School, which details the school's approaches to measuring progress for students with autism. It discusses the use of skills maps that outline learning paths, starting from basic tactile experiences to more complex writing tasks, as part of their curriculum and assessment frameworks.These form the scheme of work and programmes of study are incorporated into these. Skills map examples include things like a \u2018using my hands\u2019 skills map. This starts off with touching textures and builds in a progression all the way up to writing. Two examples are given.\fGood Autism Practice Guide \u2013 Case Studies\n33 Doc ref: GAP report - case studies_I-S\nFor the assessment of progress, the school is still using P levels at the moment. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 32, "split_id": 135, "split_idx_start": 67038, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "0d5ae2ab54cd57c62014ec6040162177e48d0433d19b6f259a2a7d7001593201", "range": [422, 505]}, {"doc_id": "6142649e8f78d6d4ae3c88a59f91817ee02803bdc9f84051f8b2bc8ea3810579", "range": [0, 166]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [-0.00011632827954599634, -0.0954698994755745, -0.03403008356690407, 0.00299269356764853, -0.03809305280447006, 0.020774390548467636, 0.02698289044201374, -0.040735144168138504, -0.012685525231063366, 0.014619565568864346, 0.044882066547870636, -0.04541898891329765, 0.060357388108968735, -0.003020130330696702, -0.010836959816515446, -0.07015657424926758, -0.012793156318366528, 0.01136494055390358, -0.11047430336475372, 0.04096364229917526, -0.0186475720256567, -0.0009575081639923155, -0.042503926903009415, 0.07454423606395721, -0.028360065072774887, -0.03070618212223053, -0.01057454850524664, -0.0287186112254858, 0.00860457681119442, -0.08607576787471771, -0.023866159841418266, 0.0135314948