9251195043325424, -0.05207882821559906, 1.9319649140172582e-34, -0.033843252807855606, -0.0225618127733469, 0.029132677242159843, -0.09551029652357101, -0.023472754284739494, 0.007167123258113861, 0.056093908846378326, 0.015380204655230045, -0.015057278797030449, -0.03513805940747261, -0.046295229345560074], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "6142649e8f78d6d4ae3c88a59f91817ee02803bdc9f84051f8b2bc8ea3810579", "content": "This chunk is located in Case Study F (Phoenix School) within the document, detailing the school's assessment practices for measuring progress in students with autism and special educational needs (SEN). It discusses the use of P levels, B Squared software, and the SCERTS framework for evaluating student learning and communication abilities.Good Autism Practice Guide \u2013 Case Studies\n33 Doc ref: GAP report - case studies_I-S\nFor the assessment of progress, the school is still using P levels at the moment. Staff carry out a baseline assessment on the student\u2019s arrival using the B Squared software ( www.bsquared.co.uk) to evidence and assess learning for students with SEN. This is updated twice a year in January and June to see how much progress there has been and whether the student is making the required progress according to CASPA (Comparison and Analysis of Special Pupil Attainment) ( www.caspaonline.co.uk ) (a simple software benchmarking tool for the analysis and evaluation of attainment and process for SEN students who are working significantly below age-related expectations).\nThe staff carry out a Determining Communication assessment as part of the SCERTS framework. Initially, staff used to complete the whole of the SCERTS assessment but have now created their own, shorter version. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_