 0.04700467735528946, 0.036283913999795914, 0.007188965566456318, -0.00582845089957118, -0.014251172542572021, -0.033593084663152695, -0.0362885445356369, -0.014265908859670162, -0.015794839709997177, 2.0168594833756285e-34, -0.011561166495084763, -0.018552135676145554, 0.02563994936645031, -0.08631867170333862, -0.024600468575954437, -0.003749867668375373, -0.009189867414534092, -0.0290507934987545, 0.0512126199901104, -0.02321723662316799, -0.033517464995384216], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "7a13f0746c35be7c420d5db946112056edb52f8e802b45117ed4b7a3eead8741", "content": "This chunk is located within Case Study D: The Garden School, which focuses on adapting the curriculum and teaching methods to support the wellbeing and success of autistic children. It discusses the use of the COMFOR assessment to establish effective communication strategies and set targets for students, emphasizing the school's commitment to tailored educational approaches.The targets need to be approved by managers.\nThe school uses \u2018COMFOR\u2019 which stands for \u2018forerunners in communication\u2019 and was developed in the Netherlands by Ina Van Berckalonnes and colleagues (Noens et al ., 2006). This assessment is used to develop the best communication and transactional systems for the student and to generate targets.\fGood Autism Practice Guide \u2013 Case Studies\n34 Doc ref: GAP report - case studies_I-S\nP8 \u2013 Adapting the curriculum, teaching and learning to promote wellbeing and success for autistic children and young people\nThe school initially operated a subject-based curriculum. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 33, "split_id": 139, "split_idx_start": 69095, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "2302ba987d216c8aad91aa5db507e6672750523a255c79008015