8417133241891861, 0.004135895520448685, -0.03572039306163788, 2.2000628034274263e-34, 0.020602723583579063, -0.0689193606376648, 0.04609590023756027, -0.08479917794466019, -0.007175844628363848, -0.019094327464699745, 0.053895797580480576, 0.005306251812726259, -0.0012313995976001024, -0.020598001778125763, -0.042107123881578445], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "b7799b938a868178e5e16fef89cd592738dacb6ebce2b06e5766ad042e049e92", "content": "This chunk is part of the case study from Hinderton Special School, focusing on the curriculum adaptations made for autistic students. It highlights the different curricular strands, specifically the \"leaves curriculum,\" which emphasizes exploration, play, communication, and the development of functional skills, aiming to enhance students' independence and real-life applications.As well as learning through exploration and play, this provides the opportunity for communication, student voice and the development of functional skills.\nThe leaves curriculum is described as a more formal curriculum for learners from P8 \u2013 KS1. It provides students with opportunities to develop their knowledge and skills within a wider range of purposeful contexts, linked to the National Curriculum, where appropriate.\nThe emphasis is on creating a curriculum which includes purposeful, meaningful activities that have application in real life and helps to build independence and skills. The photograph shows the classroom set up to make toast.\n", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 34, "split_id": 142, "split_idx_start": 70376, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "905adba68f20ca67b0c787a56e2267b6b30bbefd1b6ceaa36cfeff82336e12d1", "range": [436, 591]}, {"doc_id": "0a961f54d44936c0ceba50102ea214f3dd704b650e4fb30c74e772f90197cb00", "