059195298701524734, -0.005723120179027319, 0.009373292326927185, -0.017308954149484634, -0.025012033060193062, 0.0412294827401638, -0.06407257914543152, 1.8382163696799259e-34, 0.02653065137565136, 0.025056570768356323, 0.024758782237768173, -0.07281816750764847, 0.007942913100123405, -0.00690062390640378, -0.021020861342549324, -0.004508917685598135, 0.012416830286383629, 0.028699688613414764, -0.045518141239881516], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "62ce0bee53b87624b329a436d215b04b7f2f6c6346d63598477f85f99b350adb", "content": "This chunk is part of Case Study F: Phoenix School, which focuses on vocational pathways and promoting independence for autistic students. It highlights the practical food preparation activities for secondary students in the food technology area and their involvement in running a school caf\u00e9, emphasizing experiential learning and collaboration between different year groups.Visuals are very evident in the cafe.\nSecondary students work in the food technology area to prepare and make the food, such as soup and croutons. They then transport this down to the caf\u00e9 on trolleys. The students do the clearing away and washing up. The secondary students make snacks for the primary students and have a special uniform they wear to deliver these snacks.\f", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 34, "split_id": 146, "split_idx_start": 72079, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "8eb8b6c59db7a5c2116b178bbf85070659ffbe04bda3c0c204ddda27f3e052db", "range": [516, 554]}, {"doc_id": "e0ff3a481fdb570f2ccfec06b57e390ebf29c0c7af07d3c6404b249f24610c15", "range": [0, 122]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.02175336889922619, -0.04940808191895485, -0.015065120533108711, 0.0005781359504908323, 0.01954403519630432, 0.028504353016614914, 0.027797