0.07060081511735916, 0.004774306435137987, -0.04116334766149521, -0.019497936591506004, 0.03726310655474663, 0.0001617460511624813, -0.003508561057969928, -0.020097268745303154, -0.014909263700246811, -0.010310495272278786, -0.024781808257102966, 0.01168661005795002, 0.03915141150355339, 0.005882123950868845, 0.01499489787966013, -0.057445965707302094, -0.0291393231600523, 0.04867677390575409, 0.032145775854587555, -0.017944928258657455, 0.01004849374294281, -0.03652820363640785, -0.007442408241331577, -0.04810408130288124, -0.004280839115381241, -0.0020676718559116125, -0.03702675178647041, 0.00641721673309803, -0.0353255420923233, 0.11512473970651627, -0.011256992816925049, 0.028406143188476562, -0.03687402606010437, -0.047666165977716446, 0.04902546480298042, -0.01856209710240364, 0.03956826403737068, 0.05581865459680557, -0.016368655487895012, 0.008057927712798119, -0.0009754790225997567, 0.050726961344480515, 0.026643143966794014, -0.022482017055153847, -0.00861000269651413, -0.049285128712654114, 0.02638251893222332, -0.020088044926524162, 0.06849581003189087, -0.018741775304079056, -0.054676353931427, -0.007842153310775757, 0.02770962566137314, -0.020992016419768333, -0.02079133689403534, -0.014496698044240475, -0.06022805720567703, 2.4228180645532882e-34, 0.03482266888022423, -0.008748662658035755, 0.05626766383647919, -0.050866492092609406, 0.004305865149945021, -0.013571426272392273, -0.050350435078144073, 0.0009629246778786182, 0.0025822969619184732, 0.0139313330873847, -0.06267740577459335], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "25394af0ba027a41125031c480940fb3e4e5de980bd9292557bcf096148ceb20", "content": "This chunk is part of the transferable practices outlined in various case studies on effective autism support within educational settings. It emphasizes actionable strategies for educators, such as utilizing technology for communication, personalizing learning resources, and engaging students with their interests to enhance their educational experience and skill development.To complement the curriculum, the students visit galleries and work with a visiting artist.\nTransferable practice \u2013 actions you could try in your setting\n\u2022 Use Clicker on an iPad to support communication and to support writing for some students\n\u2022 Use software to gather evidence of learning which can be shared with parents\n\u2022 Commit to whole school/staff training with a focused induction programme for new staff and an ongoing training programme\n\u2022 Display the students\u2019 profiles in every class.\n\u2022 Use personalised visual timetables in every class showing a progression through objects of reference, to symbols, to words to student created\n\u2022 Use written within-task schedules to show items to do on the green (left) side which are moved over to the red (right) side when completed\n\u2022 Use of skills maps to outline the progression in the development of skills\n\u2022 Use students\u2019 interests as motivational tools\n\u2022 Use SCERTS framework to measure students\u2019 non-academic progress\n\u2022 Focus on work-related learning tasks incorporating real life situations into the school environment\n\u2022 Incorporate real-world structured tasks within the curriculum to build skills in these areas\n\u2022 Use visiting experts to supplement curriculum experiences, ( e.g. visiting artist; drama group).\nReferences\nBondy, A and Frost, L (2002) The Picture Exchange Communication System Training Manual , Second Edition, Pyramid Consulting\nCaldwell, P and Horwood, J (2008) Using intensive interaction and sensory integration , London: Jessica Kingsley\nDavies, G (2013) Attention Autism (www.ginadavies.com)\nKuyper, L (2011) The zones of regulation , California: Social Thinking\nHewett, D (2018) The intensive interaction handbook (2nd. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id":