ethos on inclusion for all students, particularly those with autism. It highlights the positive experiences of Max, a Year 10 autistic student, emphasizing proactive staff support, school policies on bullying, and the use of a five-point scale for behavior management.He said, \u201c If staff see I am upset, they will ask me what\u2019s wrong and what\u2019s making you feel depressed \u2013 or say, Can I do anything to help you?\u201d\nMax\u2019s form tutor is very proactive and inclusive and visited him at primary school before he started at Priestnall. He had visits to his new school and Social Stories were created for him. He was given a book with photos of key staff before he started.\n\u201c If bullying is reported, staff will always act and there is a 5-point scale where 1 is a warning \u2013 2 is \u20263 is\u2026\u201d Max knew this scale very well. There is a way of students being able to report bullying anonymously (a button on school website) and the student will receive a reply from a member of staff. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 38, "split_id": 155, "split_idx_start": 79232, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "93b9effb0d686723308ed7370c653717792c08f290a4e37ca4b69d9273129297", "range": [809, 1070]}, {"doc_id": "e69b1683a0de7f0bef88cf940dbfbc686e4b282b7f26fb9764f29a3ed05492e2", "range": [0, 159]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.06084554269909859, -0.07661866396665573, -0.0001435530575690791, 0.024383006617426872, 0.0265196580439806, 0.04342806711792946, 0.05195299908518791, -0.05412023514509201, -0.0007995018968358636, -0.012772474437952042, 0.054978471249341965, -0.04125664755702019, -0.006661653984338045, 0.010350575670599937, 0.006795473862439394, -0.06113380938768387, 0.0011531070340424776, 0.02791435271501541, -0.0629466623