515646, 0.03795061632990837, -0.046495188027620316, 0.027385545894503593, -0.00928409118205309, 0.03871630132198334, -0.04383652284741402, -0.012363798916339874, -0.0023397463373839855, 0.04164596647024155, -0.013983121141791344, -0.03339935094118118, 0.005506483837962151, 0.026683306321501732, 2.030475932390974e-34, 0.013665324077010155, 0.027801984921097755, 0.030488980934023857, -0.0982881560921669, 0.03749975934624672, 0.019470784813165665, -0.0018654244486242533, -0.01967896893620491, 0.019831551238894463, 0.0023040694650262594, -0.020432990044355392], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "e69b1683a0de7f0bef88cf940dbfbc686e4b282b7f26fb9764f29a3ed05492e2", "content": "This chunk is part of Case Study E: Priestnall Secondary School, which illustrates the school's approach to inclusivity for autistic students. It highlights the anti-bullying measures in place to foster a supportive environment and emphasizes the positive experiences of Max, a Year 10 student with autism, in building relationships and navigating school life.There is a way of students being able to report bullying anonymously (a button on school website) and the student will receive a reply from a member of staff. There are anti-bullying ambassadors and lots of staff to deal with issues that arise.\nMax enjoyed the residential trip to Wales in Year 7. Max leaves lessons a few minutes before the others in his class to avoid the crowded corridors. He has had the same form tutor all the way through secondary school and they have an excellent relationship. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 38, "split_id": 156, "split_idx_start": 79775, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "2100a04ae13eb428317f62f841ea41f7bfb4c6bf639f78c040c3a531fe59f24e", "range": [543, 702]}, {"doc_id": "223c63de378b6f