0.026224272325634956, 0.007400368805974722, 0.019383354112505913, 0.016184890642762184, -0.030729645863175392], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "530045639e9b0ff1543b71906184f5d825edf659034b2a8f9d9fd1633b0058d1", "content": "This chunk is part of Case Study E: Priestnall Secondary School, which illustrates the school\u2019s commitment to inclusivity for autistic students. It features insights from Max, a Year 10 student, about his positive experiences at the school, emphasizing the importance of friendship, mental health, and community support among students.This is very useful.\u201d\nMax\u2019s advice to new students is to \u201c be yourself and have a lot of fun and make new friends. He said, School is a place not only to learn but where you make friends. This school focuses on mental health, your life, your happiness \u2013 equally important as education.\u201d\nWhen asked what he would like to be different Max said, \u201c Nothing. This is cool. This is awesome.\u201d\nMax ended the discussion by saying, \u201c I hope this meeting goes well and Priestnall is a really great school and should always be respected and well trusted.\u201d\nMeeting with some of the autistic girls at Priestnall school\nA discussion was held with six girls who attended the school they were in Year 7 (n=3), Year 9 (n=2) and Year 11 (n=1).\n", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 38, "split_id": 159, "split_idx_start": 80924, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "0daecce0cead01db9c35ea80d803ebc1649a48de1ebd84227113bdddf73a236a", "range": [380, 495]}, {"doc_id": "5e6553c9f3fca57763bef3f8a95eea9d86a7201db80e8c7f9ae6dcb1ac91b48c", "range": [0, 357]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.04657161608338356, -0.01938779279589653, -0.008333657868206501, 0.017183760181069374, 0.030438