216721326113, -0.019583463668823242, -0.01852201111614704], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "9364d1cc37823902e545bcb446a4bd496ed92a0dd3f5e63b63f90fe5e9d1bd02", "content": "This chunk is from Case Study E: Priestnall Secondary School, detailing the experiences of Debby, a parent of an autistic student named Max. It highlights her positive perceptions of the school's understanding of autism, their inclusive practices, and her search for a supportive educational environment for her son.She wanted to portray the positive side of autism and parenting as she felt so much of the literature focused on the difficulties. She said about the school, \u201cThey \u2018get\u2019 autism here\u201d. She had looked around a number of possible schools and asked if they had quiet spaces and if they could allow Max to type instead of handwrite. Of the Priestnall staff, she said, \u201c I did not get \u2018what the heck \u2026?\u2019 answers as I had at other schools \u2013 they knew what I was asking about and were doing it already.\u201d\nShe said she found Priestnall school when she was Googling about autism and saw a training presentation by the Director of Curriculum Support, Gareth Morewood, and wanted to find out if he was the only shining star at the school or whether he was an example of the whole school culture. Her experience has proved that the whole school has developed an ethos of including students with very diverse physical, developmental and emotional needs, including autism.\n", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 39, "split_id": 164, "split_idx_start": 83584, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "a8143baf0ec2fd69dc0f143db1ff0ae41f58b049e9f7ecd0f8d9df264cf1858e", "range": [643, 774]}, {"doc_id": "48f4484b6446e379f60a4609069eb8a3d67dffdfa540a5a0791bd0e24