82878065109253, 0.04745759069919586, -0.042968686670064926, -0.027231402695178986, -0.010005802847445011, 0.0027286093682050705, -0.0006181734497658908, 0.006809660699218512, -0.0137778976932168, -0.0026135763619095087, 1.859497294410247e-34, 0.007702674251049757, 0.00985255092382431, 0.004035053309053183, -0.059582244604825974, 0.008400495164096355, -0.005048489198088646, -0.03080923669040203, 0.0008642242173664272, 0.01256830245256424, 0.014481043443083763, -0.0391070619225502], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "0c072871daa0c90317e2c5e8db871d071f55ebf014e0ff420de17e530ddccb21", "content": "This chunk is part of Case Study E, focusing on Priestnall Secondary School, which highlights the multidisciplinary approach to supporting autistic students. It details the role of Ellen, the Speech and Language Therapist, in providing individualized support and assessments, emphasizing collaboration with feeder primary schools to enhance communication and social skills for students.A similar network has been established at the University of Southampton (Parsons et al ., 2019).\nDiscussion with Ellen: the school\u2019s Speech and Language Therapist\nEllen is employed directly by the school and works term-time only. She works in eight feeder primary schools and their nurseries as part of Service Level Agreements well established for a number of years. She currently runs individual sessions with five students at Priestnall school completing assessments and supporting them through individualised therapy packages. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 40, "split_id": 169, "split_idx_start": 86900, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "1c9cb630defae4d76b47747b42c23a4a2c3834db0eb8a622bad5d76d0b3115a8", "range": [670, 758]}, {"doc_id": "6279d703eae89c5b8d80e2c75206967e