855231776833534, -0.009172649122774601, -0.0322471521794796], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "defc92f4512ec2ca474773341180aeecc4205eec63aea449593f60943c3946f9", "content": "This chunk is found in Case Study E: Priestnall Secondary School, which emphasizes the school's inclusive approach for autistic students. It highlights the role of Ellen, the Speech and Language Therapist, in facilitating social groups for Year 7 and 8 students, promoting conversation skills and social understanding in a supportive environment.Ellen explains the group to a student and then suggests they give it a go. The group usually consists of a mix of Year 7 and 8 students, not all of whom are autistic. These groups are a safe space to have a conversation that might not go very well and to learn from that. Ground rules are established and they play games and have fun. Students are asked what they would like to gain from the group.\f", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 40, "split_id": 172, "split_idx_start": 87947, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "875451bd6277825d3c734b0cd2616cc056b3e6b5bd71f1117ca999a1f9056f18", "range": [315, 390]}, {"doc_id": "240511a639215fec1fc5f0c4b229964a7c4d213aee5dd0607828e1d64d78d705", "range": [0, 64]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.061193499714136124, -0.07621699571609497, -0.00556963449344039, 0.05270197615027428, 0.017476702108979225, 0.050460346043109894, 0.03632200136780739, -0.019948719069361687, -0.03906094282865524, -0.01157934870570898, -0.023891346529126167, -0.07880397886037827, 0.04396481812000275, -0.010165013372898102, -0.0061882538720965385, -0.0554887130856514, 0.04091201722621918, 0.05088161677122116, 0.014233594760298729, -0.01832820475101471, -0.020602330565452576, 0.013874126598238945, -0.0205211341381073, 0.0346528515