7188721, -0.03874510899186134, -0.035773470997810364, 0.0053029293194413185, 0.008568787947297096, 0.01389250997453928, -0.04194781184196472, -0.011791786178946495, -0.009764389134943485, 1.78474779231245e-34, 0.009940147399902344, -0.007226394023746252, 0.02616734430193901, -0.04322277009487152, 0.04407934844493866, -0.03275598958134651, 0.0055022817105054855, -0.014448335394263268, 0.018171638250350952, 0.06309434026479721, -0.02529514767229557], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "240511a639215fec1fc5f0c4b229964a7c4d213aee5dd0607828e1d64d78d705", "content": "This chunk is from Case Study E: Priestnall Secondary School, which highlights the collaborative approach of staff, including the Speech and Language Therapist, Ellen, in supporting autistic students. It emphasizes proactive communication between Ellen, pastoral staff, Teaching Assistants (TAs), and how this collaboration benefits student support and feedback.Students are asked what they would like to gain from the group.\fGood Autism Practice Guide \u2013 Case Studies\n41 Doc ref: GAP report - case studies_I-S\nEllen also links with pastoral staff and TAs. In this way, TAs can be briefed and students flagged up. She attends the school briefing for TAs and is very proactive. Teachers approach Ellen for advice and support and Ellen might then observe a student and give feedback. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 40, "split_id": 173, "split_idx_start": 88282, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "defc92f4512ec2ca474773341180aeecc4205eec63aea449593f60943c3946f9", "range": [335, 399]}, {"doc_id": "0a87ae662f0703c120ea9af01cfc45bec81700dd70e956c937e42ed33eb47c9b", "range": [0, 105]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.06018451601266861, -0.10116372257471085, -0.025081351399421692, 0.03631700575351