7389, -0.0012500109151005745, -0.03742126747965813, 0.009472952224314213, -0.00405435636639595, -0.010725216008722782, 0.02202744036912918, 0.011730075813829899, 0.01589009538292885, 0.0009209340205416083], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "f76337fc4cf981370a4f409f1f3d99f4a46a38fc1e1a5ba2bc3bc7c641b90a11", "content": "This chunk is part of the case study for Priestnall Secondary School, which highlights the school's approach to supporting autistic students and those with additional needs. It discusses specific student experiences and transitions between schools, emphasizing the importance of peer awareness sessions led by staff to foster understanding of autism among the student body.One student who missed a lot of education at primary school and was home schooled for a time, started at Inscape House in Year 6 and then transitioned to Priestnall gradually and by Year 8 was full time at Priestnall. Another student, came to Priestnall in Year 7 and did well, but then developed mental health issues in Year 9 and went to Inscape for Years 10 and 11 and passed 5 GCSEs. He is now in his second year at a University abroad.\nPeer awareness sessions on autism\nGreg runs peer awareness sessions at Priestnall and talks about hidden disabilities to class groups. He will talk to the autistic students in the class beforehand and ask them if they want to be involved in the discussions or not. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 42, "split_id": 183, "split_idx_start": 92554, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "deecbbfee3a8af36e9821653aa270c77adfa15ca9fe7406558201c39be736f4c", "range": [527, 745]}, {"doc_id": "0eea7ace171849b2e1e5d4e228e82addd464171a9708fc3581d6c113747436e7", "range": [0, 130]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.01426785159856081, -0.053186248987