0.02339649386703968, 0.012492023408412933, 0.0005900603719055653, -0.0432623028755188, -0.009701336733996868, 0.02846452221274376, 1.2940010561575931e-34, -0.0029517500661313534, 0.011525120586156845, 0.006642681080847979, -0.0651102364063263, 0.05665023997426033, 0.0023244479671120644, -0.05041727423667908, -0.0017577717080712318, 0.0086795249953866, 0.011005104519426823, -0.023309964686632156], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "e1e63b1357a157600f05322adc5544dd86365ca55ab8beec795f6214612b73be", "content": "This chunk is part of Case Study F, which discusses the practices at Phoenix School, a community special school in Tower Hamlets, London. It highlights the school's commitment to fostering an inclusive environment for autistic children, providing targeted support, and adapting the curriculum to support their wellbeing and success.An ethos and environment that fosters social inclusion for children and young people on the autism spectrum\n7. Targeted support and measuring progress of children and young people on the autism spectrum\n8. Adapting the curriculum, teaching and learning to promote wellbeing and success for autistic children and young people\nContext\nThe school is in the Borough of Tower Hamlets in Central London. There are currently 264 children on roll and numbers will grow to 380 in the next year. Within the Borough, there has been a rapid increase in the numbers of autistic children and the school is trying to meet this demand. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 46, "split_id": 196, "split_idx_start": 101446, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "dfad934b6a4327a10e22ec102c20b14f4d319e195931efc5f5c3aeaa16a586fe", "range": [327, 438]}, {"doc_id": "c9e40a7fb02b24473f751a94178732cf80b264fa0523436656fef0a4feb1db32", "range": [0, 134]}