8754, 0.044376056641340256, 0.008864269591867924, 0.03450394421815872, -0.04180586710572243, 0.03272983431816101, -0.04327721521258354, 0.05195387080311775, -0.032462455332279205, -0.02334411069750786, 0.022327831014990807, 0.0087843993678689, -0.036534760147333145, -0.04058022424578667, -0.014090953394770622, -0.0017790863057598472, 2.060848456446566e-34, 0.015397123992443085, -0.006228108890354633, 0.011398413218557835, -0.08972667902708054, 0.011888960376381874, -0.009404203854501247, -0.022613972425460815, -0.02630404196679592, 0.018300076946616173, -0.014502245932817459, -0.00047925024409778416], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "18fe5886de3bdfc8e195f77fafbf8c812d1257457257139b7aa174ac1362f80a", "content": "This chunk is part of Case Study F: Phoenix School, which describes the school\u2019s outreach services. It highlights the extensive support provided to students with autism in mainstream schools within the Tower Hamlets borough. The excerpt emphasizes the collaboration with key staff and parents, showcasing the training and workshops facilitated by the outreach team.The service provides very effective support for pupils across the borough of Tower Hamlets and shares the strategies developed and used at Phoenix school. The Outreach Service works with pupils from the ages of 3 to 19 years in mainstream, maintained school settings. Staff provide training for key staff, and for organisations within the community. They run termly parents\u2019 evenings and a programme of parent workshops and courses throughout the year, hosted by the mainstream schools.\nEach outreach teacher works with a caseload of mainstream schools, and will allocate clusters of school visits across the school year using the red, amber and green (RAG) system. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b