-0.0038190751802176237, -0.03877298906445503, -0.01130390539765358, -0.015945477411150932, 1.9080607083391841e-34, -0.005892829969525337, -0.03753194585442543, 0.01337910071015358, -0.07795590162277222, 0.024588963016867638, -0.0019719013944268227, -0.02277318574488163, 0.007445740047842264, 0.031070280820131302, -0.00896055530756712, 0.009615235961973667], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "a1c40fb17b5c7e99004e9d20a93f93e1896d8db27036379608c765a1df4d54f8", "content": "This chunk is part of the case study from Phoenix School, which focuses on understanding and supporting autistic students. It discusses the implementation of communication systems like PECS, the importance of displaying pupil profiles, and the use of visuals to enhance engagement and learning for students with autism, promoting their voice in decision-making.the use of the Picture Exchange Communication system (PECS) (Bondy and Frost, 2002)). Pupil profiles are displayed in all the classrooms. There are mixed ability classes with one teacher and up to 5 TAs with 10 children.\nVisuals are consistently used throughout the day for timetables and tasks.\nP2 \u2013 Enabling the voice of the autistic child and young person to contribute to and influence decisions\nPupil voice is promoted across the school, both in the secondary phase and upper primary. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 48, "split_id": 205, "split_idx_start": 106180, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "4bd901e256ed30d36aacee5053ee7f0f9151b80be0c37b1202283c7de64ca25f", "range": [301, 387]}, {"doc_id": "fc5cd2a55355c083b31af1ffb2e635ef000156c1640bc3217ccc62aef6046849", "range": [0, 194]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.025744730606675148, -0.08726485073566437, -0.04323342815041542, 0.002075056079775095, -0.0091970320791