008625559508800507, 0.04255235940217972, 0.009469217620790005, -0.007844202220439911, -0.019945524632930756, -0.03348030894994736], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "f45bd75dab6e48703c1d87490e542f709fe17eadac86dac89be199e75907000d", "content": "This chunk is from the case study of Phoenix School, which highlights the importance of student voice in developing personalized profiles. It emphasizes how students contribute to their profiles, making them feel empowered while ensuring that this information is accessible to all stakeholders involved in their education, including teachers and external agencies.Heather Moran, a psychologist, has used this approach effectively with autistic students (Moran, 2006). Students have input into how their profile template is put together. Some students put things on their profiles themselves while others provide information for the staff to include. It is very empowering for the pupils and as they are given choices about what information is put on their profile. These profiles are then shared with all who work with the pupils, including external agencies ( e.g. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 49, "split_id": 208, "split_idx_start": 107522, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "0514eb0f7e58c3637ca3abcde3b768c9848b015e3a89947e615de2fb302d6672", "range": [577, 681]}, {"doc_id": "01395cf3655a6415db3fb36215e8cab77375a3229a941cafd7ebbac9416940b1", "range": [0, 101]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.07883434742689133, -0.09041935205459595, -0.031602706760168076, -0.01331829559057951, -0.018384350463747978, 0.059139665216207504, 0.04069746658205986, -0.06537459045648575, -0.054260123521089554, 0.010983760468661785, 0.05570176988840103, -0.05392976105213165, 0.004422790836542845, 0.027995577082037926, -0.00968670751899