60719823837, 0.0015033138915896416, -0.02772463485598564, 0.019003205001354218, 0.039920952171087265, -0.03764374181628227, -0.02456556260585785, -0.002076607896015048, 0.007387015502899885, 1.6031955596872027e-34, 0.004785485100001097, -0.0011086513986811042, 0.00378415547311306, -0.07831928879022598, 0.021007034927606583, 0.0006687096902169287, -0.052938517183065414, -0.02825097367167473, 0.007114445324987173, -0.006165424827486277, -0.005943463649600744], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "239be6c25d307aa9c2061d06350886873ce771dfe87660f385e296f0d65d6d17", "content": "This chunk is part of the case study detailing the practices at Phoenix School, a community special school, highlighting their efforts in supporting students with autism to understand their diagnosis in collaboration with parents. It discusses the resources used for communication and understanding, as well as the implementation of ClassDojo for parent engagement.The staff work hard to support students to understand their diagnosis where that approach is agreed with parents. The way they approach this depends on the child\u2019s intellectual ability. Some work through The Autism and me workbook (Kershaw, 2011), The ASD and me picture book (Shaul, 2017) or The conversation train (Shaul, 2014). One of the parent workshops focuses on talking about the diagnosis with the family and their child.\nClassDojo ( https://www.classdojo.com ) is used across the primary department and in some parts of the secondary school to communicate with parents. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 50, "split_id": 216, "split_idx_start": 110846, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "4f7d632c5926b23ca7e32f7810c88733fa2cd3a8d35eba39ce5caab4a7d849fc", "range": [405, 519]}, {"doc_id": "c6c8ad737b891a9044da6f19e68