.006191125605255365, 2.2558048832966634e-34, -0.0049395025707781315, -0.004020740743726492, 0.044225163757801056, -0.08157332241535187, 0.008913123048841953, 0.006827728822827339, 0.03422761708498001, -0.028478404507040977, 0.02517147921025753, -0.03586569428443909, -0.017694730311632156], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "97156b40ab68470719bcc0bad0973175961d793e4672e1b930f841423b7aab48", "content": "This chunk is located in Case Study F, which outlines the outreach work of the Phoenix School, emphasizing collaboration with parents and various organizations to enhance support for autistic children. The section also discusses workforce development, highlighting the school's comprehensive training programs for staff and parents to foster effective educational practices.The Outreach service is part of a Parent working party made up of representatives from CAMHS, Social Care, the National Autistic Society (NAS) and the Parent Advice Centre. The group shares what they offer to parents and identifies where support might be needed or where there are gaps.\fGood Autism Practice Guide \u2013 Case Studies\n51 Doc ref: GAP report - case studies_I-S\nP4 \u2013 Workforce development to support autistic children and young people\nThe school provides a very comprehensive induction to autism for professionals and parents/carers. Some sample training plans are attached to this case study. The training focuses on a wide range of topics. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 50, "split_id": 219, "split_idx_start": 112161, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "ccbee0df28c190095a1e78c593bed781a649f38b4575633098753f02a005b7d9", "range": [379, 552]}, {"doc_id": "89cad023ceef2d4561fa37981bccec4d513dbad903384b63d8a50a79ac886a77", "range": [0, 48]}]}, "score": null, "embeddi