89102037250996, -0.010885408148169518, 0.002902396721765399, 2.4592985674583483e-34, -0.0007209762698039412, -0.029870253056287766, 0.03879022225737572, -0.06179273873567581, 0.00951415579766035, -0.010373716242611408, -0.04296541213989258, -0.016350291669368744, 0.0393449030816555, 0.00020774886070284992, -0.01669228821992874], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "e0239d417ffdc1c6bc0e26e6e62bcdb2188d26c0dee7fd98272ab25f36660bdc", "content": "This chunk is part of Case Study F, which discusses Phoenix School's approaches to supporting autistic students through sensory circuits and promoting social inclusion. It highlights the collaboration between the school and universities to enhance educational practices and the individualized strategies used to help students engage in learning effectively.Thirty people from a range of universities are involved so practice and research combine to inform each other.\nP6 \u2013 An ethos and environment that fosters social inclusion for children and young people on the autism spectrum\nThe school has sensory circuits which is class-based and individually tailored to each child. The children engage with alerting, organising and calming strategies and activities in all classes for half an hour as soon as they enter school. In some classes, they focus on only two aspects, depending on the needs of the children. This session supports the children to be ready for learning. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 51, "split_id": 222, "split_idx_start": 113655, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "6634ae07abda658d62fdaaea79b0250c02a79a1392be26ad787854506603b3de", "range": [431, 542]}, {"doc_id": "c5bea68658f576c0a3fe83c9c2a79ec2a1727d0787efa40a8ad38004924fdc4f", "range": [0, 61]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [-0.003912147134