-0.0049566589295864105, 0.014170055277645588, -0.09546155482530594, 0.029455434530973434, 0.007414345629513264, -0.009084975346922874, 0.024233786389231682, 0.05808788537979126, 0.013225066475570202, -0.03449065983295441], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "87653c5262ed80289a18be80545d5f2b1f1deb9e38cbb4dd790100f496765032", "content": "This chunk is situated in the case study of Phoenix School, which highlights the use of sensory boxes and tailored strategies to support students with autism and sensory regulation issues. The document discusses the importance of individualized support from teaching assistants and occupational therapy staff in enhancing students' learning experiences.Each class also has sensory boxes which contain equipment to support pupils with sensory regulation issues and the children can ask for these at any time. They contain items such as blankets, fiddle toys and shoulder weights.\fGood Autism Practice Guide \u2013 Case Studies\n52 Doc ref: GAP report - case studies_I-S\nStaff use a sensory checklist to assess the child\u2019s needs.\nThere is an OT assistant who gives 1:1 support. The child always goes to this session with a TA who is then able to follow up and carry out the programme for the rest of the week. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 51, "split_id": 225, "split_idx_start": 115093, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "8e68e1ed3e75331d6a06de74ca6f4f46e9faef5fa81b20f65b15547bc0b5556e", "range": [518, 673]}, {"doc_id": "ddf2b5d88a218cd3182041836f97bdb68dbfa8c233b3de39a0ed7cec17b6c6a1", "range": [0, 132]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [-0.0006231702282093465, -0.11144355684518814, -0.038145046681165695, 0.04230109974741936, 0.0051594809629023075, 0.028119726106524467, 0.039659518748521805, -0.038810908794403076, -0.0