824363205581903, 0.034064874053001404, 0.03193144127726555, -0.0015678404597565532, 0.021278098225593567, 0.02376801148056984, -0.025411467999219894, 0.017117662355303764, -0.04366636648774147, 0.06530247628688812, -0.0488220639526844, -0.03481191024184227, 0.022228479385375977, -0.005446834955364466, -0.023195182904601097, -0.03698522597551346, -0.04608684033155441, 0.01863282360136509, 1.944263753251477e-34, -0.016977300867438316, 0.003494774689897895, 0.03825013339519501, -0.046457283198833466, 0.03422455117106438, -0.005216420628130436, -0.053969159722328186, 0.008899313397705555, 0.03317595273256302, 0.0019566654227674007, -0.013632641173899174], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "c0cb5b933d9b1bcdc3cac122bd2fce499fe79bd41ed13466c2a1a8ed37b7c818", "content": "This chunk is part of Case Study F: Phoenix School, which highlights the school's focus on supporting autistic girls. It discusses a program aimed at developing their identity and social understanding through group activities facilitated by Speech and Language Teaching Assistants, emphasizing the school's commitment to tailored support and effective communication strategies.Part of the aim of the programme is to support the girls in developing their identity and their social understanding. As there are not many girls in each class, the group is drawn from across classes. The last group had 6 girls across two KS3 classes.\nSpeech and Language Support\nEach class has a Speech and Language Teaching Assistant who attends with the group of girls and follows up activities back in the class. These TAs have a training session every week for 1.5 hours. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 53, "split_id": 234, "split_idx_start": 118334, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "e80dae41d43ece462dd5c02231