07, 0.00713013019412756, -0.0021955673582851887, 0.03943600878119469, 0.015366987325251102, -0.0419207327067852, -0.05520137771964073], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "e81c431660ddd1c3d2b7987ec10ceed39449da6394b8178290d3fb7df4f6879c", "content": "This chunk is part of Case Study F, which focuses on Phoenix School, a community special school. It highlights the school's approach to learning and development, particularly in measuring progress for autistic children. It discusses the implementation of targeted support and innovative assessment methods, such as the SCERTS framework and questionnaires.Good Autism Practice Guide \u2013 Case Studies\n54 Doc ref: GAP report - case studies_I-S\nLearning and development\nP7 \u2013 Targeted support and measuring progress of children and young people on the autism spectrum\nLearning objectives are printed on students\u2019 work using symbols and words.\nThe school measures the impact of their interventions through a series of questionnaires, as part of an ongoing bigger research project.\nThey are piloting a different approach to monitoring progress using the SCERTS Appreciative Inquiry model and peer-to-peer monitoring. Staff have introduced appreciative inquiry learning walks to go into class to identify inter-personal and transactional support. They are working on a checklist for teachers to use as their standards. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 54, "split_id": 237, "split_idx_start": 119291, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "e8151a7706ad5c3c8d9a7c9c8aead0a9b9e43005f86dbc42302cb4ea8e6c1c0d", "range": [260, 541]}, {"doc_id": "031a1b4aabc3c0e6f6b7c412c828bc19695c6b1563bff3820fea1c46c8d85f97", "range": [0, 72]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.009994546882808208, -0.05043032020330429, -0.04392641037