32, 0.004652467090636492, -0.04045616090297699, -0.04661841318011284, -0.06018706411123276, -0.019153909757733345, 2.0354635181873143e-34, -0.005749047268182039, -0.009369689971208572, 0.017050033435225487, -0.0017730738036334515, -0.03083621896803379, -0.005697597283869982, -0.02717057801783085, 0.02762395329773426, 0.05578761547803879, -0.016609108075499535, -0.04228071868419647], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "40188b07c334372fb16a4ea688ff6cc896ac2c19b7dc81bb4743567e7b35bbf0", "content": "This chunk is part of the case study for Phoenix School, which focuses on the teaching strategies and individualized support for autistic students. It highlights the school's approach to modeling tasks, simplifying language, and effectively communicating educational targets through Personal Education Plans (PEPs) shared with parents to enhance collaboration and support at home.Teaching is in context and the task is modelled, staff use reduced language and only single prompts.\nThe school is now working on making sure that targets are written so that they are clearly understood by all staff and by the students. Individual PEPs (personal education plans) are shared with parents at termly meetings. Information goes home in a home-school book. Staff make suggestions in the book as to what can be tried at home and staff believe this has enhanced home\u2013school communication. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 55, "split_id": 244, "split_idx_start": 122654, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "59a347ab2625e92f0553390149834a3227facac6581a1a88ba0a5bac9b41c05b", "range": [419, 520]}, {"doc_id": "e3434c3670b55877b8752abb43abbb509d1c72ded45bd2b92fa02d66cc356124", "range": [0, 130]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.02701657824218273, -0.08402437716722488, -0.035471