, -0.026307251304388046, 0.03390151262283325, 0.008274388499557972, -0.032149165868759155, -0.04182373732328415, -0.012747708708047867, -0.00732029415667057, 1.6542909930395858e-34, -4.2415581447130535e-06, 0.0012462903978303075, 0.02020803652703762, -0.07145882397890091, 0.0140221631154418, 0.009864062070846558, -0.009093793109059334, 0.005574957933276892, 0.026041395962238312, -0.026069538667798042, -0.009615783579647541], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "f456e854979a61611b2e2bd9fcae88bfde86ebab9281c780f93c9210b50c2f84", "content": "This chunk is part of the Good Autism Practice Guide, specifically under Case Study G: Springfields School. It discusses how the school adapts the curriculum and teaching methods to foster the wellbeing and success of autistic students, highlighting the use of activity sheets, task boards, and work-based learning initiatives.Good Autism Practice Guide \u2013 Case Studies\n56 Doc ref: GAP report - case studies_I-S\nP8 \u2013 Adapting the curriculum, teaching and learning to promote wellbeing and success for autistic children and young people\nActivity sheets are adapted to suit the needs of the children using symbols and words. Alongside these, the children use mini task boards to record what they have done during the lesson, as in this Maths example.\nThere is a focus on work-based learning. Students in the secondary classes take part in work-related learning in the school caf\u00e9 and run a mobile coffee cart. Students have an Enterprise Fair once a term and there are good links with employers in the area. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 56, "split_id": 248, "split_idx_start": 124061, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "d649887d26cf3a00e845d364e236776af8c547e55fc5e455e03a62ec9e551034", "range": [359, 654]}, 