876277, 0.001417076215147972, -0.012909460812807083, 0.04445914924144745, -0.009772264398634434, -0.006468509323894978], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "c491b365f14f5b1b3e2bcc03b2db83ebf5f94196ea5ca02504fe1c66b8ad13af", "content": "This chunk is located in Case Study F, which discusses the Phoenix School\u2019s approach to autism education, highlighting the use of the Zones of Regulation framework to enhance students' self-awareness and emotional regulation. It also describes their involvement in outdoor work-related learning activities, promoting life skills and community engagement.It will include looking at the \u2018zones of regulation\u2019 in an interactive environment so that students begin to develop a better understanding of themselves and their arousal levels.\nThis photo shows an immersive environment.\nStudents are also involved in working in the local park doing maintenance tasks as part of their outdoor work-related learning.\fGood Autism Practice Guide \u2013 Case Studies\n57 Doc ref: GAP report - case studies_I-S\nTransferable practice \u2013 actions you could try in your setting\n\u2022 Use the AET Autism Standards and Competency Framework for each age group (Early Years, School and Post-16) in your setting to audit whole school practice and the skills and knowledge of staff. These are free to download from the AET website www.autismeducationtrust.org.uk.\n", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 56, "split_id": 251, "split_idx_start": 125240, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "04c7f50539769e854e47fd39dabf9b11faed7863eb96a755db7a838f289664f9", "range": [272, 452]}, {"doc_id": "379e7724dbf88ca2a28518f96ebf1e41e83a0ceb88045dbc1e71ba4a4063c08e", "range": [0, 81]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.04568695276975632, -0.130593702197074