1c601d3c535e8006977fc97fb2311b302b631f5c", "content": "This chunk is part of the \"Further resources\" section in the \"Good Autism Practice Guide \u2013 Case Studies\", which provides various links and references to training programs, assessment tools, and frameworks aimed at improving autism practice in educational settings for children and young people aged 5 to 25.Good Autism Practice Journal 8,2, 51\u201363. \u2013 can be accessed from the AET website in the National Standards for Schools\fGood Autism Practice Guide \u2013 Case Studies\n58 Doc ref: GAP report - case studies_I-S\nFurther resources\nCygnet training developed by Barnardo\u2019s at https://www.barnardos.org.uk/cygnet.htm\nClass dojo \u2013 an app to enable staff to communicate with parents https://www.classdojo.com\nEarwig assessment system for early years http://earwigacademic.com/\nPart 2 of MAPP \u2013 the Continuum of Skill Development can be found at http://www.thedalesschool.org/article/assessment-progression-mapp/275 .\nASDAN https://www.asdan.org.uk/courses\nFrom the Autism Education Trust ( http://www.autismeducationtrust.org.uk/)\nNational Standards for Schools 5\u201316 which sets out key elements of good autism practice in settings for autistic children and young people aged 5 to 16 years\nCompetency Framework for Staff 5\u201316 which sets out knowledge and skills required for teaching staff working with autistic children and young people aged 5 to 16 years\nNational Standards 16\u201325 \u2013 which sets out key elements of good autism practice in settings for autistic children and young people aged 16 to 25\u00df years\nCompetency Framework 16\u201325 which sets out knowledge and skills required for teaching staff working with autistic children and young people aged 16 to 25 years\nAET Parent/Carers Guide: What to look for in your child\u2019s school: a document for parents/\ncarers to complete identifying what they deem to be important for their autistic son or daughter \u2013 free to download from the AET website.\nAET Tools for Teachers\nAET Progression Framework \u2013 a means of recording progress specifically for autistic children and young people and for setting learning intentions.\nThe AET also has a number of autism training hubs in different parts of England which offer training on autism at different levels and for the different age phases ( i.e. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 57, "split_id": 254, "split_idx_start": 128095, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "39eb549e0a165834cc2abceecb7a9f616c34054512283ded5599a0b96224ce97", "range": [1249, 1290]}, {"doc_id": "7674d033e882355334a5ea253706cafafdd9f81e95652fde08ce360d0bec2213", "range": [0, 171]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.029293639585375786, -0.10277318209409714, -0.028837988153100014, 0.006542793940752745, -0.012035218998789787, 0.038215067237615585, 0.0530470535159111, -0.03188294917345047, -0.023188212886452675, 0.012688308022916317, 0.012756574898958206, -0.06660960614681244, 0.047039274126291275, -0.025082498788833618, 0.00211946084164083, -0.08010094612836838, 0.02095107175409794, 0.036336541175842285, -0.060012247413396835, 0.02823888324201107, 0.029036398977041245, 0.016441550105810165, -0.03358262777328491, 0.03875179961323738, 0.0183300469070673, -0.024851540103554726, -0.007649994920939207, -0.0011377534829080105, 0.03155221790075302, -0.088707335293293, 0.04047931358218193, -0.005047828424721956, -0.021501999348402023, 0.030909981578588486, 2.431912434985861e-06, 0.013486876152455807, 0.01081059966236353, -0.02193070389330387, -0.0019841385073959827, -0.006422132253646851, -0.04639497026801109, 0.005489483941346407, 0.04114153981208801, 0.06896855682134628, -0.009913760237395763, -0.026985200121998787, 0.043664123862981796, 0.03288279846310