 0.03686551749706268, 0.00039921398274600506, 0.0031525413505733013, -0.057355139404535294, 0.0496857613325119, -0.037100691348314285, 0.06447988003492355, -0.014467659406363964, -0.04083675518631935, 0.026337305083870888, 0.0225882176309824, -0.003939944319427013, -0.029433298856019974, -0.03058684803545475, -0.01714727096259594, 2.1251215947313996e-34, -0.006160849705338478, 0.011903191916644573, 0.030587779358029366, -0.11289024353027344, -0.01561385951936245, -0.01598094217479229, -0.042330507189035416, 0.008095242083072662, 0.035253290086984634, -0.018977385014295578, -0.032429758459329605], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "5f42d0e6189a5230e2563723eeb530750a3057f7651cd986a03d1b9651927ed6", "content": "This chunk is part of Case Study G: Springfields School, which focuses on personalized learning for students with autism. It details the school\u2019s strategies for monitoring student progress through termly work scrutiny and individualized support systems, including personalized work desks and self-regulation tools, to enhance independent learning and engagement.There is a focused work scrutiny termly to monitor evidence of personalised strategies to enable students to achieve their outcomes independently and to support students to be engaged with their learning. The SENCo also holds termly meetings for teachers to discuss the needs of individual students and ensure understanding is developed across the staff team.\nYoung people have their own personalised work desk in the school. There are 5-point scales (outlined in the book The Incredible 5-point Scale by Kari Dunn Buron and Mitzi Curtis in 2003) to develop self-regulation and personalised strategies their desks for students to refer to. Scales are individual and allow students to identify whether they are working, whether they need five minutes or whether they need time outside the room. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.googl