0.037444014102220535, 0.032271355390548706, 0.018467964604496956, -0.015888823196291924], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "b83ae430dcff5c99d8b4dde881e32d79eddf6e25991de6a7ddb27cea21bd3d07", "content": "This chunk is part of the case study discussing the practices at Springfields School, emphasizing how staff engagement with students fosters a sense of belonging and pride. It highlights the importance of incorporating student feedback into curriculum discussions and targeted support frameworks for measuring progress, particularly during the transition phases in Key Stage 4.Staff feel that the impact of this is that the students feel listened to, they feel proud of their school and develop a sense of belonging. The students\u2019 ideas are then presented to staff who have a discussion about how they might act on some of the issues mentioned.\nLearning and development\nP7 \u2013 Targeted support and measuring progress of children and young people on the autism spectrum.\nStaff hold termly meetings to discuss the breadth of the curriculum, conduct audits around staff expertise, receive feedback around current pathways and look for opportunities to increase pathway options. Students in KS4 have an active transition plan which includes personalised strategy suggestions for teaching and learning.\n", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 61, "split_id": 263, "split_idx_start": 134456, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "6eec50bec3abe507df75f4aa5d2ae40888e385e4e2d311e0553c6b71fcc3d92b", "range": [518, 658]}, {"doc_id": "0e73ac130f66ca9c8227d3b17de56cb8db003aecf63611fa6221fac49ec262ca", "range": [0, 123]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.014906528405845165, -0.06382663547992706, -0.026530398055911064, -0.002443989971652627, -0.026921983808279037, 0.03281587