c86fd30d72fd2b926c4eaaec00b5b041c4bc4fff3157921d647f0531356b5aec", "content": "This chunk is part of Case Study G, which details the practices at Springfields School, a community special school supporting students with autism and other learning difficulties. It focuses on adapting the curriculum for Key Stage 4 students to promote success and wellbeing through personalized vocational pathways aligned with student interests.P8 \u2013 Adapting the curriculum, teaching and learning to promote wellbeing and success for autistic children and young people\nThe specialist Key Stage 4 curriculum is based on five key principles:\n\u2022 Personalisation and choice\n\u2022 Challenge and enjoyment\n\u2022 Breadth and depth\n\u2022 Progression and outcomes\n\u2022 Collaboration.\nThe school has created 6 vocational pathways that students can follow at KS3/4 in Years 9, 10 and 11. There are currently 57 pupils in KS4. There are just four girls and 89% of the students are autistic. All pupils make a choice in Year 8 about which pathway they will follow and then spend ten hours a week doing this. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 61, "split_id": 265, "split_idx_start": 135690, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "0e73ac130f66ca9c8227d3b17de56cb8db003aecf63611fa6221fac49ec262ca", "range": [638, 952]}, {"doc_id": "f4f68d8df4379d677aadb6895f99634b1be59ec0785c97cb3cca7882ad691131", "range": [0, 116]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.03421821817755699, -0.08556093275547028, -0.020162032917141914, -0.009344181977212429, -0.014527793042361736, 0.025333162397146225, -0.008823690004646778, -0.07718302309513092, -0.03391590341925621, 0.026568368077278137, 0.02837848849594593, -0.0026855790056288242, -0.005346987396478653, 0.01896013133227825, -0.029884779825806618, -0.0