0060053193010389805, 0.003465980524197221, 0.06538774073123932, 0.01832493394613266, -0.03307723253965378, 0.006100379396229982, -0.05161742493510246, 0.0030930163338780403, -0.023552071303129196, 0.049739327281713486, 0.0006795086665078998, -0.03533453494310379, 0.003078916808590293, 0.01995205320417881, 0.011234579607844353, 0.00015894875105004758, -0.047944486141204834, -0.02814926952123642, 2.445537936714035e-34, 0.0024975568521767855, 0.019084395840764046, 0.028627224266529083, -0.08271019905805588, 0.012060195207595825, -0.006799743045121431, -0.018483852967619896, 0.002043063985183835, 0.002065433654934168, 0.04688004404306412, -0.036297332495450974], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "c62a724ee0ebcbdf66a9514fc3a46b67c6629fd8e91238638e0ba87ce3e1db0f", "content": "This chunk is part of Case Study G: Springfields School, which discusses the school's vocational pathways for students aged 14-19, emphasizing their enjoyment and motivation in learning through practical activities. It highlights the impact of personalized pathways on student engagement and the popularity of specific vocational tracks such as Outdoor Learning and Engineering.School staff say students are happiest to learn when they are following a pathway \u2013 they are very motivated. There are high levels of engagement, for example, during learning on survival skills in the woods for the outdoor Learning pathway.\nStudents say that they love the pathways and enjoy all the experience they have through the vocational activities. Choosing to do something the students enjoy promotes engagement and gives greater breadth and depth to their education. The most popular pathways are Outdoor Learning, Engineering and Transport, and Business and Enterprise which includes Computing and ICT. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bc