, 0.0013610129244625568, -0.022453103214502335, 0.0779450535774231, 1.3766648407909088e-05, -0.05622464790940285, -0.0001580713433213532, 0.019046323373913765, -0.010920439846813679, -0.030409350991249084, 0.003036351641640067, -0.01644013449549675, 2.1633586810892216e-34, -0.00014688970986753702, -0.022803550586104393, 0.039205022156238556, -0.0754036158323288, 0.031077595427632332, -0.0119478739798069, -0.03192457929253578, -0.0038501753006130457, -0.0003060527960769832, 0.007964743301272392, -0.023488420993089676], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "466d95adad4cd4cdd8d860fb7dc64ab5416889b01c58c0fbad9383aebd8690de", "content": "This chunk is part of Case Study H: St. John the Baptist Church of England Primary School, which highlights the experiences of a preschool child named John with autism. It details the teacher's modifications to the nursery environment, including replacing traditional play equipment to better engage John and facilitate his participation in classroom activities.the non-carpeted area of the classroom). He appeared to be interested in the activities\nin the non-carpeted area, as he would point to things within it. She lowered some of the play equipment such as the sand tray and John entered the area to play with this. He did not use the water bath so the teacher replaced the water in the bath with a blue silky fabric and put the boats on top of this. He then played with the boats and once he was familiar with using the bath, she replaced the fabric with water.\n", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 67, "split_id": 283, "split_idx_start": 147283, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "1a9419bc92af12096195eadda4e41619de49f748cb75c4c1b6e4790c19c92535", "range": [600, 641]}, {"doc_id": "d846081a6caa2340849b6b28800cac506e4f40ed854cc84