, -0.009754215367138386, 0.02464551292359829, -0.007584383711218834, -0.011190918274223804], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "ce9264a00456c6c205a0e66358f8a862602a6b5f58088653d09322f2c8c074ce", "content": "This chunk is part of Case Study H, which discusses the inclusion of a nursery child named John, who is on the autism spectrum. It highlights the importance of the relationship between the nursery teacher and John's mother, emphasizing trust and collaboration in supporting John's transition and development in the nursery setting.His mother is now only at the nursery to drop him off and collect him. The teacher feels that her relationship with his mother is key and that his mother trusts her judgement.\fGood Autism Practice Guide \u2013 Case Studies\n68 Doc ref: GAP report - case studies_I-S\nP4 \u2013 Workforce development to support autistic children and young people\nThe nursery teacher\u2019s professional development journey began with an email invitation to a conference run by the AET training team. Two teachers from the school attended this in April 2018 where Ros Blackburn, an autistic adult, spoke and took from this, \u2018 have the same expectations of an autistic child as you would for every other child\u2019 but make adjustments to enable the child to be successful. Following the conference, the teacher then completed the AET Training Tiers 1 and 3, and put things in place as a result of the training. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 67, "split_id": 289, "split_idx_start": 149881, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "3a2646cd8d542e9d3cd627f18290b4be6c4be3b61156a085596f41d9975bb515", "range": [538, 609]}, {"doc_id": "045f89091914bdf0ad06f47309ae4ab07095bbae803844691eebb2db57f893b0", "range": [0, 138]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": 