30474111437797546, -0.040029529482126236, -0.005949690006673336, 0.009678960777819157, -0.009814733639359474, -0.03745951130986214, -0.006837441120296717, -0.03046625480055809, 1.8454673560734503e-34, 0.008298237808048725, -0.03504609316587448, 0.03578873723745346, -0.04383356496691704, 0.05247994884848595, -0.017556261271238327, -0.03423498943448067, 0.002934990916401148, 0.03807450458407402, 0.013153058476746082, -0.013088039122521877], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "b99b0b5cf6fd505c909b569bf9c3344754d15146df19ef27a5d75dd5d0042cbb", "content": "This chunk is part of a case study focused on the inclusion of John, a nursery child with autism, at St. John the Baptist Church of England Primary School. It highlights the teacher's strategies to enhance social interactions and communication skills by rotating groupings of children to facilitate peer relationships and support John's engagement in classroom activities.Over time, she gradually rotated the groupings so that different children replaced friends within the group to build John\u2019s social skills and to familiarise other children with John. As a result John\u2019s interactions with his peers have improved. He\fGood Autism Practice Guide \u2013 Case Studies\n69 Doc ref: GAP report - case studies_I-S\ncan now regularly be seen helping another little girl with SEN at snack time by passing her a cup. When he is building with blocks, he now passes blocks to his partner. Other children are modelling language, asking John things like, \u2018 Shall we build it higher?\u2019 and using actions to demonstrate this as they have seen adults do so when working with John. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 68, "split_id": 296, "split_idx_start": 153196, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "13c4c6c0d60e