507764816, -0.01129800546914339, 0.0063882204703986645, 1.3937476063377443e-34, 0.007607684936374426, -0.029464269056916237, 0.06799615919589996, -0.05045388638973236, 0.033397376537323, -0.020442312583327293, -0.04030841588973999, 0.015328101813793182, 0.03426847234368324, 0.006750314962118864, -0.03466280177235603], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "6dcc6c6593235b248230fda0c19542c67ea8a4f8f8a5980e41dca8be4e528d6d", "content": "This chunk is part of Case Study H: St. John the Baptist Church of England Primary School, focusing on the successful inclusion of a nursery child named John, who is on the autism spectrum. It highlights his progress in social interactions and language skills, as well as plans for his transition to Reception.Every child said, \u2018 Thank you John\u2019 and he smiled in acknowledgement. When she takes the register he now smiles and attempts to say \u2018 Morning\u2019. He can recognise colours and is beginning to name these.\nPlans are already in place to transition John into the Reception class, starting with visits with his teacher to join in story time.\fGood Autism Practice Guide \u2013 Case Studies\n70 Doc ref: GAP report - case studies_I-S\nTransferable practice \u2013 actions you could try in your setting\n\u2022 Use familiar and favourite items to encourage children to use classroom areas and equipment they are not making use of.\n", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 69, "split_id": 299, "split_idx_start": 154603, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "40e50a3ba0c6c21151ab14a84900aa03ebd9fb4f29af8b2a893622585a5fe3e2", "range": [292, 362]}, {"doc_id": "edf8bb3ccc9e4abefae979386dc34ef63993911fe7e2973085c5c8417a7f8101", "range": [0, 268]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.04127706214785576, -0.055922944098711014, -0.011