79", "content": "This chunk is part of the recommendations in the Good Autism Practice Guide, which emphasizes the importance of engaging parents in their child's education and the benefits of collaboration between parents and educators. It also lists further resources and tools provided by the Autism Education Trust to support parents and staff in effectively working with autistic children.\u2022 Explain your approach in detail to parents so that they understand and can work in partnership.\n\u2022 Involve parents in training events and invite them to share their ideas and strategies.\nFurther resources\nMarvellous Me app ( http://www.marvellousme.com )\nAutism Education Trust ( http://www.autismeducationtrust.org.uk/)\nNational Standards for Schools 5\u201316 \u2013 which sets out key elements of good autism practice in settings for autistic children and young people aged 5 to 16 years\nCompetency Framework for Staff 5\u201316 which sets out knowledge and skills required for teaching staff working with autistic children and young people aged 5 to 16 years\nAET Parent/Carers Guide: What to look for in your child\u2019s school: a document for parents/\ncarers to complete identifying what they deem to be important for their autistic son or daughter \u2013 free to download from the AET website.\nAET Progression Framework \u2013 a means of recording progress specifically for autistic children and young people and for setting learning intentions.\nThe AET also has a number of autism training hubs in different parts of England which offer training on autism at different levels and for the different age phases ( i.e. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "gap-report-case-studies_i-s.pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo_7Q7LxUFHSLjQLhEE8MuxPnqGFEGEo", "source_id": "8bfe7c3cc9a918e195ac64f0cc2adc98cd589bcc8b9655b9f78b69ba9bfddeb0", "page_number": 70, "split_id": 301, "split_idx_start": 155711, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "edf8bb3ccc9e4abef