0.01698443852365017], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "b14278d35eaf2d33d66d9e1dc866b27a6e3fe05140e9b2ca979ca70dca03bbab", "content": "This chunk discusses the eight principles of Good Autism Practice incorporated in the AET Post-16 Standards Framework. It highlights how these principles are organized within key themes and their development based on research, policy, and autistic individuals' insights, along with references to available reports on the Autism Education Trust website.These principles are embedded within the AET\u2019s themes of \u2018Understanding the learner\u2019, \u2018Positive and effective relationships\u2019, \u2018Enabling environments\u2019, and \u2018Learning and development\u2019. The eight principles have emerged from an examination of the current evidence from research and practice, as well as policies and the perspectives and insights of autistic individuals (Guldberg et al., 2019). The reports outlining this work are available for free on the Autism Education Trust website. They consist of two reports, the \u2018Good Autism Practice: Full Report,\u2019 and a \u2018Practitioner Guide to Good Autism Practice in Education\u2019.\n", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "Post-16-Standards-Framework_T-HUB-Sc-Mt (1).pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1J2sZ87itIDhKcF_wmZN2TcBeZRLjlMJP", "source_id": "8c4c774884107306cf77966fe0ff24690d0139f6223da0babe2c1bcd4b2a95b5", "page_number": 3, "split_id": 4, "split_idx_start": 3489, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "b9b847b0d5ab1a153d6a4222b8fdb7b1519a5534ba424334ed2ee2447f8fc85f", "range": [607, 790]}, {"doc_id": "62f0420769e6ee4a893485cfdde47a35d79489065a2feb06dc2c6a56bc07b240", "range": [0, 135]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.029845811426639557, -0.08043073862791061, -0.031961940228939056, 0.01845768839120865, 0.017041228711605072, 0.0003414549573790282, -0.0010998210636898875, -0.036734048277139664, -0.03459689021110535, 0.02380714751780033, -0.0017128442414104939, -0.0