5, -0.011011269874870777, 0.03866105154156685, 0.019032828509807587, -0.05092371627688408, 0.004544632975012064, 0.03736843168735504, 0.009774590842425823, 0.030773648992180824, 0.002121791709214449, -0.008214873261749744, -0.050248757004737854, 0.019431425258517265, -0.027747251093387604, 0.06422024220228195, -0.01939767599105835, 0.007127981632947922, -0.00598160782828927, 0.01765085943043232, -0.0005829738802276552, -0.060045406222343445, -0.06003481149673462, 0.02838016487658024, 1.360997576080992e-34, -0.03287968412041664, -0.026079118251800537, 0.02317548543214798, -0.043127331882715225, 0.009205272421240807, 0.0027043132577091455, -0.017245082184672356, 0.0005546891479752958, 0.007243466563522816, 0.009063147939741611, -0.016963370144367218], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "62f0420769e6ee4a893485cfdde47a35d79489065a2feb06dc2c6a56bc07b240", "content": "This chunk is located in the section detailing the evidence base supporting the AET Post-16 Standards Framework and its Eight Principles of Good Autism Practice (GAP). It specifically references two comprehensive reports and a set of case studies that demonstrate the application of these principles in various educational settings.They consist of two reports, the \u2018Good Autism Practice: Full Report,\u2019 and a \u2018Practitioner Guide to Good Autism Practice in Education\u2019.\nThere are also a set of 8 case studies that serve to illustrate how the 8 principles of GAP have been embedded into a number of different education settings across the three phases.\fPost-16 Standards Framework\n3 The AET Eight Good Autism Practice Principles\nThis is how the Eight Principles of Good Autism Practice are embedded in the four themes. Principle One: Understanding the strengths, interests, and needs of each autistic learner.\nPrinciple Eight: Adapting the curriculum, teaching, and learning to promote wellbeing and success for autistic learners.\n", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "Post-16-Standa