, 0.02448303811252117, -0.045317575335502625, -0.008742600679397583, -0.011728860437870026, -0.017298799008131027, 0.01057346910238266, 9.2368594778236e-05, 8.364399400306866e-05, -0.01910877227783203], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "bda900d4f050b0081a4a09989153d0fd382f6010b9999e62f8bda806fe019e19", "content": "This chunk outlines three of the Eight Principles of Good Autism Practice (GAP) within the AET Post-16 Standards Framework, specifically focusing on fostering social inclusion, workforce development, and effective leadership. These principles are essential components aimed at enhancing inclusive practices for autistic learners, promoting a supportive environment and professional growth among staff.Principle Six: An ethos and environment that fosters social inclusion for autistic learners.\nPrinciple Four: Workforce development related to Good Autism Practice.\nPrinciple Five: Leadership and management that promotes and embeds Good Autism Practice.\nUnderstanding Enabling Positive and\neffective relationships\nenvironm ents\nthe learnerLearning and developm ent P1\nP2\nP3P6\nP7\nP8\nP4P5\fPost-16 Standards Framework\n4 The AET Inclusion Promises\nThe eight GAP principles are also distilled into eight Inclusion Promises created by the Autistic Young Experts panel. We aim to:\nUnderstand what you are good at, what you like doing, and when you might need help.\n", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "Post-16-Standards-Framework_T-HUB-Sc-Mt (1).pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1J2sZ87itIDhKcF_wmZN2TcBeZRLjlMJP", "source_id": "8c4c774884107306cf77966fe0ff24690d0139f6223da0babe2c1bcd4b2a95b5", "page_number": 4, "split_id": 7, "split_idx_start": 5039, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "32d62d1004b8884b744cedc836cf8737e6c681df7cdbc4392f535a65694a40a7", "range": [490, 583]}, {"doc_id": "2c500d169420be9845593aed2cefe06b8090716150e09abf33cb8f4b1290aa66", "range": [0, 95]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.03440266102552414, -0.039478156715