6077442169, 0.0314282663166523, -0.023643672466278076, -0.03931476175785065, -0.05853385105729103, 0.017741212621331215, 2.1022839435468303e-34, 0.006013128440827131, -0.06551656126976013, 0.028613917529582977, -0.07979318499565125, -0.010590893216431141, 0.011117441579699516, -0.022429846227169037, 0.009579558856785297, -0.002548528602346778, -0.051569368690252304, -0.02831285260617733], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "4ce83be3d72fd770a3610e1f76bcfeddfa08d9c2417a4078de2b01d4d30d28f6", "content": "This chunk is part of the section detailing how to effectively use the AET Post-16 Standards, emphasizing the importance of monitoring and documenting progress in implementing these standards. It is situated between the guidance on evaluation and the interlinking of key principles and competencies that support understanding and empowering autistic learners.Collect data before and after so you can document the progress of implementing the standard.\fPost-16 Standards Framework\n7 The following tables outline how the four themes, the Eight Principles of Good Autism Practice, the AET Post-16 Standards, and the AET Post-16 Competencies are interlinked. At a Glance: The four themes, Eight Principles of Good Autism Practice, the AET Post-16 Standards, and the AET Post-16 Competencies.\fPost-16 Standards Framework\n8 Understanding the learner\nSTANDARDS COMPETENCIES\nSTANDARDS COMPETENCIES\nPrinciple One: Understanding the strengths, interests, and needs of each autistic learner.\nPrinciple Two: Empowering autistic learners to be at the centre of decision-making about their education and to make informed decisions about their future .\n", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "Post-16-Standards-Framework_T-HUB-Sc-Mt (1).pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1J2sZ87itIDhKcF_wmZN2TcBeZRLjlMJP", "source_id": "8c4c774884107306cf77966fe0ff24690d0139f6223da0babe2c1bcd4b2a95b5", "page_number": 7, "split_id": 16, "split_idx_start": 9734, "_split_overlap": [{"d