7401387542e-05, -0.062096331268548965, 0.014653894118964672, -0.04463587701320648, 0.09121371060609818, -0.017737936228513718, -0.013483001850545406, 0.033983223140239716, 0.011323891580104828, -0.004463831428438425, -0.028037333860993385, -0.025746555998921394, -0.003954277839511633, 1.9749721656342667e-34, -0.016232596710324287, -0.010079208761453629, 0.017005957663059235, -0.07530615478754044, 0.01863948628306389, -0.009980452246963978, 0.000674257637001574, 0.015324054285883904, -0.01523055974394083, -0.017184298485517502, -0.015258725732564926], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "1aa6db2395e2c75e5d43fd7417b8deaee993ad91ffc3d821863c7eb2058c7551", "content": "This chunk is part of the AET Post-16 Standards Framework, specifically focusing on the principles related to understanding and empowering autistic learners. It emphasizes the importance of actively listening to learners, considering their co-occurring differences, and collaboratively developing strategies to support their emotional regulation and learning needs.I understand the importance of listening to the autistic learner and regularly consult, involve, and arrive at joint decisions with the autistic learner.\nS2. We take account of how each autistic learner\u2019s co-occurring differences, including anxiety and stress, can affect behaviour and can cause barriers to learning. We work closely with the autistic learner to understand their needs, and to find strategies for regulating feelings and emotions.\nS4. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "Post-16-Standards-Framework_T-HUB-Sc-Mt (1).pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1J2sZ87itIDhKcF_wmZN2TcBeZRLjlMJP", "source_id": "8c4c774884107306cf77966fe0ff24690d0139f6223da0babe2c1bcd4b2a95b5", "page_number": 9, "split_id": 19, "split_idx_start": 11181, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "48c16cb0c9ed1b5fd7d5cabe84423bdab6b99b0d5a9989f842422f150fcda293", "range": [502, 656]}, {"doc_id": "b6ff4448959eee3547ef52f25e64bf1b663be4b51018d0b