72947359085, -0.0028893635608255863, -0.03336683288216591, 0.001900741015560925, -0.047125790268182755, 0.09374409914016724, -0.006595059763640165, -0.020673438906669617, 0.01596463844180107, 0.009464007802307606, -0.0038099545054137707, -0.047884393483400345, -0.04896845296025276, 0.02631593868136406, 1.8235422053375809e-34, -0.017718808725476265, 0.0031648247968405485, 0.031898319721221924, -0.06955418735742569, 0.032940421253442764, -0.0063593462109565735, -0.015058358199894428, 0.023197537288069725, -0.0163683220744133, -0.004787107463926077, -0.033898502588272095], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "61bafaf7e5f03eb9257e5a6f95d2352b11b4cfd77a5eeec7851d225aea6266d3", "content": "This chunk is part of the section discussing the AET Eight Good Autism Practice Principles, specifically focusing on workforce development related to Good Autism Practice. It highlights the importance of using the AET Competencies framework to assess and enhance staff knowledge and practice concerning autism, thereby improving educational outcomes for autistic learners through collaborative efforts.We use the AET Competencies framework to audit staff confidence levels, understanding and knowledge of autism, and the changes staff make to their practice as a result of undertaking professional development. C6. I collaborate with the autistic learner, and with health, social care, parents/carers, and other professionals to improve educational experiences and outcomes for the autistic learner. C8. I utilise the competency framework as a working document to gather data, reflect on, and note down the implications of my learning and development for my own practice, and ultimately for the autistic learner(s) I work with.\f", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "Post-16-Standards-Framework_T-HUB-Sc-Mt (1).pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1J2sZ87itIDhKcF_wmZN2TcBeZRLjlMJP", "source_id": "8c4c774884107306cf77966fe0ff24690d0139f6223da0babe2c1bcd4b2a95b5", "page_number":