0.05173775181174278, -0.04110202565789223, -0.0017011507879942656, 0.012310182675719261, -0.004284040071070194, 0.011451728641986847, -0.031919050961732864, -0.053403496742248535, 0.012859046459197998, 1.8270834968638032e-34, -0.017333922907710075, 0.036189932376146317, 0.004069922957569361, -0.02531331218779087, 0.008858848363161087, -0.011609047651290894, -0.017473561689257622, -0.00019846460781991482, 0.03235182538628578, 0.014991351403295994, -0.004301127046346664], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "a159b3f13c99c6374212d50cd6b70b6b0d36be1e75b93672d3e7ebdfd4ad4d0b", "content": "This chunk is located within the \"Understanding the learner\" section of the AET Post-16 Standards Framework, emphasizing the importance of recognizing the diverse experiences of autistic individuals, particularly girls, women, and those from LGBTQ+ communities. It highlights the need for awareness and appropriate support, addressing potential biases in traditional views of autism.We are aware that autism has traditionally been viewed as a male condition, but that many autistic girls and women are being diagnosed later in life, are misdiagnosed, or their differences are missed. We also recognise that some boys and men, gay, trans and non-binary people often do not have their needs recognised. We are attuned to this and provide appropriate support.\nROOM FOR IMPROVEMENT\nROOM FOR IMPROVEMENT\nROOM FOR IMPROVEMENT\nDEVELOPING DEVELOPING DEVELOPING WE DO THIS REALLY WELL\nWE DO THIS REALLY WELL\nWE DO THIS REALLY WELL\nExamples of how we do this:\fPost-16 Standards Framework\n14 Understanding the learner\nWhat has been the impact of implementing this standard?\nOn autistic learners:\nOn parents/carers:\nOn staff:\nHow do we document evidence of working on this standard? ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "Post-16-Standards-Framework_T-HUB-Sc-Mt (1).pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1J2sZ87itIDhKcF_wmZN2TcBeZRLjlMJP", "source_id": "8c4c774884107306cf77966fe