45913748443, -0.016618018969893456, -0.02847486361861229, -0.05960017070174217, 0.019183102995157242, 2.0617202048938456e-34, -0.010382106527686119, 0.008749092929065228, 0.02839607372879982, -0.08899848163127899, 0.015713348984718323, 0.011017056182026863, -0.03244214877486229, 0.006829726044088602, -0.020555105060338974, -0.04135238006711006, -0.009718921966850758], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "f9d17ed13a26fd2e7831f95de95ebd11d0e28c234ffdcf8c023d38068a2d542e", "content": "This chunk is part of the section discussing the importance of understanding the individual needs of autistic learners, emphasizing the necessity of conducting comprehensive assessments that go beyond primary needs. It highlights the significance of considering sensory processing differences and environmental factors that may impact behavior, guiding the implementation of reasonable adjustments in educational settings.A detailed assessment of need should ensure that the full range of an individual\u2019s needs is identified, not simply the primary need. The support provided to an individual should always be based on a full understanding of their particular strengths and needs and seek to address them all (SEND Code of Practice, 2015:96).We recognise that autistic learners may have sensory processing differences. We conduct environmental audits in partnership with the autistic learner when possible, and we consider transport, the workplace, and community facilities.\nWe take account of the effect of external environmental factors when analysing why autistic learners might show behaviours that indicate distress. We engage with the autistic learner to find ways of making reasonable adjustments and regulating behaviour. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "Post-16-Standards-Framework_T-HUB-Sc-Mt (1).pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1J2sZ87itIDhKcF_wmZN2TcBeZRLjlMJP", "source_id": "8c4c774884107306cf77966fe0ff24690d0139f6223da0babe2c1bcd4b2a95b5", "page_number"