393647879362, -0.019995175302028656, 0.0188928060233593, -0.09997828304767609, 0.01176753081381321, -0.0022865808568894863, 0.01231563463807106, -0.00836051907390356, -0.029300548136234283, 0.008119218051433563, -0.01666182652115822], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "6d544d177a62cae5c475647ee42da5c2410a018fec9adb977dfc8f31b7dd81ab", "content": "This chunk is part of the AET Post-16 Standards Framework, specifically addressing Standard Nine, which focuses on creating a supportive and enabling environment for staff regarding autism knowledge and practice. It outlines the importance of having designated staff members to lead autism initiatives and support colleagues in implementing effective strategies for autistic learners.Standard Nine: We promote a supportive, enabling environment in which all staff are knowledgeable about autism and we empower staff to approach senior staff when their input is required.\nROOM FOR IMPROVEMENT\nDEVELOPING WE DO THIS REALLY WELL\nExamples of how we do this: Link to statutory framework\nWe have a named and experienced member of staff (for example, an autism champion or lead/advanced practitioner) to take the lead on developing effective autism practice. They provide general information on autism, specific information about individuals and advice on strategies and approaches to all other members of staff.\nROOM FOR IMPROVEMENT\nDEVELOPING WE DO THIS REALLY WELL\fPost-16 Standards Framework\n32 Enabling environments\nWhat has been the impact of implementing this standard?\nOn autistic learners:\nOn parents/carers:\nOn staff:\nHow do we document evidence of working on this standard? ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "Post-16-Standards-Framework_T-HUB-Sc-Mt (1).pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1J2sZ87itIDhKcF_wmZN2TcBeZRLjlMJP", "source_id": "8c4c774884107306cf77966fe0ff24690d0139f6223da0babe2c1bcd4b2a95b5", "page_number": 32, "split_id": 66, "split_idx_start": 40472, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "00