59977429749e-34, 0.00040787315811030567, 0.011546037159860134, 0.010212367400527, -0.08238387107849121, 0.017796427011489868, 0.002953003626316786, -0.013216708786785603, 0.007138584740459919, -0.006180905736982822, -0.020428180694580078, -0.01738622598350048], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "225771e35521fa2e322ef1ffac95aa383c5c9813ac5815b0ea29ce65eeeae4d4", "content": "This chunk emphasizes the importance of supporting children and young people with Special Educational Needs (SEN) towards greater independence and employability. It highlights the necessity for this support to be aligned with the individual's aspirations and needs, as outlined in the SEND Code of Practice. It fits within the section on \"Principle Five: Leadership and management that promotes and embeds Good Autism Practice,\" which focuses on creating inclusive systems to enhance the educational experiences of autistic learners.Being supported towards greater independence and employability can be life transforming for children and young people with SEN. This support needs to start early, and should centre around the child or young person\u2019s own aspirations, interests and needs. All professionals working with them should share high aspirations and have a good understanding of what support is effective in enabling children and young people to achieve their ambitions (SEND Code of Practice, 2015:122).\nOur systems and structures are proactively adjusted to promote social inclusion and to reduce exclusions.\nROOM FOR IMPROVEMENT\nROOM FOR IMPROVEMENT\nDEVELOPING DEVELOPING WE DO THIS REALLY WELL\nWE DO THIS REALLY WELL\nPrinciple Five: Leadership and management that promotes and embeds Good Autism Practice.\n", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "Post-16-Standards-Framework_T-HUB-Sc-Mt (1).pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1J2sZ87itIDhKcF_wmZN2TcBeZRLjlMJP", "source_id": "8c4c774884107306cf77966fe0ff24690d0139f6223da0babe2c1bcd4b2a95b5", "page_number": 34, "split_i