he impact of Good Autism Practice standards on autistic learners, their parents/carers, and staff. It emphasizes the importance of structured activities and well-planned transitions to support autistic learners in their educational environment.On autistic learners:\nOn parents/carers:\nOn staff:\nHow do we document evidence of working on this standard? We document evidence of working on this standard through assessment results, the production of resources, case studies, learner profiles, and the goals we co-\ncreate with autistic learners and parents/carers (where appropriate/possible), and by documenting our reasonable adjustments and the impact of those.\nEvidence to show how we meet this standard:\fPost-16 Standards Framework\n37 Enabling environments\nWe recognise that some autistic learners experience difficulties with change, ranging from changes in everyday routine to changing between settings. We ensure that activities are well organised and structured, and we plan ahead for transitions.\nThe vast majority of young people with SEN are capable of sustainable paid employment with the right preparation and support. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "Post-16-Standards-Framework_T-HUB-Sc-Mt (1).pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1J2sZ87itIDhKcF_wmZN2TcBeZRLjlMJP", "source_id": "8c4c774884107306cf77966fe0ff24690d0139f6223da0babe2c1bcd4b2a95b5", "page_number": 37, "split_id": 74, "split_idx_start": 46019, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "9f9d89810c079bd89203f6867541306fae8317c53d21ed50c7c6b37b68627562", "range": [748, 856]}, {"doc_id": "20aab161beb5beee1f429109c5bc8b6a1f26aee4969720636248cf652c9e3a39", "range": [0, 126]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.0031350762583315372, -0.06872934848070145, -0.022005146369338036, -0.010945139452815056, -0.004601937253028154, 0.016319749876856804, 0.02947266399860382, -0.02880709245800972, -0.04406297579407692, 0.005548754706978798, 0.05869971215724945, -0.037077102810144424, 0.0308702997863292