9270477, 0.018061701208353043, 0.00962771289050579, -0.01976650580763817, -0.03451860696077347, -0.026712758466601372], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "c68fc0695c8bb9bc8927c08fc948ab79030a42d71076d6f5402837676a42c3e2", "content": "This chunk is part of the section detailing the educational expectations and support mechanisms for autistic learners within Post-16 settings. It emphasizes the importance of designing inclusive study programmes that cater to individual learning needs, highlighting the collaborative creation of learner profiles and support plans to facilitate progress and achievement.Schools and colleges are expected to design study programmes which enable learners to progress to a higher level of study than their prior attainment, take rigorous, substantial qualifications, study English and maths, participate in meaningful work experience and non-qualification activity (SEND Code of Practice, 2015:130).\nWe co-create learner profiles and specialist learner support plans with autistic learners. These are tailored to the learner\u2019s strengths and needs. They have clear learning targets and strategies. They enable the autistic learner and staff to monitor progress, and they are reviewed regularly. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "Post-16-Standards-Framework_T-HUB-Sc-Mt (1).pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1J2sZ87itIDhKcF_wmZN2TcBeZRLjlMJP", "source_id": "8c4c774884107306cf77966fe0ff24690d0139f6223da0babe2c1bcd4b2a95b5", "page_number": 43, "split_id": 83, "split_idx_start": 52217, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "db6cd478a9a3277c73401ebdfca0cb3edafde27d0c6a8adbfdd0dfb81996703f", "range": [912, 1239]}, {"doc_id": "31dd0f96640fbbb53a5f241a1656c41a347e549e6e8c39394e40788525285372", "range": [0, 97]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.020905664190649986, -0.07740112394094467, -0.02237398363649845, -0.0007325129699893296, 0.005135978106409311, 0.05243203416466713, 0.04268399998545647, -0.04721613973379135, -0.0191842373460