9429171, -0.00292116217315197, -0.05620267987251282, 0.0353328213095665, 0.0028178419452160597, 0.038195714354515076, 0.007500985637307167, -0.054499849677085876, 0.002255956409499049, 0.015247263945639133, 0.017910681664943695, 0.012177940458059311, -0.013340173289179802, -0.004635294899344444, -0.047174759209156036, 0.01736406795680523, -0.04865304380655289, 0.08198948204517365, 0.020916935056447983, -0.01989402063190937, 0.004813745617866516, 0.012673170305788517, -0.0028100749477744102, -0.04192585498094559, -0.07139018177986145, 0.017774149775505066, 2.2040470864282408e-34, 0.0017755466978996992, -0.005914365407079458, 0.02501636929810047, -0.06466223299503326, 0.010619144886732101, 0.0027114979457110167, -0.02093624882400036, 0.0051689958199858665, -0.016724860295653343, -0.03953677788376808, -0.019420182332396507], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "c3c813d60bc7bb9aa008b694391f65c928f2215792a72885933781894396aab5", "content": "This chunk is situated within the section discussing targeted support and measuring the progress of autistic learners. It emphasizes the importance of self-advocacy, the involvement of parents and young people in decision-making, and the development of personal and social skills through various educational programs, aligning with the principles outlined in the AET Post-16 Standards Framework.Self-advocacy is a central element of our work and we monitor wellbeing through regular tutorials and mentoring sessions. Early years providers, schools and colleges should know precisely where children and young people with SEN are in their learning and development. They should ensure decisions are informed by the insights of parents and those of children and young people themselves; have high ambitions and set stretching targets for them; track their progress towards these goals; keep under review the additional or different provision that is made for them; promote positive outcomes in the wider areas of personal and social development, and ensure that the approaches used are based on the best possible evidence and are having the required impact on progress (SEND Code of Practice, 2015:25).\nWe develop and promote social and emotional skills through relationships and sex education (RSE), personal, social, health, and economic (PSHE) education, as well as in the wider curriculum. Autistic learners have access to enhanced, differentiated and scaffolded learning and teaching in these areas. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "Post-16-Standards-Framework_T-HUB-Sc-Mt (1).pdf", "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1J2sZ87itIDhKcF_wmZN2TcBeZRLjlMJP", "source_id": "8c4c774884107306cf77966fe0ff24690d0139f6223da0babe2c1bcd4b2a95b5", "page_number": 45, "split_id": 86, "split_idx_start": 54397, "_split_overlap": [{"doc_id": "809e4bf15fa2f4bd72c97deb5203630f6051c1333bc30129ce1d1a657882ae75", "range": [801, 923]}, {"doc_id": "dd7d130ee1565c8ec86ea5873f27f4815bde778c11175c4b3ddf8a98882a52cf", "range": [0, 111]}]}, "score": null, "embedding": [0.035890862345695496, -0.07507694512605667, -0.02004299871623516, -0.015965068712830544, 0.0003456362755969167, 0.05406542867422104, 0.04702388122677803, -0.05355464667081833, -0.03656831383705139, 0.012231439352035522, 0.03237149864435196, -0.06107297167181969, 0.026347080245614052, -0.010919301770627499, -0.0073993573896586895, -0.054834626615047455, 0.028717640787363052, 0.020512377843260765, -0.004068418871611357, 0.020142748951911926, -0.01853078231215477, 0.022333834320306778, -0.04308250546455383, 0.027380583807826042, 0.041683319956064224, -0.03861444070935249, 0.01969025284051895, -0.001238346565514803, 0.008638949133455753, -0.05536367744207382, 0.018636004999279976, 0.013850027695298195, 0.005459400825202465, -0.02095320075750351, 2.3893733214208623e-06, 0.01935446634888649, 0.0153994495049119, -0.02588729001581669, -0.019978519529104233, 0.0050046867690980434, -0.05435549095273018, 0.026427892968058586, 0.015679173171520233, 0.06361953169107437, -