0455, 0.011420630849897861, 0.008652713149785995, 0.0520215667784214, -0.0032307046931236982, 0.0009723790572024882, -0.03954906761646271, -0.01666942797601223], "sparse_embedding": null}, {"id": "d4aea764e1a234d89b50ad962571a7b2914fc7d3748e45b19b0058732c517cce", "content": "This chunk is located in the section discussing the implementation of the AET Post-16 Standards, specifically within the theme of \"Learning and Development.\" It focuses on evaluating the use of digital literacy programs and assistive technology tailored for autistic learners, highlighting the importance of tracking the impact of such initiatives on learners and documenting improvements.ROOM FOR IMPROVEMENT\nDEVELOPING WE DO THIS REALLY WELL\nExamples of how we do this: Link to statutory framework\nWe use a number of digital literacy programmes, devices and applications that have been specifically developed with autistic learners in mind. ROOM FOR IMPROVEMENT\nDEVELOPING WE DO THIS REALLY WELL\fPost-16 Standards Framework\n47 Learning and development\nWhat has been the impact of implementing this standard?\nOn autistic learners:\nOn parents/carers:\nOn staff:\nHow do we document evidence of working on this standard? We document evidence of working on this standard through assessment results, the production of resources, case studies, learner profiles, and the goals we co-\ncreate with autistic learners and parents/carers (where appropriate/possible), and by documenting our reasonable adjustments and the impact of those.\nEvidence to show how we meet this standard:\fPost-16 Standards Framework\n48 Additional resources Please click here to access: \u2022 AET resources and templates\n\u2022 External resources\n\u2022 Useful websites\n\u2022 Legislation and guidance\n\u2022 Videos\fPost-16 Standards Framework\n49 Author and acknowledgements\nA note on authorship: The AET standards have been co-created with a number of people. ", "dataframe": null, "blob": null, "meta": {"file_path": "Post-16-Standa